hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6001","5991 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, both saying, from Sa'id ibn Mansur from Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Khaybar: ""I shall surely give the banner tomorrow to a man through whom Allah will grant victory."" The people spoke about which of them would be given it, and he said: ""Where is Ali ibn Abi Talib?"" They said: O Messenger of Allah, he is complaining of his eye. They sent for him and he was brought; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spat in his eye and supplicated for him, and he was healed as if he had no pain. He gave him the banner, and Ali said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I fight them until they become like us? He said: ""Go forth at your ease until you come down in their courtyard, then call them to Islam and inform them of what is due upon them of the right of Allah, for that Allah should guide through you is better than red camels being yours."""
"6002","5992 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yasar al-Nasa'i from Qutayba ibn Sa'id from Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Hazim ibn Dinar: Some men came to Sahl ibn Sa'd disputing about the pulpit and its wood, and asked him about that. He said: By Allah, I know what it is; I saw it the first day it was placed and the first day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat upon it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to so-and-so - a woman whom Sahl named - saying: ""Order your carpenter boy to make me some pieces of wood upon which I may sit when I address the people."" She ordered him, and he made it from the tamarisk of the thicket; then he brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it was sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered it placed here. Then I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronounce the takbir while upon it, then bow while upon it, then come down backwards and prostrate at the foot of the pulpit, then ascend; when he finished he turned to the people and said: ""O people, I did this only so that you may follow me and learn my prayer."""
"6003","5993 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Nasa'i from Qutayba ibn Sa'id from Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have come to give myself to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at her, raising his gaze to her and lowering it, then bowed his head. When the woman saw that he had decided nothing concerning her, she sat down. A man from among his Companions stood up and said: O Messenger of Allah, if you have no need of her, marry her to me. He said: ""Do you have anything?"" He said: No. He said: ""What do you have with you of the Qur'an?"" He said: Such-and-such sura and such-and-such sura. He said: ""Then I have married her to you for what you have with you of the Qur'an."""
"6004","5994 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yasar from Qutayba ibn Sa'id from Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd: It reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that there was a dispute among Banu Amr ibn Awf, and he mentioned the hadith; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Glorifying Allah is for men and clapping is for women."""
"6005","5995 - With the same chain from Sahl ibn Sa'd, he said: We used to say: The pulpit stands upon a water-channel from among the water-channels of Paradise."
"6006","5995 / 2 - With the same chain: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""People remain upon good as long as they hasten their breaking of the fast."""
"6007","5996 - With the same chain, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""I am your forerunner at the Basin; whoever comes to it drinks, and whoever drinks never thirsts again. Some peoples will come to me whom I recognize and who recognize me, then a barrier will be placed between me and them."""
"6008","5997 - With the same chain from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: A woman came with a woven cloak and said: O Messenger of Allah, I wove this with my hand to clothe you with it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it, being in need of it, and came out to us wearing it as his waist-wrapper. A man from among the people felt it and said: O Messenger of Allah, clothe me with it. He said: ""Yes."" He sat in the gathering as long as Allah willed, then returned, folded it, and sent it to him. The people said to him: You did not do well; you asked him for it knowing that he never turns away a beggar. The man said: By Allah, I asked for it only so that it may be my shroud on the day I die. Sahl said: And it was his shroud. Qutayba said: He was Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas."
"6009","5998 - With the same chain: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The people of Paradise look upon a chamber in Paradise just as you look upon a star on the horizon of the sky."""
"6010","5999 - With the same chain, he said: I asked Sahl ibn Sa'd: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat fine sifted flour? Sahl said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never saw fine flour from the time Allah sent him until Allah took him."
"6011","6000 - With the same chain from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi came and invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his wedding feast; his wife served them that day, and she was the bride. Abu Usayd said: Do you know what I gave the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to drink? I soaked dates for him overnight."
"6012","6001 - With the same chain from Sahl: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met the polytheists and they fought; when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withdrew to his camp, the others withdrew to their camp. Among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a man who left none of them, scattered or isolated, except he followed it with a stroke of his sword - and he mentioned the hadith."
"6013","Abu Sakhr Humayd ibn Ziyad from Abu Hazim. 6002 - Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Harun ibn Ma'ruf from Ibn Wahb from Abu Sakhr Humayd ibn Ziyad from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described Paradise and said: ""In it is what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never occurred to a human heart."""
"6014","6003 - Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam from Abd Allah ibn Suwayd from Abu Sakhr from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) describing Paradise until he reached its description, he then said: ""In it is what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never occurred to a human heart."" Then he recited this verse: ""Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope, and spending from what We have provided them"" up to His saying: ""As a reward for what they used to do."""
"6015","Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Misri from Abu Hazim. 6004 - Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini from Yahya ibn Ayyub and Attaf ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""A morning going or an evening going in the path of Allah is better than the world and what is in it."""
"6016","Isa ibn Musa al-Ansari, a Syrian, from Abu Hazim. 6005 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti al-Himsi - and narrated Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutayba al-Asqalani from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari, both saying, from al-Walid ibn Muslim - from Abu Muhammad Isa ibn Musa al-Ansari from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Among the descendants of the descendants of the descendants of men from among my Companions are men and women who enter Paradise without reckoning."" Then he recited: ""And others among them who have not yet joined them; and He is the Mighty, the Wise."""
"6017","Kharija ibn Mus'ab al-Khurasani from Abu Hazim. 6006 - Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad from Hammad ibn Mas'ada from Kharija ibn Mus'ab from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: We used to take the midday rest after the Friday prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6018","6007 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karama from Ubayd Allah ibn Musa from Kharija ibn Mus'ab from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a drink while there was a boy on his right and elders on his left. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank, then said to the boy: ""Shall I give it to the elders?"" The boy said: I would never prefer anyone over your leftover or your favor to me. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave it to him; he drank and left the elders."
"6019","6008 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karama from Ubayd Allah ibn Musa from Kharija ibn Mus'ab from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""Clapping is only for women and glorifying Allah is for men."""
"6020","6009 - With the same chain, he said: I heard Sahl ibn Sa'd say: No day was dearer to us than Friday. And he said: An old woman used to cook beet roots on Friday and throw grains of barley upon them; when we returned from the Friday prayer we would enter and eat."
"6021","Yahya ibn al-Ala' al-Bajali al-Razi from Abu Hazim. 6010 - Narrated Abu Zur'a Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Kufi from Yahya ibn al-Ala' from Abu Hazim from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatima: ""Where is your husband?"" She said: Words passed between me and him, and he went out in anger. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: ""Look for Ali for me."" He said: O Messenger of Allah, there he is in the mosque. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him while the wind blew dust upon him, and said: ""Rise, O Abu Turab."" Sahl said: By Allah, it was the dearest of his names to him."
"6022","Yahya ibn Maymun al-Hadrami from Sahl. 6011 - Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' from Ayyash ibn Uqba al-Hadrami, who said: I heard Yahya ibn Maymun al-Hadrami say: Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi stopped by us while we were in the mosque, and Sahl said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Whoever sits in the mosque awaiting the prayer is in prayer."""
"6023","6012 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd Allah ibn Salih from Bakr ibn Mudar from Ayyash ibn Uqba al-Hadrami from Yahya ibn Maymun al-Hadrami, who said: Sahl ibn Sa'd passed by me while I was sitting in the mosque, and said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Whoever is in the mosque awaiting the prayer is in prayer."""
"6024","Abu Zur'a Amr ibn Jabir al-Hadrami from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6013 - Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam - and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd Allah ibn Yusuf, both saying, from Ibn Lahi'a - from Abu Zur'a Amr ibn Jabir, who said: I heard Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not revile Tubba', for he had accepted Islam."""
"6025","Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6014 - Narrated Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi from Ibn Lahi'a from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far from Safwan ibn Sulaym from Nafi' ibn Jubayr from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""When one of you prays toward a sutra, let him draw near to it so that Satan does not pass between him and his prayer."" This is how Ibn Lahi'a narrated it from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far from Safwan ibn Sulaym from Nafi' ibn Jubayr from Sahl ibn Sa'd; Ibn Uyayna narrated it from Safwan ibn Sulaym from Nafi' ibn Jubayr from Sahl ibn Abi Hathma."
"6026","6015 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Zunbur from Isma'il ibn Ja'far from Isa ibn Maymun ibn Iyas ibn al-Bukayr from Safwan ibn Sulaym from Nafi' ibn Jubayr from Sahl ibn Sa'd: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When one of you prays toward a sutra, let him draw near to it so that Satan does not cut off his prayer."""
"6027","Bakr ibn Sawada from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6016 - Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Bakr ibn Sawada from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: There was a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named Aswad - black - so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Abyad - white."
"6028","6017 - Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman from his father, and Yahya ibn Bukayr, both saying, from Ibn Lahi'a from Bakr ibn Sawada from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""By Him in Whose hand is my soul, you will surely ride the ways of those before you, sole matching sole."""
"6029","Abu Abd Allah al-Ghifari from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6018 - Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Muhammad ibn Marzuq and al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad, both saying, from Ubayd ibn Waqid Abu Abbad al-Qaysi from Abu Abd Allah al-Ghifari, who said: I heard Sahl ibn Sa'd say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Gabriel commanded me with the tooth-stick until I thought I would wear away my gums."""
"6030","6018 / 2 - Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad from Ubayd ibn Waqid from Abu Abd Allah al-Ghifari, who said: I heard Sahl ibn Sa'd say: I was sitting with a maternal uncle of mine from the Ansar when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: ""Go out to the thicket, bring me wood from it, and make me a pulpit upon which I address the people."" So he made a pulpit of two steps, and he sat upon it."
"6031","Abu Suhayl Nafi' ibn Malik from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6019 - Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Abu Musa al-Ansari from Asim ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Suhayl Nafi' ibn Malik from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: We used to take the midday rest after the Friday prayer."
"6032","Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6020 - Narrated Abu Zayd Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatim al-Muradi al-Misri from Nu'aym ibn Hammad from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Abi al-Zinad from Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his uncle al-Abbas, may Allah have mercy on him: ""I am the seal of the prophets."" Then he raised his hand and said: ""O Allah, forgive al-Abbas, the sons of al-Abbas, and the sons of the sons of al-Abbas."""
"6033","Abd Allah ibn Ubayda al-Rabadhi from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6021 - Narrated Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi from Qabisa from Sufyan from Musa ibn Ubayda from his brother Abd Allah ibn Ubayda from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were reciting the Qur'an, teaching it to one another, and said: ""Praise be to Allah; the Book of Allah is one; among you are the black and the red. Recite the Qur'an, recite the Qur'an, recite the Qur'an before there come peoples who recite the Qur'an, setting its letters straight as an arrow is set straight, not passing their collarbones, hastening its reward and not deferring it."""
"6034","6022 - Narrated al-Fadl ibn Abi Rawh al-Basri from Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman from Musa ibn Ubayda from his brother Abd Allah ibn Ubayda from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were teaching one another, and said: ""Praise be to Allah; one Book; among you are the red and the black. Recite the Qur'an, recite the Qur'an; recite before there come peoples who set it straight as an arrow is set straight, not passing their collarbones, hastening its reward and not deferring it."""
"6035","Ibn Abi Dhu'bab from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6023 - Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal - and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba - and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, the latter two saying, from Isma'il ibn Ulaya, both saying, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq - from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiya from Ibn Abi Dhu'bab from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising his hands at all in supplication upon a pulpit or elsewhere; rather I saw him say thus, pointing with the index finger and joining the middle finger to the thumb."
"6036","Wafa' ibn Shurayh al-Misri from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6024 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Rushdin al-Misri from Ahmad ibn Salih from Ibn Wahb from Amr ibn al-Harith from Bakr ibn Sawada from Wafa' ibn Shurayh from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us one day while we were reciting to one another, and said: ""Praise be to Allah; the Book of Allah is one; among you is the white and among you is the black. Recite it before there come peoples who recite it, straightening it as an arrow is straightened; one of them hastens its reward and does not defer it."""
"6037","Imran ibn Abi Anas from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6025 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Waki' from Rabi'a ibn Uthman from Imran ibn Abi Anas from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: Two men differed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the mosque founded upon piety; one said: It is the mosque of Medina, and the other said: It is the mosque of Quba'. They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: ""It is this mosque of mine."""
"6038","Abu Yahya al-Aslami from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6026 - Narrated Musa ibn Sahl Abu Imran al-Jawni from Hisham ibn Ammar from Hatim ibn Isma'il from Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya al-Aslami from his father, who said: We entered upon Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi in his house, and he said: If I were to give you to drink from the well of Buda'a you would dislike it, yet by Allah I gave the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to drink from it with my hands."
"6039","Ziyad and Ilaqa, the sons of Zayd, from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6027 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki from Ya'qub ibn Humayd from Kathir ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Kathir from Ziyad and Ilaqa, the sons of Zayd, from Sahl ibn Sa'd: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever has a landed property in Medina, let him hold fast to it; and whoever has no landed property in it, let him acquire one in it, for there will come upon people a time when he who has no landed property in it will be like one leaving it, passing through to somewhere else."""
"6040","Qudama ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Hatib al-Jumahi from Sahl ibn Sa'd. 6028 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani from Mus'ab ibn Abd Allah al-Zubayri from his father from Qudama ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Hatib al-Jumahi, who said: I was present when al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf was beating Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi over the affair of Ibn al-Zubayr; his father Sahl came in his waist-wrapper and cloak and shouted at al-Hajjaj: Will you not observe the bequest of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning us? He said: And what did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bequeath concerning you? He said: He bequeathed that good be done to the doers of good among the Ansar and that their wrongdoers be pardoned. So he released him."
"6041","593 - Suhayl ibn Bayda' al-Qurashi then al-Fihri, a Badri, died in Medina in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over him in the mosque. His lineage: Suhayl ibn Wahb ibn Rabi'a ibn Hilal ibn Uhayb ibn Dabba ibn al-Harith ibn Fihr; Bayda' is his mother, her name being Da'd bint Asad ibn Jahdham ibn Umayya ibn al-Harith ibn Fihr. 6029 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Barqi from Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham from Ziyad ibn Abd Allah from Ibn Ishaq with this lineage."
"6042","6030 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki from Sa'id ibn Mansur from Fulayh ibn Sulayman from Salih ibn Ajlan and Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Abbad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Suhayl ibn Bayda' only in the mosque."
"6043","6031 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abi al-Aswad from Urwa regarding the naming of those who attended Badr, from Quraysh, then from Banu al-Harith ibn Fihr: Suhayl ibn Bayda'."
"6044","6032 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab regarding the naming of those who attended Badr from Quraysh, then from Banu al-Harith ibn Fihr: Suhayl ibn Bayda'."
"6045","6033 - Narrated al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Ibn al-Had - and narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri from Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam from Yahya ibn Ayyub and Ibn Lahi'a, both saying, from Ibn al-Had - from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim from Sa'id ibn al-Salt from Suhayl ibn Bayda', who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, Suhayl being the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon his camel, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Suhayl ibn Bayda'!"" raising his voice. Suhayl said: At your service, raising his voice; he did that two or three times. Suhayl said: The people realized he wanted to say something to let them hear it, so those behind us caught up with us and those ahead of us were held back for us until they gathered. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever testifies that there is no god but Allah, Allah makes Paradise obligatory for him and forbids him through it to the Fire."""
"6046","6034 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki from Harmala ibn Yahya from Ibn Wahb from Haywa ibn Shurayh from Ibn al-Had from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim from Sa'id ibn al-Salt from Suhayl ibn Bayda', of Banu Abd al-Dar, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Suhayl ibn Bayda' being the riding companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Suhayl ibn Bayda'!"" raising his voice two or three times, each time Suhayl answering him; the people heard the voice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and realized he meant them, so those ahead sat and those behind caught up until they gathered. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever testifies that there is no god but Allah, Allah forbids the Fire for him and makes Paradise obligatory for him."""
"6047","594 - Suhayl ibn Rafi' al-Ansari, a Badri. 6035 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab regarding the naming of those who attended Badr from the Ansar, then from Banu al-Najjar: Suhayl ibn Rafi' ibn Abi Amr; the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon a drying-place belonging to him and his brother."
"6048","595 - Suhayl ibn Ubayd ibn al-Nu'man al-Ansari, a Badri. 6036 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba from Ibn Shihab regarding the naming of those who attended Badr from the Ansar, then from Banu al-Najjar: Suhayl ibn Ubayd ibn al-Nu'man; he left no offspring."
"6049","596 - Suhayl ibn Amr ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Wudd ibn Nasr ibn Malik ibn Hisl ibn Amir ibn Lu'ayy, known as Abu Yazid; died in Syria in the plague of Amwas in the year eighteen. 6037 - Narrated Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj from Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Suhayl ibn Amr died in Syria in the year eighteen."
"6050","6038 - Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Arim Abu al-Nu'man from Jarir ibn Hazim, who said: I heard al-Hasan say: The nobles of Quraysh gathered at the door of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, among them al-Harith ibn Hisham, Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, and Suhayl ibn Amr, along with those freedmen and clients from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). His chamberlain came out and admitted Bilal, Suhayb, and their likes, leaving the others. Abu Sufyan said: I never saw the like of today; he admitted these slaves and left us sitting at his door without admitting us. Suhayl ibn Amr, a sensible man, said: O people, by Allah I see what is in your faces; if you are angry, be angry with yourselves. The people were called and you were called; they hastened and you lagged. By Allah, what you have been beaten to of excellence is a greater loss to you than this door of yours over which you compete. Al-Hasan said: Allah does not make a servant who hastens to Him like a servant who lags from Him."
"6051","597 - Suhayl ibn Hanzala. 6039 - Narrated Abu Ma'n Thabit ibn Nu'aym al-Hawji from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani from Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman from his father from Qatada from Abi al-Aliya from Suhayl ibn Hanzala, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""No people sit in a gathering remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, therein and then rise until it is said to them: Rise, Allah has forgiven your sins and exchanged your evil deeds for good ones."""
"6052","598 - Salman al-Farisi, known as Abu Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him; he accepted Islam upon the arrival of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Medina, was kept busy by slavery, missed Badr and Uhud with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his first battle was the Trench. It is said in some narrations that he accepted Islam in Mecca, but his Islam in Medina is more established, and Allah knows best. From the reports of Salman, and his death. 6040 - Narrated Mas'ada ibn Sa'd al-Attar al-Makki from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Ibn Abi Fudayk from Kathir ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani from his father from his grandfather: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marked out the Trench from Ahmar al-Baskhatayn at the edge of Banu Haritha in the year of the Confederates until he reached al-Madhahib, assigning forty cubits to every ten. The Muhajirun and the Ansar disputed over Salman al-Farisi, may Allah have mercy on him, a strong man; the Muhajirun said: Salman is from us, and the Ansar said: Salman is from us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Salman is from us, the People of the House."""
"6053","6041 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ibrahim ibn Yusuf al-Sayrafi from Ali ibn Abis from al-A'mash from Amr ibn Murra and Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, both saying: Ali was asked about Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: He recited the Qur'an, stood at its ambiguous verses, declared its lawful lawful and its unlawful unlawful. He was asked about Ammar and said: ""A forgetful believer; when reminded he remembers; packed from between his horn to his heel with faith."" He was asked about Hudhayfa and said: ""He was the most knowledgeable of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the hypocrites; he asked about them and informed them."" They said: Narrate to us about Salman. He said: ""He grasped the first knowledge and the last knowledge, an inexhaustible sea, from us, the People of the House."" They said: Inform us about Abu Dharr. He said: ""A vessel of knowledge wasted by people."" They said: Then inform us about yourself. He said: ""That is what you wanted; when I asked I was given, and when I was silent I was prompted, and between the two sides of the beard is abundant knowledge."""
"6054","6042 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Isma'il from Hibban ibn Ali al-Anazi from Abd al-Malik ibn Jurayj from Abi Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad from his father, and from a man from Zadhadh al-Kindi, both saying: We were one day with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, when the people found him in good spirits and mood, and said: O Commander of the Believers, narrate to us about your companions. He said: About which of my companions? They said: About the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: All the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are my companions; about which of them do you ask? They said: About those whom we see you favor with your mention and invocation of blessings upon them apart from the people. He said: About which of them? They said: About Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud. He said: ""He recited the Qur'an, knew the Sunna, and that suffices."" He said: By Allah, we did not know what he meant by his saying: that suffices - whether recitation of the Qur'an and knowledge of the Sunna suffice, or Abd Allah suffices. He said: He was asked about Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: He asked much and was given and denied; he was keen, scrupulous over his religion, keen upon knowledge, a sea filled for him in a vessel of his until it was full. We said: Then narrate to us about Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: ""He knew the names of the hypocrites and asked about difficult problems until overlooked matters - you find him knowledgeable in them."" They said: Then narrate to us about Salman. He said: ""Who has the like of him - Luqman the Wise; that is a man from us, the People of the House; he grasped the first knowledge and the last knowledge, and read the first Book and the last Book, an inexhaustible sea."" We said: Narrate to us about Ammar ibn Yasir? He said: ""A man whose flesh, blood, hair, and skin Allah has mixed with faith; wherever it moves, with him; the Fire should not eat anything of him."" We said: Then narrate to us about yourself? He said: ""Gently; Allah has forbidden self-praise."" A man said to him: Allah, mighty and majestic, says: ""And as for the favor of your Lord, proclaim it."" He said: ""I proclaim the favor of my Lord; when I asked I was given, and when I was silent I was prompted."""
"6055","6042 / 2 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Maymun al-Jud'ani from Attab ibn Bashir from Ali ibn Badhima, who said: The goods of Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, were sold and reached fourteen dirhams."
"6056","6043 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i from Ubayd Allah ibn Musa from Shayban from Firas from al-Sha'bi from al-Jazl from the wife of Salman, Buqayra, who said: When death came to Salman he called me while he was in an upper room with four doors, and said: Open these doors, O Buqayra, for I have visitors today, and I do not know from which of these doors they will enter upon me. Then he called for his musk and said: Dissolve it in a brass basin. I did so, then he said: Sprinkle it around my bed, then go down and wait; you will soon look upon my bier upon my bed. I looked, and his soul had been taken, as if sleeping upon his bed, or something like that."
"6057","6044 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nu'aym from al-Hasan ibn Salih from Abi Rabi'a al-Basri from al-Hasan from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""Three to whom the wide-eyed houris are led: Ali, Ammar, and Salman, may Allah be pleased with them."""
"6058","6045 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Ali ibn Bahr from Salama ibn al-Fadl al-Abrash from Imran al-Ta'i, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Paradise longs for four: Ali ibn Abi Talib, Ammar ibn Yasir, Salman al-Farisi, and al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, may Allah be pleased with them."""
"6059","6046 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Nuh ibn Harb al-Askari from Khalid ibn Yusuf al-Samti from Abd al-Nur ibn Abd Allah from Yunus ibn Shu'ayb from Abi Umama, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gaze with his sight toward the sky. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is this? He said: ""I saw an angel ascending with the deed of Salman."""
"6060","6047 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini from Kathir ibn Yahya - and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani, both saying, from Sufyan ibn Uyayna - from Kathir al-Nawwa' from Abi Idris from al-Musayyab ibn Najaba, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Every prophet was given seven noble companions, and we have been given fourteen. We said to Ali: Who are they? He said: ""I, my two sons, Ja'far, Hamza, Abu Bakr, Umar, Mus'ab ibn Umayr, Bilal, Salman, Ammar, and Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them."" The number of fourteen is not completed in this hadith."
"6061","6048 - Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Kathir al-Nawwa' from al-Musayyab ibn Najaba from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every prophet was given nine companions, and I have been given fourteen."" It was said to Ali: Who are they? He said: ""I, my two sons al-Hasan and al-Husayn, Hamza, Ja'far, Abu Bakr, Umar, al-Miqdad, Salman, Talha, and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them."" Fitr ibn Khalifa differed with Ibn Uyayna in the chain of this hadith."
"6062","6049 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nu'aym from Fitr ibn Khalifa from Kathir, seller of date-stones, who said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Mulayl say: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""No prophet was ever given seven noble, excellent minister-companions, and I have been given fourteen: Hamza, Ja'far, Ali, Hasan, Husayn, Abu Bakr, Umar, Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, Abu Dharr, al-Miqdad, Hudhayfa, Ammar, Salman, and Bilal, may Allah have mercy on them."""
"6063","6050 - Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna al-Anbari from Abd Allah ibn Sawwar al-Anbari from Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit al-Bunani: Abu al-Darda' went with Salman al-Farisi to propose marriage on his behalf to a woman of Banu Layth. He entered and mentioned the merit of Salman, his precedence and his Islam, and mentioned that he was proposing their girl so-and-so to them. They said: As for Salman, we shall not marry him, but we shall marry you. So he married her, then went out and said: Something happened, and I am shy to mention it. He said: ""And what is it?"" Abu al-Darda' informed him of the news, and Salman said: ""I am more entitled to be shy before you for proposing her, when Allah had decreed her for you."""
"6064","6051 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri from his father from al-Mughira ibn Shibl from Tariq ibn Shihab: He spent the night with Salman to see his striving. He said: He rose to pray at the end of the night, as if he did not see what he had expected. He mentioned that to him, and Salman, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""Guard these five prayers, for they are expiations for these wounds as long as the mortal blow is not struck. When people pray the night prayer they depart in three states: among them one against whom and not for whom, among them one for whom and not against whom, and among them one neither for whom nor against whom. A man seizes the darkness of the night in people's heedlessness and plunges headlong into disobedience - that is against him and not for him. One for whom and not against whom is a man who seizes the darkness of the night and people's heedlessness and stands praying - that is for him and not against him. Among them one neither for whom nor against whom is a man who prays then sleeps - that is neither for him nor against him. Beware of extremes, and keep to moderation and constancy."""
"6065","6052 - Narrated Mas'ada ibn Sa'd al-Attar al-Makki from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Sufyan ibn Hamza from Kathir ibn Zayd from al-Walid ibn Rabah: Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated to him that there was a dispute between Salman al-Farisi and a person, and Salman said: ""O Allah, if he is lying, do not cause him to die until one of three reaches him."" When his anger subsided, I said: O Abu Abd Allah, with what did you supplicate against this one? He said: ""I inform you: the trial of the Dajjal, the trial of a ruler like the trial of the Dajjal, and greedy avarice coming upon people, when a man gains wealth caring not from what he gained it."""
"6066","6053 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Razzaq from Isra'il from Abi Ishaq from Abi Layla al-Kindi, who said: Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, came with twelve or thirteen riders from among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when the prayer came they said: Step forward, O Abu Abd Allah. He said: We neither lead you in prayer nor marry your women; Allah, mighty and majestic, guided us through you. A man from among the people stepped forward and prayed four units; when he finished Salman said: ""What have we to do with four; half of four would have sufficed us, and we are more in need of the concession."" Abd al-Razzaq said: Meaning in travel."
"6067","6054 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nu'aym from Abd al-Salam ibn Harb from Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib from Abi al-Bakhtari, who said: Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, acquired a slave-girl and said to her in Persian: ""Pray."" She said: No. He said: ""Prostrate once."" She said: No. It was said: O Abu Abd Allah, what avails her a single prostration? He said: ""If she prayed, she would have prayed; one who has a share in Islam is not like one who has no share."""
"6068","6055 - Narrated Isma'il ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri from Zuhayr ibn Abbad al-Rawasibi from Yazid ibn Ata' from al-A'mash from Ibrahim al-Taymi from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, who said: Salman al-Farisi used to say when he finished his food: Praise be to Allah who sufficed us the burden and widened for us the provision."
"6069","6056 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar from Ayyub from Ibn Sirin: Abu al-Darda' used to keep alive the night of Friday and fast its day; Salman came to him - the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having made brotherhood between them - and slept at his place. Abu al-Darda' wanted to rise for his night, but Salman rose to him and did not let him until he slept and broke his fast. Abu al-Darda' came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Uwaymir, Salman knows better than you; do not single out the night of Friday for prayer nor its day for fasting."""
"6070","6057 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki from Muhammad ibn Mu'awiya from al-Hudhayl ibn Bilal al-Fazari from Salim, freedman of Zayd ibn Suhan, who said: I was with my master Zayd ibn Suhan in the market when Salman al-Farisi passed us having bought a wasq of food. Zayd said to him: O Abu Abd Allah, you do this while you are a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: ""When the soul secures its provision it is reassured, devotes itself to worship, and whispering despairs of it."""
"6071","6058 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Ubayda ibn Ma'n al-Mas'udi from his father Muhammad ibn Abi Ubayda from his father from al-A'mash from Abi al-Bakhtari, who said: Al-Ash'ath ibn Qays and Jarir ibn Abd Allah al-Bajali came to Salman, entering upon him in a reed hut on the outskirts of al-Mada'in. They greeted him, then said: Are you Salman al-Farisi? He said: Yes. They said: Are you a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I do not know. They doubted and said: Perhaps he is not the one we seek. He said to them: ""I am your man whom you seek; I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sat with him, but his Companion is only he who enters Paradise with him. What is your need?"" They said: We come to you from a brother of yours in Syria. He said: Who is he? They said: Abu al-Darda'. He said: Then where is his gift he sent with you? They said: He sent no gift with us. He said: Fear Allah and render the trust; no one ever came from him except he came with a gift. They said: Do not press this upon us; we have wealth, so judge concerning it. He said: I do not want your wealth, but I want the gift he sent with you. They said: By Allah, he sent nothing with us except that he said: ""Among you is a man with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when alone with him, sought no one else; when you come to him, convey my greeting of peace to him."" He said: A gift - did I want from you other than this? And what gift is better than peace - a greeting from Allah, blessed and good?"
"6072","6059 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im: Salman al-Farisi sought a place to pray in, and a non-Arab woman said to him: Seek a pure heart and pray wherever you wish. He said: ""You have understood."""
"6073","6060 - Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Khallad ibn Yahya from Fitr ibn Khalifa from Abi Ishaq from Abi al-Hajjaj al-Azdi, who said: I heard Salman al-Farisi in Isfahan say: ""No servant believes until he knows that what struck him could never have missed him, and what missed him could never have struck him."""
"6074","What Salman narrated with a chain; what Abu Hurayra narrated from Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him. 6061 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Rashid al-Asbahani from Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Khalid al-Missisi from Hajjaj ibn Muhammad from Ibn Jurayj from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah from Abu Hurayra, who said: Salman ibn al-Islam narrated to me, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever says: O Allah, I call You to witness, and I call Your angels, the bearers of the Throne, the heavens and those in them, the earths and those in them to witness, and I call all Your creation to witness that You are Allah, there is no god but You, and I disavow whoever refuses that among the first and the last, and I testify that Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger - whoever says it once, a third of him is freed from the Fire; whoever says it twice, two-thirds of him is freed from the Fire; and whoever says it three times is freed from the Fire."""
"6075","6062 - Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Sufi from Zayd ibn al-Hubab from Humayd, freedman of the family of Alqama al-Makki, from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah from Abu Hurayra from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever says: O Allah, I call You to witness, and I call Your angels, the bearers of Your Throne to witness, and I call those in the heavens and the earth to witness that You are Allah, there is no god but You, alone without partner, and I testify that Muhammad is Your servant and Messenger - whoever says it once, Allah frees a third of him from the Fire; whoever says it twice, Allah frees two-thirds of him from the Fire; and whoever says it three times is freed entirely from the Fire."""
"6076","Abu Sa'id from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. 6063 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan al-Tha'labi from Yahya ibn Ya'la from Nasih ibn Abd Allah from Simak ibn Harb from Abi Sa'id al-Khudri from Salman, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, every prophet has a legatee; who is your legatee? He was silent toward me; later he saw me and said: ""O Salman!"" I hastened to him and said: At your service. He said: ""Do you know who was the legatee of Musa?"" I said: Yes, Yusha' ibn Nun. He said: ""Why?"" I said: Because he was the most knowledgeable of them. He said: ""Then my legatee, the keeper of my secret, the best of those I leave behind me, who fulfills my promise and pays my debt, is Ali ibn Abi Talib."" Abu al-Qasim said: His saying: my legatee means that he entrusted him with his family, not with the caliphate; and his saying: the best of those I leave behind me means among his household, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"6077","Ka'b ibn Ujra from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. 6064 - Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Ammar from Sadaqa ibn Khalid from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz from Ubada ibn Nasiy from Ka'b ibn Ujra: Salman passed by him while he was stationed in the land of Persia and said: Shall I not inform you of something that will aid you in your stationing? He said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Stationing a day in the path of Allah is better than fasting a month and standing it in prayer."""
"6078","What Ibn Abbas narrated from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. 6065 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi from Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham al-Sadusi from Ziyad ibn Abd Allah al-Bakka'i - and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr from Yunus ibn Bukayr - and narrated al-Hasan ibn al-Abbas al-Razi from Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida, all from Muhammad ibn Ishaq - from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada from Mahmud ibn Labid from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, from whom Salman narrated his story directly, saying: I was a Persian man from the people of Isfahan, from a village called Jayy; my father was the headman of his village, and I was the dearest of Allah's creation to him. His love for me kept me confined in his house as a slave-girl is confined. I strove in Magianism until I became the keeper of the fire, kindling it and never letting it die for a single hour. My father had a great estate; one day he was busy and said to me: My son, I am busy today with my estate, so go and look after it. He instructed him concerning some of what he wanted therein, then said to me: Do not be long away from me, for if you are long away you will weigh upon me more than my estate and distract me from everything. I went out toward his estate, and passed a church of the Christians, hearing their voices therein praying. I knew nothing of people's affairs because of my father's confining me in his house. When I heard their voices I entered to see what they did; when I saw them their prayer pleased me and I desired their religion, saying: By Allah, this is better than the religion we follow. I did not leave them until the sun set, neglecting my father's estate. Then I said to them: Who taught you this religion? They said: A man in Syria. Then I returned to my father, who had sent seeking me, and I had distracted him from his work. My father said: My son, where were you? Did I not enjoin upon you what I enjoined? I said: I passed people praying in their church, entered among them, and remained with them praying until the sun set. He said: My son, there is no good in that religion; your religion and your fathers' religion is better. Then he confined me in his house, and I sent to the Christians saying: When a caravan comes to you from Syria, inform me of them. A caravan of Christian merchants came to them from Syria, and they informed me; I said to them: When they finish their needs and wish to return to their land, inform me. I cast the iron from my legs and went out with them until I reached Syria. When I arrived I said: Who is the most learned of this religion? They said: The bishop in the church. I came to him and said: I have desired this religion and wish to be with you, serving you in your church, learning from you, and praying with you. He said: Enter. I entered with him, but he was an evil man, commanding charity and urging them to it, yet when they brought him any of it he hoarded it for himself, giving no one anything, until he amassed pitchers of gold and silver. I hated him intensely for what I saw him do. Then he died and the Christians gathered to bury him, and I said to them: This was an evil man; he commanded you to give charity and urged you to it, yet when you brought it to him he hoarded it for himself and gave the poor nothing. They said: How do you know that? I said: I shall show you his treasure. They said: Show us. I showed them, and they brought out gold and silver; when they saw it they said: By Allah, we shall never bury him. They crucified him, then stoned him. Another man was there whom they put in his place. Salman says: I never saw a man who does not pray the five prayers better than him, more abstinent in this world, more desirous of the Hereafter, or more diligent night and day. I loved him as I never loved anything, and remained with him a time until death came to him. I said to him: So-and-so, I was with you and loved you as I never loved anything, and what you see of Allah's decree has come upon you; to whom do you commend me, and what do you command me? He said: My son, by Allah, I know no one upon what I was upon; people have perished, altered, and abandoned much of what they were upon, except a man in Mosul, so-and-so, upon what I was upon - join him. When he died and was buried I joined the man of Mosul, saying: So-and-so, so-and-so commended me at his death to join you, informing me you are upon his way. He said: Stay with me. I stayed with him and found him the best of men upon his companion's way; soon he died, and when death came I said: So-and-so, so-and-so commended me to you and commanded me to join you, and what you see of Allah's decree has come; to whom do you commend me, and what do you command me? He said: My son, I know of no one left whom I command you to go to except a man in Ammuriyya in the land of Rome upon the like of what we are upon. When he died and was buried I joined the man of Ammuriyya, informing him of my story. He said: Stay with me. I stayed with the best of men upon the guidance and way of his companions, and earned until I had small cows and a few sheep. Then Allah's decree came upon him; when he was dying I said: So-and-so, I was with so-and-so who commended me to so-and-so, then so-and-so commended to so-and-so, then so-and-so commended me to you; to whom do you commend me, and whom do you command me to? He said: By Allah, I know of no one among people who remains upon the like of what we are in whom I command you to go to; but the time of a prophet sent with the religion of Ibrahim, peace be upon him, shades you. He emerges in the land of the Arabs toward a land - I think he said - of palm-trees, with marks not hidden: he eats the gift and does not eat charity; between his shoulders is the seal of prophethood. If you can join that land, do so. Then he died and was buried. I remained in Ammuriyya as long as Allah willed, until a group of Kalb merchants passed me. I said to them: Will you carry me to the land of the Arabs for these cows and sheep of mine? They said: Yes. I gave them and they carried me with them; when they reached Wadi al-Qura they wronged me and sold me to a Jewish man. I was with him and saw the palm-trees, hoping for the land my companion described, though it did not settle in my mind. While I was with him, a cousin of his from Banu Qurayza came, bought me from him, and carried me to Medina. By Allah, no sooner did I see it than I recognized it by my companion's description. I stayed there, and Allah sent His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he remained in Mecca as he remained, hearing nothing of him because of my preoccupation with slavery. Then he emigrated to Medina. By Allah, I was atop a date-cluster of my master working therein, my master sitting beneath me, when a cousin of his came and stood over him, saying: May Allah destroy Banu Qayla; by Allah, they gather around a man who came to them from Mecca today claiming to be a prophet. When I heard it joy seized me until I thought I would fall upon my master; I came down from the palm and kept asking his cousin: What does he say? My master became angry and struck me a hard blow, saying: What is this to you? Attend to your work. I said: Nothing; I only wished to ask him about what he said. I had something I had gathered; in the evening I took it and went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Quba'. I entered upon him and said: It has reached me that you are a righteous man with needy stranger companions, and this is something I had as charity; I deemed you more entitled to it than others. I brought it near to him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: ""Eat"" - refraining himself, eating none of it. I said to myself: This is one. Then I left him, gathered something, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved to Medina; then I came to him with it and said: I saw you do not eat charity, and this is a gift honoring you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate from it and ordered his Companions, and they ate; I said to myself: These are two. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Baqi' al-Gharqad, having followed the funeral of a man of the Ansar, while he sat. I greeted him, then circled to look at his back: would I see the seal my companion described? When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me circling, he knew I sought to verify something described to me, and cast his cloak from his back. I looked at the seal and recognized it, threw myself upon him kissing him and weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""Come around."" I came around and sat before him, narrating my story to him as I narrated to you, O Ibn Abbas. It pleased the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that his Companions should hear that. Then slavery kept Salman busy until he missed Badr and Uhud with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Write a contract, O Salman."" I contracted with my master for three hundred palm-trees to plant for him, and forty ounces. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: ""Help your brother."" They helped me with the palms - one man with thirty, one with twenty, one with fifteen, one with ten, one according to what he had - until three hundred palms gathered for me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""Go, O Salman, and let me know, so that I place them with my hand."" I dug for them with my companions' help; when I finished I came and informed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with me to them; I brought him the seedlings and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed them with his hand until we finished. By Him in Whose hand is Salman's soul, not a single seedling died. I delivered the palms, and the wealth remained upon me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought something like a hen's egg of gold from one of the expeditions, and said: ""What has become of the Persian contractee?"" I was called to him, and he said: ""Take this and pay with it what is upon you."" I said: Where does this fall, O Messenger of Allah, against what is upon me? He said: ""Take it, for Allah, mighty and majestic, will pay it on your behalf."" I weighed out to him from it - by Him in Whose hand is Salman's soul - forty ounces, fulfilled their right, and Salman was freed. He attended the Trench with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then missed no battle."
"6079","6066 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Sari ibn Mihran al-Naqid from Bashshar ibn Musa al-Khaffaf from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada from Mahmud ibn Labid from Ibn Abbas, from whom Salman narrated to me that he: Brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a gift, and he and his Companions ate; and brought him charity, and he did not eat from it."
"6080","6067 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki from Muhammad ibn Bakkar al-Ayshi from al-Hajjaj ibn Farrukh al-Wasiti from Ibn Jurayj from Ata' from Ibn Abbas, who said: Salman returned from an absence of his, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, met him, saying: A pleasing servant to Allah. He said: He married into Kinda; on the night he was to go in to his family, the house was adorned and women were therein. He said: Has the Ka'ba moved to Kinda, or is it a red dye? ""My friend Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to take from goods only furnishings like a traveler's furnishings, and to take from women only whom we marry."" The women left and he went in to his family, saying: O woman, will you disobey me or obey me? She said: Rather I obey you in whatever you wish. He said: ""My friend (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us that when one of us goes in to his family, he stands and prays, commands her to pray behind him, supplicates while she says amen."" He did and she did. When he sat in the gathering of Kinda, a man from among the people said to him: How are you this morning, O Abu Abd Allah? How did you find your family last night? He was silent; he repeated a second time, and he said to him: What is with one of you asking about what walls and doors conceal? It suffices one of you to ask about a matter, answered or passed over in silence."
"6081","Anas ibn Malik from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. 6068 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari, both saying, from Mu'alla ibn Mahdi al-Mawsili from Imran ibn Khalid al-Khuza'i from Thabit al-Bunani from Anas ibn Malik, who said: Salman visited Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, while he reclined upon a cushion and threw it to him. Salman said: Allah is greatest; Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth. Umar said: Narrate to us, O Abu Abd Allah. He said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he reclined upon a cushion and threw it to me, then said: ""O Salman, no Muslim visits his Muslim brother and he throws him a cushion honoring him except Allah forgives him."""
"6082","6069 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi' al-Jurjani from Abd al-Razzaq from Ja'far ibn Sulayman from Thabit from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I visited Salman and saw his house in disrepair, and said to him about that. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenanted with me that your provision in this world be like a rider's provision."
"6083","Burayda ibn al-Husayb al-Aslami from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. 6070 - Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Musa ibn Ishaq al-Kinani al-Kufi from Zayd ibn al-Hubab from al-Husayn ibn Waqid from Abd Allah ibn Burayda from his father from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him: He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a tray of fresh dates, and he said: ""What is this?"" He said: This is charity upon you and your Companions. He said: ""O Salman, we do not eat charity."" Salman took it away; the next day Salman came to him with a tray of fresh dates, and he said: ""What is this tray?"" He said: This is a gift. He said to his Companions: ""Draw near and eat."" And he ate."
"6084","Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathila from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. 6071 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Ibn al-Asbahani from Sharik from Ubayd al-Mukattib from Abi al-Tufayl from Salman, who said: I brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) charity and he returned it; I brought him a gift and he accepted it."
"6085","6072 - With the same chain, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me the like of this of gold, and had Uhud been placed in one scale and it placed in another, my ransom's redemption would have outweighed it."
"6086","6073 - Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari from Sa'id ibn Salman al-Wasiti from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Quddus from Ubayd al-Mukattib from Abi al-Tufayl, from whom Salman al-Farisi narrated to me, saying: I was a man from the people of Jayy; the people of my village worshipped piebald horses, and I knew they were upon nothing. It was said to me: The religion you seek is in the West. I went out until I reached Mosul, asking about the best man therein; I was guided to a man in a hermitage. I came to him and said: I am a man from the people of Jayy, come seeking knowledge to learn; take me to yourself that I may serve and accompany you, teaching me from what Allah taught you? He said: Yes. I accompanied him, and he provided for me the like of what he received - vinegar, oil, and grains. I remained with him until death came upon him; I sat at his head weeping for him, and he said: What makes you weep? I said: It makes me weep that I left a land seeking good, and Allah provided me your companionship; you taught me and treated me well, yet death has come upon you and I know not where to go. He said to me: A brother in the Jazira in such-and-such place is upon the truth - go to him, convey my greeting to him, and inform him that I commended you to him and commended his companionship to you. When the man died I went out until I came to the man he described, informing him of the news, conveying his companion's greeting, and informing him that he had perished and commanded me to his companionship. When that man died I went out until I came to the man he described, informing him and conveying his companion's greeting that he had perished and commanded me to his companionship. He took me to himself, providing for me as had been provided with the other; I accompanied him as Allah willed, then death came upon him. When death came I sat at his head weeping, and he said to me: What makes you weep? I said: I left my land seeking good, and Allah provided me the companionship of so-and-so; he treated me well and taught me, and when death came upon him he commended me to you; you took me in, treated me well, taught me, yet death has come upon you and I know not where to turn. He said: Go to a brother of mine near Rome upon the truth - go to him, convey my greeting and accompany him, for he is upon the truth. When the man died I went out until I came to him, informing him of my story and the previous one's bequest. He took me to himself, providing for me as was provided; when death came upon him I sat weeping at his head, and he said to me: What makes you weep? I told my story, saying: Allah provided me your companionship and you treated me well, yet death has come upon you and I know not where to turn. He said: Nowhere; I know of no one left upon the religion of Isa on earth, but this is a time in which a prophet emerges - or has emerged - in Tihama. You are upon the road; none passes you except you ask him about him, and when it reaches you that he has emerged, go to him, for he is the prophet whom Isa, peace be upon him, gave glad tidings of. The sign of that is that between his shoulders is the seal of prophethood, and he eats the gift but does not eat charity. None passed me except I asked him about him, until people of Mecca passed me. I asked them, and they said: Yes, a man has appeared among us claiming to be a prophet. I said to some of them: Will you take me as a servant of one of you, carrying me in turn and feeding me from crumbs, and when you reach your land, if he wishes to sell he sells, and if he wishes to enslave he enslaves? One of them said: I. I became his servant until he brought me to Mecca, placing me in his garden with Abyssinians therein. I went out asking, meeting a woman from my homeland; I asked her, and her household had accepted Islam. She said to me: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is in the Hijr with his Companions when the sparrow cries in Mecca, until dawn brightens for them and they disperse. I went to the garden, spending my night coming and going; the Abyssinians said to me: What is with you? I said: My belly aches - doing that only so they would not miss me when I went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). At the hour the woman told me in which he and his Companions sit, I went out walking until I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he sat with knees drawn up, his Companions around him. I came to him from behind, and the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew what I wanted, loosening his garment; I looked at the seal of prophethood between his shoulders and said: Allah is greatest; this is one. Then I left; the next night I gathered good dates and brought them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), placing them before him. He said: ""What is this?"" I said: It is a gift. He ate from it and said to the people: ""Eat."" I said: I testify that there is no god but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah. He asked me about my affair and I informed him. He said: ""Go and buy yourself."" I went to my master and said: Sell me myself? He said: Yes, upon your planting a hundred palm-trees for me; when they grow, bring me the weight of a date-stone of gold. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""Buy yourself with what he asked and bring me a bucket of water from the well from which you watered those palms."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for me therein, then I watered them; by Allah, I planted a hundred palms, not leaving one but it grew. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing him the palms had grown, and he gave me a piece of gold. I went with it, placing it in the scale's pan and placing a date-stone in the other side; by Allah, the piece of gold did not lift from the ground. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he freed me."
"6087","6074 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qatwani from Sufyan from Ubayd al-Mukattib from Amir ibn Wathila from Salman, who said: I am from Jayy."
"6088","6075 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad from Mu'awiya ibn Hisham from Sufyan from Awf from Abi Uthman from Salman, who said: I am from the people of Ramahurmuz."
"6089","6076 - Narrated Abu Habib Yahya ibn Nafi' al-Misri from Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam from Ibn Lahi'a from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from al-Salm ibn al-Salt al-Abdi from Abi al-Tufayl al-Bakri: Salman the Good narrated to him, saying: I was a man from the people of Jayy, the city of Isfahan. While I was thus, Allah cast into my heart something of the truth of the heavens and the earth, so I went to a man who would not speak to people, being scrupulous, and asked him: Which religion is best? He said: What have you to do with this talk? Do you want a religion other than your father's? I said: No, but I wish to know who is the Lord of the heavens and the earth, and which religion is best. He said: I know no one upon this except a monk in Mosul. I went to him and stayed with him; he was straitened in this world, fasting the day and standing the night, and I worshipped as he worshipped. I remained with him three years, then he died. I said: To whom do you commend me? He said: I know no one of the people of the East upon what I am upon; go to a monk beyond the Jazira and convey my greeting to him. I came to him, conveyed the greeting, and informed him that he had died; I remained with him also three years, then he died. I said: To whom do you command me to go? He said: I know no one of the people of the earth upon what I am upon except an old monk in Ammuriyya, and I doubt whether you will reach him. I went to him and stayed with him; he was a man of means. When death came to him I said to him: Where do you command me to go? He said: I know no one of the people of the earth upon what I am upon; but if you reach a time in which you hear of a man emerging from the House of Ibrahim, peace be upon him - and I doubt you will reach him, though I had hoped to reach him - if you can be with him, do so, for he is upon the religion. The sign of that is that his people say: sorcerer, madman, soothsayer; he eats the gift and does not eat charity; and at the cartilage of his shoulders is the seal of prophethood. While I was thus, a caravan came from the direction of Medina. I said: Who are you? They said: We are from the people of Medina, trading people living by our trade; but a man from the household of Ibrahim, peace be upon him, has emerged and come to us, his people fighting him, and we feared he would come between us and our trade, yet he has taken Medina. I said: What do they say of him? They said: They say sorcerer, madman, soothsayer. I said: This is the sign; guide me to your master. I came to him and said: Will you carry me to Medina? He said: What will you give me? I said: I find nothing to give you except that I am a servant to you. He carried me; when I arrived he placed me in his palms, and I drew water as a camel draws until my back and chest were galled, finding no one who understood my speech, until a Persian old woman came drawing water. I spoke to her and she understood my speech. I said to her: Where is the man who emerged - guide me to him? She said: He will pass you early morning when he prays the dawn at daybreak. I went out gathering dates; in the morning I came and offered him the dates. He said: ""What is this - charity or gift?"" I gestured that it was charity. He said: ""Go to those"" - his Companions being with him - and they ate while he did not eat. I said: This is the sign. The next day I came with dates, and he said: ""What is this?"" I said: This is a gift. He ate and called his Companions, and they ate. Then he saw me reaching to look at the seal, knew, and cast his cloak; I turned it over and embraced him, and he said: ""What is with you?"" He asked me and I informed him of my story. He said: ""You stipulated to them that you are a servant; buy yourself from them."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought him for bringing them a hundred palm-trees and forty ounces of gold, then he is free. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Plant."" He planted, then said: ""Go, cast the bucket upon the well, then do not lift it until it rises; when it fills it rises. Then sprinkle at their roots."" He did so, and the palms grew with the swiftest growth. He said: Glory be to Allah, we never saw the like of this servant; this servant has a matter. The people gathered around him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him raw gold - forty ounces therein."
"6090","Abu al-Ja'd al-Damri from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. 6077 - Narrated al-Hasan ibn Hammad ibn Fadala al-Sayrafi al-Basri from Harun ibn Musa al-Farwi from Abu Damra Anas ibn Iyad from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqama from Ubayda ibn Sufyan al-Hadrami from Abi al-Ja'd al-Damri from Salman al-Farisi: He passed by Ibn al-Simt while he stationed in the path of Allah, and said to him: Shall I not encourage you in what you are in? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Stationing a day in the path of Allah is better than fasting a month and standing it in prayer; whoever dies stationed in the path of Allah, his deed - or his deeds - continue for him and he is protected from the trial of the grave."""
"6091","Abu Sabra al-Jufi - he has companionship - from Salman. Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar; Abbad ibn Ahmad al-Arzami told us; my uncle Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman told us, from his father, from Imran ibn Muslim, from Khaythama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sabra, from Salman al-Farisi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Salman, say often: My Lord, settle my debt for me and enrich me so I am free of poverty."
"6092","Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid al-Nakhai from Salman. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Salman al-Farisi who said: The polytheists said: We see your companion teaches you everything, even how to relieve yourselves. He said: He forbids us from facing the qiblah, and that any of us clean himself with his right hand; and he forbade us dung and bones, and said: None of you suffices with less than three stones."
"6093","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi; Muawiya ibn Amr told us; Zaida told us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Salman who said: A man from the polytheists said to me: By Allah, I see your companion teaches you everything, even relieving yourselves. He said: Yes. He forbade us from facing the qiblah, and from cleaning ourselves with our right hands, and from using less than three stones, and from cleaning ourselves with dung or bone."
"6094","Narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri; Yahya ibn Salman al-Jufi told us; Hafs ibn Ghiyath told us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us from facing the qiblah while relieving ourselves, and from cleaning ourselves with less than three stones, and from cleaning ourselves with bone or dung."
"6095","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba told us; Abu Muawiya and Waki told us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid who said: They said to Salman: Your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has taught you everything. He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade facing the qiblah with stool or urine, and that any of us clean himself with the right hand, or with fewer than three stones, or with dung or bone."
"6096","Abu Wail Shaqiq ibn Salama from Salman. Narrated Bishr ibn Musa; Khallad ibn Yahya told us. And narrated Abu Khalifa al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab and Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri; both said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi told us; both said: Qays ibn al-Rabi told us, from Uthman ibn Shabur, from Shaqiq ibn Salama who said: We entered upon Salman and he brought near whatever was in the house, and said: Were it not that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us - or: we were forbidden - that a man burden himself for his brother, I would have burdened myself for you."
"6097","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari told us; Husayn ibn Muhammad told us, from Sulayman ibn Qarm, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us burdening ourselves for a guest."
"6098","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj; Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Tusi told us; Husayn ibn Muhammad told us; Sulayman ibn Qarm told us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq ibn Salama who said: I went with a companion of mine to Salman al-Farisi, and Salman said: Were it not that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us burdening ourselves, I would have burdened myself for you. Then he brought bread and salt. My companion said: If only our salt had some thyme. So Salman sent his ablution vessel and pawned it, then brought thyme. When we ate, my companion said: Praise be to Allah who has given us enjoyment of what He provided. Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If you had been content with what Allah provided you, my ablution vessel would not be pawned."
"6099","Narrated Musa ibn Zakariyya al-Tustari; Amr ibn al-Husayn al-Uqayli told us; Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali told us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When the heart of the believer trembles in the path of Allah, his sins fall away, just as the date-clusters of the palm-tree fall."
"6100","Zayd ibn Wahb from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli and Muadh ibn al-Muthanna; both said: Ali ibn al-Madini told us; Said ibn Muhammad al-Warraq told us, from Musa al-Juhani, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Salman who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The most satiated of people in this world will be the hungriest on the Day of Resurrection."
"6101","And I heard him say: This world is the prison of the believer and the paradise of the disbeliever."
"6102","Masruq ibn al-Ajda from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ali al-Jarudi al-Naysaburi; Ahmad ibn Hafs told us; my father told me; Ibrahim ibn Tahman told us, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Masruq, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When the believing servant stands in prayer, his sins are placed upon his head, then they scatter from him, just as the clusters of the palm-tree scatter right and left."
"6103","Al-Qartha al-Dabbi from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri; Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi told us; Abu Awana told us, from Mughira, from Ziyad ibn Kulayb, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, from Qartha, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Salman, do you know what Friday is? I said: It is the day in which your father - or: your forefather - was gathered. He said: No, but I shall tell you about Friday: there is no Muslim who purifies himself, puts on his best clothes, applies perfume of his family if they have perfume, otherwise water, then comes to the mosque and listens quietly until the imam comes out, then prays, except it is an expiation for him between it and the next Friday, as long as bloodshed is avoided, and that is throughout the year."
"6104","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba told us; Ishaq ibn Mansur told us, from Abu Kudayna, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, from Qartha, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6105","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Uthman ibn Abi Shayba told us; Jarir told us, from Mansur, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, from al-Qartha al-Dabbi - and al-Qartha was among the first reciters - from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Salman, Friday is the day in which your father - or: your forefather - was gathered. There is no man who purifies himself on Friday as commanded, then leaves his house until he comes to the Friday prayer, then sits and listens quietly until he completes his prayer, except it is an expiation for what came before it of the past Friday."
"6106","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami; Abu Kurayb told us; Hasan ibn Atiyya told us, from Qays, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Qartha, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Salman, do you know what Friday is? In it your father Adam was gathered."
"6107","And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bathes on Friday, then comes to the mosque, he is forgiven - or: expiation is made for him."
"6108","Abu Zibyan al-Janbi from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Idris ibn Jafar al-Attar al-Baghdadi; Shuja ibn al-Walid told us, from Qabus ibn Abi Zibyan, from his father, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Salman, do not hate me, lest you leave your religion. I said: How could I hate you, when through you Allah guided me? He said: Do not hate the Arabs, lest you hate me."
"6109","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi; Shihab ibn Abbad al-Abdi told us; Shuja ibn al-Walid told us; Qabus ibn Abi Zibyan told us, from his father, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me, and he mentioned the same as it."
"6110","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad; Ibrahim ibn Yusuf al-Maqdisi told us; Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi told us; Sufyan told us, from al-Amash, from Abu Zibyan, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is nothing better than a thousand like it except man."
"6111","Zadhan Abu Amr from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib; Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar told us. And narrated Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi; Abu Bilal al-Ashari told us; both said: Qays ibn al-Rabi told us, from Abu Hashim, from Zadhad, from Salman who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I read in the Torah: The blessing of food is in ablution before it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The blessing of food is in ablution before it and after it."
"6112","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi; they said: Hilal ibn Bishr told us; Abd al-Malik ibn Musa al-Tawil told us, from Abu Hashim al-Rummani, from Zadhad, from Salman, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: One who loves you loves me, and one who hates you hates me."
"6113","Narrated Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Hinnai; Ahmad ibn Abda told us; al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan told us; Jafar ibn Ziyad al-Ahmar told us, from Yazid ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu Hashim, from Zadhad, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I got a nosebleed in the presence of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he commanded me to renew my ablution."
"6114","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; al-Qasim ibn Dinar told us; Ishaq ibn Mansur told us; Huraym ibn Sufyan told us, from Amr al-Qurashi, from Abu Hashim, from Zadhad, from Salman who said: Blood flowed from my nose, so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Renew ablution for what has occurred."
"6115","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Fasawi; Khalaf ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Sarakhsi told us; Abu al-Sabbah Abd al-Ghafur ibn Said al-Ansari told us, from Abu Hashim al-Rummani, from Zadhad, from Salman, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Three things are from the Ignorance: boasting of lineages, disparaging lineages, and wailing."
"6116","And with its isnad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is no servant who seeks to rise a degree in this world and rises, except Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, lowers him in the Hereafter greater than it. Then he recited: And the Hereafter is greater in degrees and greater in excellence."
"6117","And with its isnad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever is pleased that Satan find with him neither food nor a midday rest, let him give salam when he enters his house, and mention Allah's name over his food."
"6118","And with its isnad, he said: Our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to ransom Muslim captives and to give their beggars. Then he said: Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs; and whoever leaves a debt, it is upon me, and upon the rulers after me, from the treasury of the Muslims."
"6119","And with its isnad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever dies in one of the two Sanctuaries deserves my intercession, and on the Day of Resurrection he will be among the secure."
"6120","Narrated Muhammad ibn Nuh ibn Harb al-Askari; Muhammad ibn Harun al-Razi told us; Husayn ibn Ulwan told us; Amr ibn Khalid told us, from Abu Hashim al-Rummani, from Zadhad, from Salman who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: In Paradise there are open plains, so plant them often. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is their planting? He said: Glory be to Allah, and praise be to Allah, and there is no god but Allah, and Allah is Most Great."
"6121","Narrated Muhammad ibn Nuh al-Askari; Wahb ibn Hafs al-Harrani told us; Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud told us; Amr ibn Khalid told us, from Abu Hashim, from Zadhad, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me visiting me in illness, and when he wanted to leave he said: O Salman, may Allah remove your harm, forgive your sin, and grant you health in your religion and your body until your appointed term."
"6122","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Usayd al-Asbahani; Abu al-Asbat Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Kufi told us; Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Hammad told us, from Abu Khalid, from Abu Hashim, from Zadhad, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever feeds a sick person his craving, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will feed him from the fruits of Paradise."
"6123","Narrated Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi; Qutayba ibn Said told us; Abd al-Karim ibn Muhammad al-Jurjani told me, from Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Abu Hashim al-Rummani, from Zadhad, from Salman who said: No one was of greater sanctity than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when his Companions wrote to him they wrote: From so-and-so to Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6124","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; both said: Yahya al-Himmani told us; Qays ibn al-Rabi told us, from Muhammad ibn Rustum, from Zadhad, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves al-Hasan and al-Husayn, I love him; and whomever I love, Allah loves; and whoever hates them both, I hate him; and whomever I hate, Allah hates him."
"6125","Salama al-Ijli from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki; Qays ibn Hafs al-Darimi told us; Maslama ibn Alqama al-Mazini told us; Dawud ibn Abi Hind told us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Salama al-Ijli who said: A nephew of mine from the desert, called Qudama, came to me and said: I wish to meet Salman al-Farisi and greet him. We went out and found him at al-Madain, when he was over twenty thousand, and found him on a bed weaving palm leaves. We greeted him. I said: O Abu Abd Allah, this is a nephew of mine who came to me from the desert and wishes to greet you. He said: And upon him peace and Allah's mercy. I said: He claims he loves you. He said: May Allah love him. We talked and said to him: O Abu Abd Allah, will you not tell us of your origin, and from whom you are? He said: As for my origin and from whom I am, I am a man of the people of Ramahurmuz; we were Magians. A Christian man from the people of the Jazira, whose mother was from us, came to us, settled among us and took a monastery among us. I was in the Persian school, and there was always a boy with me in the school who came beaten, weeping; his parents had beaten him. I said to him one day: What makes you weep? He said: My parents beat me. I said: Why do they beat you? He said: I come to the master of this monastery, and when they know it they beat me; if you came to him you would hear wondrous speech. I said: Take me with you. We came to him and he told us of the beginning of creation, the creation of heaven and earth, Paradise and Hell; he told us wondrous reports. I kept going to him with him. Boys of the school noticed us and began coming with us. When the villagers saw that, they came to him and said: You have neighbored us and seen only good neighborliness from us, yet we see our boys coming to you; we fear you will corrupt them against us; leave us. He said: Yes. He said to the boy who came to him: Come out with me. He said: I cannot; you know my parents' severity toward me. I said: But I shall come out with you; I was an orphan without father. I went out with him. We took the mountain of Ramahurmuz, walking and trusting, eating tree fruits, until we came to the Jazira; we came to Nasibin. My companion said: O Salman, here are a people, the most devout of earth's people, and I wish to meet them. We came to them on Sunday when they had gathered. My companion greeted them; they greeted him, welcomed him and said: Where were you absent? He said: I was among brethren of mine from Persia. We talked. Then my companion said to me: Rise, O Salman, let us go. I said: No, leave me with these. He said: You cannot bear what they bear: they fast Sunday to Sunday and do not sleep this night; among them is a man of kings' sons who left kingship and entered worship. I stayed among them till evening; they went one by one to the cave wherein each stayed. When evening came, that man of kings' sons said: Who is this boy? Do not neglect him; let one of you take him. They said: Take him yourself. He said to me: Come, O Salman. He took me to his cave, saying: O Salman, here is bread and condiment; eat when hungry, fast when active, pray as you please, and sleep when lazy. Then he stood in prayer and spoke to me only that, nor looked at me. Grief seized me those seven days, none speaking to me, until Sunday; we went to their gathering place where they gather every Sunday and break fast together. [Next weeks likewise:] I stayed with the next keeper a week in grief, then a third; when Sunday came we returned to them and they broke fast and gathered, and he said to them: I intend Bayt al-Maqdis. They said: What do you seek there? He said: I have not visited it. They said: We fear something happen to you and another tend you; we wished to tend you. He said: I must visit it. When I heard that I rejoiced, saying: We shall travel and meet people, and the grief I felt will leave. I went out with him; he fasted Sunday to Sunday, prayed all night and walked by day; when we camped he prayed, until we reached Bayt al-Maqdis. At the gate was a crippled man begging. He said: Give me. He said: I have nothing. We entered Bayt al-Maqdis; when its people saw him they welcomed him gladly, and he said to them: This boy of mine, treat him well. They took me and fed me bread and meat, while he entered prayer and did not turn to me until Sunday. Then he turned and said to me: O Salman, I wish to rest my head; when the shadow reaches such-and-such place, wake me. He slept; the shadow reached the mark and I did not wake him, pitying him for his striving. He awoke startled, saying: O Salman, did I not tell you to wake me when the shadow reaches so? I said: Yes, but pity for you for your exertion held me. He said: Woe to you, O Salman, I hate that any time pass me wherein I do no good for Allah. Then he said: O Salman, know that the best of our religion today is Christianity. I said: And will there be after today a religion better than Christianity? - a word cast upon my tongue - He said: Yes; soon a prophet will be sent who eats gifts and does not eat charity; between his shoulders is the seal of prophethood; when you meet him, follow and believe him. I said: Even if he commands me to leave Christianity? He said: Yes, for he is a prophet who commands only truth and speaks only truth; by Allah, if I met him and he commanded me to fall into fire, I would fall. Then we left Bayt al-Maqdis and passed that crippled man, who said: You entered and gave me nothing; now you leave, so give me. He looked, saw none around, said: Give me your hand. He took his hand, saying: Rise by Allah's permission. He rose sound and upright and headed to his family; I followed him with my eyes in wonder. My companion hastened, I followed; a party of Kalb Bedouins met me and enslaved me, carried me on a camel bound, and traders passed me until I fell to Madina. A man of the Ansar bought me and placed me in his palm-grove. Thence I learned palm-leaf work: I buy leaves for a dirham, work them and sell for two dirhams; I put a dirham back into leaves and spend a dirham, loving to eat from my hand's work - and he was then governor over twenty thousand. News reached us at Madina that a man had arisen at Makka claiming Allah sent him. We stayed as Allah willed, then he emigrated to us. I said: By Allah I shall test him. I went to the market, bought a camel's meat for a dirham, cooked it and made a dish of tharid, carried it on my shoulder to him and placed it before him. He said: What is this, charity or gift? I said: Charity. He said to his Companions: Eat in Allah's name, and held back and did not eat. I stayed days, then bought meat again for a dirham, made the like, carried it to him and placed it before him. He said: What is this, gift or charity? I said: Rather a gift. He said to his Companions: Eat in Allah's name, and ate with them. I said: By Allah, he eats gifts and does not eat charity. I looked and saw between his shoulders the seal of prophethood like a pigeon's egg, and I accepted Islam. Then I said to him one day: O Messenger of Allah, which people are the Christians? He said: There is no good in them. I loved them intensely for their striving. Then I asked him after days: O Messenger of Allah, which people are the Christians? He said: There is no good in them nor in those who love them. I said to myself: By Allah, I love them. That was when he sent expeditions and bared the sword: one expedition entering, one leaving, the sword dripping. I said: He will now speak of me that I love them, and send to me and strike my neck. I sat in the house. A messenger came one day, saying: O Salman, answer. I said: Whom? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: By Allah, this is what I feared. I said: Yes, till I catch up. He said: No, by Allah, until you come. I told myself that if he left I would flee. He took me to him. When he saw me he smiled, saying: O Salman, rejoice, Allah has relieved you. Then he recited to me these verses: Those to whom We gave the Book before it believe in it; and when it is recited to them they say: We believe in it; it is the truth from our Lord; we were Muslims before it; those will be given their reward twice for their patience, repel evil with good, and spend of what We provided them; and when they hear idle talk they turn from it and say: To us our deeds and to you your deeds; peace be upon you; we seek not the ignorant. I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Him who sent you with truth, I heard him say: If I met him and he commanded me to fall into fire, I would fall; he is a prophet who speaks only truth and commands only truth."
"6126","Abu Uthman al-Nahdi from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him; Asim ibn Sulayman al-Ahwal from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami; Said ibn Amr al-Ashathi told us; Hafs ibn Ghiyath told us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three at whom Allah will not look on the Day of Resurrection: a white-haired old adulterer, a poor man who is arrogant, and a man who makes Allah his merchandise: he buys only with an oath and sells only with an oath."
"6127","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal; my father told me; Hisham ibn Lahiq al-Madaini Abu Uthman told us, in the year 185; Asim al-Ahwal told us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people of kindness in this world are the people of kindness in the Hereafter, and the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter."
"6128","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal; my father, may Allah have mercy on him, told me; Hisham ibn Lahiq told us; Asim al-Ahwal told us, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: Fever sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to it: Who are you? It said: I am fever; I wither flesh and suck blood. He said: Go to the people of Quba. So it came to them, and they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with their faces yellowed, and complained of fever to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: As you wish: if you wish I supplicate Allah and He removes it from you, and if you wish you leave it and it drops the rest of your sins. They said: Rather leave it, O Messenger of Allah."
"6129","And with its isnad, from Salman, who said: A man came and greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And upon you peace and the mercy of Allah. Then another came and said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and the mercy of Allah. He said: And upon you peace and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Then another came and said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: And upon you. The man said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so and so-and-so came to you and you greeted them with better than you greeted me? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You left nothing, for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: And when you are greeted with a greeting, greet with better than it or return it. So I returned the greeting to you."
"6130","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari; Muhammad ibn al-Muammal ibn al-Sabbah told us; Bakr ibn Yahya ibn Zabban told us; Mindal ibn Ali told us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - three times - and he said: What do you count as a martyr among you? They said: One who is killed in the path of Allah. He said: Then the martyrs of my ummah are few: being killed in the path of Allah is martyrdom, plague is martyrdom, childbirth is martyrdom, burning is martyrdom, drowning is martyrdom, consumption is martyrdom, and abdominal disease is martyrdom."
"6131","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani; Abbad ibn al-Walid al-Anbari told us; Bakr ibn Yahya ibn Zabban told us; Mindal told us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you count as a martyr among you? They said: One who is killed in the path of Allah. He said: Then the martyrs of my ummah are few: being killed is martyrdom, plague is martyrdom, abdominal disease is martyrdom, and childbirth is martyrdom."
"6132","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba told us; Abu Muawiya told us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: The sun will be given the heat of ten years on the Day of Resurrection, then brought near the skulls of people - then he mentioned the hadith - he said: They will come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: O Prophet of Allah, you are the one by whom Allah opened, and forgave you what preceded of your sin and what followed, and you see what we are in, so intercede for us to our Lord. He will say: I am your man. Then he will go out herding the people until he reaches the gate of Paradise, take a golden ring on the gate and knock. It will be said: Who is this? It will be said: Muhammad. It will be opened for him, and he will come until he stands before Allah and prostrate. He will be called: Raise your head, ask and you will be given, intercede and you will be granted intercession. That is the Praised Station."
"6133","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani; al-Qasim ibn Yazid ibn Kulayb told us; Muhammad ibn Fudayl told us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not be the first to enter the market, nor the last to leave it, for in it Satan laid his eggs and hatched."
"6134","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Bundar al-Asbahani; Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Askar told us; Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi told us; Sufyan told us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: People came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said to them: Do you have food? They said: Yes. He said: Do you have drink? They said: Yes. He said: Do you strain it? They said: Yes. He said: And do you cool it? They said: Yes. He said: Their end is like the end of this world: one of you goes to behind his house and holds his nose from its stench."
"6135","Sulayman al-Taymi from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman al-Farisi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man is in a barren land and prayer comes, let him perform ablution; if he finds no water let him do tayammum. If he calls iqama, his two angels pray with him; and if he calls adhan and iqama, there prays behind him of Allah's troops whose ends cannot be seen."
"6136","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Jarir al-Suri; Zakariyya ibn Nafi al-Arsuqi told us; al-Sari ibn Yahya told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding his acceptance of Islam, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madina, I made food and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: What is this, O Salman? I said: Charity. He said to his Companions: Eat, and he did not eat. Then I returned and gathered food and brought it to him. He said: What is this, O Salman? I said: A gift. He struck with his hand and ate, and said to his Companions: Eat. I said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me about the Christians? He said: There is no good in them nor in those who love them. So I stood up heavy-hearted, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent down: You will surely find the most intense of people in enmity to the believers the Jews and those who associate others - until: overflowing with tears. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to me and said to me: O Salman, your companions are those whom Allah mentioned."
"6137","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Jarir al-Suri; Zakariyya ibn Nafi al-Arsuqi told us; al-Sari ibn Yahya told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Qatada, from Uqba ibn Abd al-Ghafir, from Abu Said al-Khudri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man from those before you was granted wealth and children by Allah, and he said to his family: When I die, burn me until I become charcoal, then crush me, then scatter me; for if my Lord gains power over me He will punish me with a punishment He punishes none of the worlds with. They did that, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commanded him and he was gathered, and there he was standing before Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. He said: What carried you to do what you did? He said: Fear of You, my Lord. So He forgave him."
"6138","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Jarir al-Suri; Zakariyya ibn Nafi al-Arsuqi told us; al-Sari ibn Yahya told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it, and he said: Scatter half of me on land and half of me in the sea."
"6139","Narrated Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri; Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani told us; Sayf ibn Harun told us; Sulayman al-Taymi told us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about furs, ghee and cheese. He said: The lawful is what Allah made lawful in His Book, and the unlawful is what Allah made unlawful in His Book, and what He was silent about is of what He pardoned."
"6140","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji; both said: Bishr ibn Musa told us; Ashath ibn Ashath al-Sadani told us; Imran al-Qattan told us; Sulayman al-Taymi told us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Muslim prays with his sins raised upon his head; each time he prostrates they fall away. He finishes when he finishes his prayer, with his sins fallen away."
"6141","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi; Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq told us. And narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal told us; both said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman told us, from his father, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah created a hundred mercies: one mercy by which this creation shows mercy to one another, and ninety-nine on the Day of Resurrection."
"6142","Narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf; Said ibn Abi Maryam told us; Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Attar told us; Abu Abd Allah al-Basri told me, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Blessing is in three: in the congregation, in tharid, and in suhur."
"6143","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib and Musa ibn Harun; both said: Said ibn Yaqub al-Talqani told us; Yahya ibn al-Durays told us; Abu Mawdud told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Nothing repels the decree except supplication, and nothing increases life except kindness."
"6144","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi and Musa ibn Harun; both said: Muhammad ibn al-Faraj told us; Abu Hammam Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about locusts, and he said: The most numerous of Allah's troops on earth."
"6145","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi; Muhammad ibn al-Faraj told us. And narrated al-Abbas ibn Hamdan al-Hanafi; Jamil ibn al-Hasan told us; both said: Abu Hammam Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan told us; Sulayman al-Taymi told us, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is shy from a servant raising his hands to Him and returning them disappointed."
"6146","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad; Abu al-Rabi al-Harithi told us; Yazid ibn Sufyan ibn Abd Allah ibn Rawaha told us; Sulayman al-Taymi told us, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not be the first to enter the market, nor the last to leave it, for it is the battlefield - or he said: the resting place - of Satan, and his banner is there."
"6147","And with its isnad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That the belly of one of you fill with pus until it overflows is better than that it fill with poetry."
"6148","And with its isnad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A sin that is not forgiven, a sin that is not left, and a sin that is forgiven. As for what is not forgiven, it is associating partners with Allah. As for what is forgiven, it is a sin between him and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. And as for what is not left, it is the wronging of servants by one another."
"6149","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami told us; Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari told us; Sulayman al-Taymi told us, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Guarding a day in the path of Allah is better than this world and what is in it, and the space of a bow of one of you in Paradise is better than this world and what is in it."
"6150","Narrated Ahmad ibn Ali al-Jarudi; Hafs ibn Amr al-Rabali told us; Ziyad told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Let not the call of Bilal prevent any of you from his suhur, for Bilal calls to return your standing one who is in prayer, and to waken your sleeper."
"6151","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram; Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Sufi told us; Said ibn Amr al-Ashathi told us; Sufyan ibn Uyayna told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, that Bilal said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do not precede me with Amin."
"6152","Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr and al-Bazzar; Humayd ibn al-Rabi told us; Ali ibn Asim told us; Sulayman al-Taymi told us, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O son of Adam, three: one is Mine, one is yours, and one is between Me and you. As for what is Mine: worship Me and associate nothing with Me. As for what is yours: whatever deed you do, I reward you for it; if I forgive, I am the Forgiving, the Merciful. And as for what is between Me and you: from you supplication and asking, and upon Me answering and giving."
"6153","Narrated Muhammad ibn Nuh al-Askari; Yahya ibn Yazid al-Ahwazi told us; Abu Hammam Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan told us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever eats clay helps in killing himself."
"6154","Thabit al-Bunani from Abu Uthman from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Amir ibn Sayyar told us; Said ibn Zarbi told us, from Thabit, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever performs ablution in his house and perfects it, then comes to the mosque, he is a visitor of Allah, and it is due upon the Visited to honor the visitor."
"6155","Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar; Ahmad ibn Ashras al-Warraq told us; Abu Jabir Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik told us; al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar told us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man entered the mosque while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prayed, and he said: Is there no man who will give charity to this one and pray with him."
"6156","Said al-Jurayri from Abu Uthman from Salman. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Asim ibn Ali told us; Ishaq al-Azraq told us; al-Jurayri told us, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, he raised it, who said: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: When My servant draws near to Me a span, I draw near to him a cubit; when he draws near to Me a cubit, I draw near to him a fathom; and when he comes to Me walking, I come to him running."
"6157","Narrated Yaqub ibn Mujahid al-Basri; al-Mundhir ibn al-Walid al-Jarudi told us; my father told us; Shaddad Abu Talha al-Rasibi told us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No people raise their palms to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, asking Him anything, except it is due upon Allah to place in their hands what they asked."
"6158","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Shuayb al-Simsar; Khalid ibn Khidash told us. And narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Abd Allah ibn Muawiya al-Jumahi told us; both said: Salih al-Murri told us; Abu Masud al-Jurayri told us, from Abu Uthman, who said: Salman wrote to Abu al-Darda: My brother, let the mosque be your house, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The mosque is the house of every pious one, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has guaranteed for those whose houses are the mosques: rest, mercy, and passing over the Sirat."
"6159","Dawud ibn Abi Hind from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi from Salman. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Uthman ibn Abi Shayba told us; Abu Muawiya told us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, created on the day He created the heavens and the earth a hundred mercies, each mercy the layers of what is between heaven and earth; one mercy by which a mother is tender to her child and beasts to one another, and He delayed ninety-nine until the Day of Resurrection."
"6160","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Mukram al-Baghdadi; Said ibn Yahya ibn Shuba al-Umawi told us; my uncle told us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim who performs ablution and perfects it, except he is a visitor of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and it is due upon the Visited to honor His visitor."
"6161","Awn ibn Abi Shaddad from Abu Uthman from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar told us; Ubays ibn Maymun told us, from Awn ibn Abi Shaddad, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever goes early to the dawn prayer is given a quarter of faith, and whoever goes early to the market is given the banner of Iblis, and he is with the first who go early and the last who return."
"6162","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal; Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan al-Allaf told us; Ubays ibn Maymun told us, from Awn ibn Abi Shaddad, from Abu Uthman, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam began strange, and it will return strange."
"6163","Jafar ibn Maymun al-Anmati from Abu Uthman from Salman. Narrated Muhammad ibn Kamil al-Sarraj and Muhammad ibn Rashid al-Asbahani; both said: Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari told us; Abu Usama told us; Jafar ibn Maymun told me; he heard Abu Uthman narrating, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is shy when the servant raises his hands, to return them empty with nothing in them."
"6164","Abu al-Awwam al-Jazzar from Abu Uthman from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Muslim ibn Ibrahim told us; Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Umara al-Dari told us; Faid Abu al-Awwam al-Jazzar told us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Salman, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about locusts, and he said: That is the most numerous of Allah's troops; I neither eat it nor forbid it."
"6165","Al-Jad Abu Uthman from Abu Uthman from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri told us; Salim ibn Ghaylan told us, who said: I heard Jad Abu Uthman say: Abu Uthman al-Nahdi told me, from Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a Muslim meets his Muslim brother and takes his hand, their sins fall away from them, just as leaves fall from the dry tree on a windy day; otherwise they are forgiven, even if their sins were like the foam of the sea."
"6166","Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan from Abu Uthman from Salman. Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi; Ibrahim ibn Humayd al-Tawil told us. And narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal told us; both said: Hammad ibn Salama told us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi who said: I was with Salman al-Farisi under a tree, and he took a dry branch and shook it until its leaves fell. Then he said: Ask me why I do this? I said: Why do you do it? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did so; I was with him under a tree, and he took a dry branch, shook it until its leaves fell, then said: Will you not ask me why I do this, O Salman? I said: Why do you do it? He said: When a Muslim performs ablution and perfects it, then prays the five prayers, his sins fall away, just as these leaves fell. Then he recited this verse: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day and in the hours of the night; indeed good deeds remove evil deeds; that is a reminder for those who remember."
"6167","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad; Zayd ibn al-Harish told us. And narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar; Humayl ibn al-Hasan al-Ataki told us; both said: Abu Hammam Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan told us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Uthman who said: Salman led us in prayer, then stood to a dry tree branch, moved it and its leaves fell. Then he said: Do you know why I did this? They said: No. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, then stood to a dry tree branch, moved it and its leaves fell, and said: When a servant performs ablution and perfects it, then prays and perfects the prayer, his sins fall away as the leaves of this tree fall - and the wording is of Abdan's hadith."
"6168","Uthman ibn Ghiyath from Abu Uthman from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar; Abd Allah ibn Ishaq al-Attar told us; Khalid ibn Hamza al-Attar told us; Uthman ibn Ghiyath told us; Abu Uthman told us, from Salman, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man will come on the Day of Resurrection with good deeds by which he thinks he will be saved, but a man will keep coming who was wronged by him, and his good deeds will be taken and given to the wronged, until no good deed remains. Then one who seeks from him will come with nothing left of his good deeds, and the sins of the wronged will be taken and placed upon his sins."
"6169","Abu al-Ala - I think Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir - from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Hashim ibn Marthad al-Tabarani; Safwan ibn Salih told us; Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman ibn Abi al-Jawn told us, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Ala, from Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is night prayer, for it is the practice of the righteous before you, a means of nearness to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, an expiation for evil deeds, a prevention from sin, and a repeller of disease from the body."
"6170","Abu Qurra al-Kindi from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Qasim ibn Muhammad al-Dallal al-Kufi; Mukhawwal ibn Ibrahim told us; Israil told us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Qurra al-Kindi, from Salman al-Farisi who said: I gathered firewood and made food, and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was little. I placed it before him, and he said: What is this? I said: Charity. He said to his Companions: Eat, and he did not eat. I said: This is one of his signs. Then I stayed as long as Allah willed, then said to my mistress: Grant me a day? She said: Yes. I went, gathered firewood, sold it for more than that, made food, and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was among his Companions. I placed it before him, and he said: What is this? I said: A gift. He placed his hand and said to his Companions: Take, in Allah's name. I said: I bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. He said: How so? I told of the man, then said: Will he enter Paradise, O Messenger of Allah, for he told me you are a prophet? He said: None enters Paradise except a Muslim soul."
"6171","Amr ibn Abi Qurra from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Ahmad ibn Yunus told us; Zaida ibn Qudama told us; Amr ibn Qays told us, from Amr ibn Abi Qurra, who said: Hudhayfa was at al-Madain, and he mentioned things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to some of his Companions in anger. People who heard that from Hudhayfa would go to Salman and mention Hudhayfa's words to him, and Salman would say: Hudhayfa knows best what he says. They would return to Hudhayfa and say: We mentioned it to Salman, and he neither confirmed nor denied you. So Hudhayfa came to Salman while he was in his dome, and said: O Salman, what prevents you from confirming what I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Salman said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would become angry and say in anger to some of his Companions, and would fall ill and say in illness to some of his Companions. Will you not stop until you bequeath men love of men, and men hatred of men, until you cause dissension and division? You know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: Whichever man of my ummah I insult, or curse from my anger - for I am from the children of Adam, I anger as they anger - and Allah sent me only as a mercy to the worlds, so make it a prayer for him on the Day of Resurrection. By Allah, you will stop, or I shall write about you to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6172","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba told us. And narrated Jafar ibn Ahmad al-Shami al-Kufi; Abu Kurayb told us; both said: Abu Usama told us; Misar told us, from Umar ibn Qays, from Amr ibn Abi Qurra who said: Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Hudhayfa would narrate things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his anger to people. I said: O Hudhayfa, you will stop, or I shall write about you to Umar. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am from the children of Adam, so whichever servant of my ummah I curse or insult without its full merit, make it a prayer for him."
"6173","Aws ibn Damaj from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil and Isa ibn Muhammad al-Simsar al-Wasiti; they said: Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari told us; Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri told us; Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Abbas told us, from Abu Ishaq, from Aws ibn Damaj, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Your superiority by the merit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning the Arabs - we do not marry your women."
"6174","Abu Abd Allah al-Jadali from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari; Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Abd Allah al-Mawsili told us; Ali ibn Mushir told us, from Abu Ismail, meaning Bishr, from Muslim al-Batin, from Abu Abd Allah al-Jadali, from Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about cheese, ghee and furs, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lawful is what Allah made lawful in the Quran, the unlawful is what Allah made unlawful in the Quran, and what He was silent about, He has pardoned."
"6175","Said ibn al-Musayyib from Salman. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal told us; Hammad ibn Salama told us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib and Humayd, from Murawwiq al-Ijli, that Sad ibn Malik and Ibn Masud entered upon Salman visiting him in illness, and he wept. They said: What makes you weep, O Abu Abd Allah? He said: A covenant the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made with us, none of us kept: Let the provision of one of you be like the provision of a rider. Murawwiq said: They looked in his house and there was such-and-such a saddle-bag."
"6176","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib told us; Hakim ibn Hizam told us; Ali ibn Zayd told us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever gives iftar to a fasting one in Ramadan from lawful earning, the angels pray upon him."
"6177","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani; Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali told us; al-Fadl ibn Qurra told us, from al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever gives iftar to a fasting one with food and drink from the lawful, the angels pray upon him in the hours of Ramadan, and Jibril, peace be upon him, prays upon him on the Night of Decree."
"6178","Narrated Bakr ibn Muhammad al-Qazzaz al-Basri; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Ghalib al-Sulami told us; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah Abu Bakr al-Abdi told me, from Ishaq ibn Yunus ibn Sad, from Hilal al-Wazzan, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take a house in the Fire; and whoever rejects a hadith conveyed to him from me, let him take a house in the Fire; and whoever rejects a hadith conveyed to him from me, I am his disputant on the Day of Resurrection; and when a hadith from me reaches you and you do not recognize it, say: Allah knows best."
"6179","Abu Muslim, freedman of Zayd ibn Suhan, from Salman. Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, Abu Khalifa al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab and Muhammad ibn Yaqub ibn Sawra al-Baghdadi; they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi told us; Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat told us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Abu Shurayh, from Abu Muslim, freedman of Zayd ibn Suhan, who said: I was with Salman and he saw a man who had relieved himself and wanted to remove his leather socks for ablution. Salman said: Wipe over them and over your turban. And Salman said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wipe over his head-cover and his leather socks."
"6180","Narrated Abdan; Zayd ibn al-Harish told us; Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr told us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Dawud ibn al-Furat, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Abu Shurayh, from Abu Muslim, from Salman, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wipe over the turban and the leather socks."
"6181","Narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi; Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi told us; Shuayb ibn Ishaq told us; Said ibn Abi Aruba told us, from Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat al-Kindi, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Abu Shurayh, from Abu Muslim, that he saw Salman come out from relieving himself and wipe over his leather socks. It was said: You do this? He said: Yes. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wipe over the leather socks and the turban."
"6182","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Yahya al-Himmani told us; Abd al-Salam ibn Harb told us, from Said ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Abu Shurayh, from Abu Muslim, from Salman, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the leather socks and the head-cover."
"6183","Abu al-Khalil from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi told us; Amr ibn Hurayth told us; Bardhaa ibn Abd al-Rahman told us, from Abu al-Khalil, from Salman who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I named them - meaning al-Hasan and al-Husayn - by the names of the sons of Harun: Shabbar and Shubayr."
"6184","Abu Amr al-Basri from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Muhammad ibn Ammar al-Mawsili told us; Isa ibn Yunus told us, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Ulatha, from al-Hajjaj ibn Furafisa, from Abu Umar, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Souls are grouped troops: those of them that recognize one another unite, and those that do not recognize one another differ."
"6185","And with its isnad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When speech appears and deeds are hoarded, tongues unite and hearts hate one another, and every possessor of kinship severs his kinship, then Allah curses them, deafens them and blinds their eyes."
"6186","Abu Rashid from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Abda ibn Abd Allah al-Saffar told us; Bishr ibn Ubayda al-Darisi told us; Maslama ibn al-Salt told us, from Amr ibn Yazid al-Azdi, from Abu Rashid who said: I asked Salman al-Farisi about the tashahhud, and he said: I teach you as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me the tashahhud letter by letter: Greetings are for Allah, and prayers and good words; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness there is no god but Allah alone without partner, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger."
"6187","Al-Harith ibn Umayra from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Muhammad ibn Ammar al-Mawsili told us; al-Muafa ibn Imran told us; Abd al-Ala ibn Abi al-Musawir told us, from Ikrima, from al-Harith ibn Umayra who said: When I came to al-Madain, I set out and there was a man in worn clothes with a red hide tanning it. He turned, looked at me and gestured with his hand: Stay where you are, O servant of Allah. I stood and said to those with me: Who is this man? They said: This is Salman. He entered his house, put on white clothes, then came, took my hand, shook it and asked me. I said: O Abu Abd Allah, you never saw me before, nor did I see you, nor did you know me. He said: Yes indeed, by Him in whose Hand is my soul, my soul recognized your soul when I saw you. Are you not al-Harith ibn Umayra? I said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Souls are grouped troops: those of them that recognize one another for Allah's sake unite, and those that do not recognize one another for Allah's sake differ."
"6188","A man from Banu Abs from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Abu Nuaym told us, from Abd al-Salam ibn Harb, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from a man of Banu Abs who said: I was traveling with Salman on the bank of the Tigris, and he said: O brother of Banu Abs, dismount and drink. I drank, then he said: Drink. I drank. He said: Your drinking did not diminish the Tigris. I said: How could it diminish? He said: Knowledge is likewise: taken from yet not diminished. Then he said: Mount. We passed heaps of wheat and barley, and he said: Do you see this opened for us, and withheld from the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for our good and their harm? I said: I do not know. He said: But I know: it is harm for us and good for them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not eat his fill three consecutive days until he met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."
"6189","Ulaym al-Kindi from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Barra al-Sanani and al-Hasan ibn Abd al-Ala al-Tursi; both said: Abd al-Razzaq told us; al-Thawri told us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Sadiq, from Ulaym, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The first of this ummah to come to its Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the first of them in Islam: Ali ibn Abi Talib."
"6190","Hamiya ibn Rabab from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Yahya al-Himmani told us; Nusayr ibn Ziyad al-Tai told us, from al-Salt al-Dahhan, from Hamiya ibn Rabab who said: I heard Salman, asked about His saying: That is because among them are priests and monks - he said: The monks are those in the cells. Salman said: It was sent down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That is because among them are priests and monks."
"6191","Muhammad ibn Adi from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; Said ibn Yahya al-Umawi told us; Muhammad ibn Hamza al-Raqqi told us, from al-Khalil ibn Murra, from Abd al-Karim, from Muhammad ibn Adi, from Salman al-Farisi who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever glorifies Allah with a tasbih, or praises Him with a tahmid, or declares His oneness with a tahlil, or magnifies Him with a takbir, Allah plants for him thereby a tree in Paradise, its root a red ruby, crowned with pearls, its fruit like the breasts of virgins, sweeter than honey and softer than butter."
"6192","Shurahbil ibn al-Simt al-Kindi from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl; Abd Allah ibn Salih told us; Abu Shurayh Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh al-Maafiri told me that Abd al-Karim ibn al-Harith told him, from Abu Ubayda ibn Uqba ibn Nafi, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt al-Kindi, from Salman al-Khayr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Guarding a day and night is better than fasting a month and standing it in prayer; whoever dies guarding in the path of Allah, the like thereof in reward continues for him, his provision is continued for him, and he is secured from the tester."
"6193","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi; Abd Allah ibn Salih told us; al-Layth told me, from Ayyub ibn Musa ibn Amr ibn Said ibn al-As, from Makhul al-Dimashqi, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt, from Salman who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Guarding a day and night in the path of Allah is better than fasting a month and standing it in prayer; whoever dies guarding, his deed which he did continues for him, and he is secured from the tester."
"6194","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl; Shuayb ibn Yahya told us, from Nafi ibn Yazid, who said: Muawiya ibn Yazid ibn Shurahbil told me that Abd Allah ibn al-Walid, freedman of al-Mughira, told him that he heard Ibn Abi Zakariyya narrating from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt, that he saw Salman al-Farisi while guarding at the coast of Hims, and said: Why are you upon this? He said: Guarding. Salman said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Guarding a day in the path of Allah is like fasting a month and standing it in prayer; whoever dies guarding, his deed which he did continues for him, he is secured from the tester, and he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection a martyr."
"6195","Narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi; Abu Umayya Amr ibn Hisham told us; Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman told us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt, from Salman who said: I went out seeking religion and met among the monks remnants of the People of the Book - Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: They recognize him as they recognize their sons - and they said: This is the time of a prophet soon to arise from the land of the Arabs; he has signs, among them a round mole between his shoulders, the seal of prophethood. I joined the land of the Arabs; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arose, and I saw all they said, and saw the seal. I bore witness there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. I heard him say: Guarding a day and night is better than fasting a month and standing it in prayer, standing never slackening, fasting never breaking; if he dies guarding, the like of his righteous deed continues for him until he is raised, and he is protected from the torment of the grave."
"6196","Narrated Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani; Ismail ibn Abd Allah ibn Zurara al-Raqqi told us; Ismail ibn Ayyash told us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwa, from Makhul, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt al-Kindi, from Salman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Four of the deeds of the living continue for the dead: a man who leaves righteous offspring who supplicate for him and their supplication follows him; a man who gives ongoing charity after him, its reward his as long as it continues after him; and a man who teaches knowledge and it is acted upon after him, his the like of the reward of whoever acts upon it, without diminishing the reward of whoever acts upon it."
"6197","Amir ibn Abd Allah from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf; Ahmad ibn Salih told us; Ibn Wahb told us; Abu Hani told me, from Amir ibn Abd Allah, from Salman al-Khayr who said: When death came to him, they noticed some distress in him, and said: What distresses you, O Abu Abd Allah, when you had precedence in good, and witnessed great conquests with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: It grieves me that our beloved Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he parted us, made a covenant with us, saying: Let one of you suffice like the provision of a rider. That is what grieves me. Salman's wealth was gathered and its value was fifteen dirhams."
"6198","Amir ibn Atiyya from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani; Said ibn Anbasa al-Razi told us; Sad ibn Muhammad al-Warraq told us; Musa al-Juhani told us, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Amir ibn Atiyya, from Salman who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The longest of people in hunger on the Day of Resurrection are the most satiated in this world. O Salman, this world is the prison of the believer and the paradise of the disbeliever."
"6199","Abu Sukhayla, a Kufan, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ali ibn Ishaq al-Wazir al-Asbahani; Ismail ibn Musa al-Suddi told us; Umar ibn Said told us, from Fudayl ibn Marzuq, from Abu Sukhayla, from Abu Dharr and from Salman, both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the hand of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: This is the first who believed in me, and he is the first who will shake my hand on the Day of Resurrection; this is the greatest truthful one, and this is the distinguisher of this ummah, distinguishing between truth and falsehood; and this is the chief of the believers, while wealth is the chief of the wrongdoer."
"6200","Abu al-Azhar, a Madinan, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar; Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali told us; Makki ibn Ibrahim told us; Musa ibn Ubayda told us, from Abu al-Azhar, from Salman, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out visiting a man of the Ansar. When he entered upon him he placed his hand on his forehead, saying: How do you find yourself? He gave him no reply. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, he is occupied from you. He said: Leave me with him. The women left him, leaving the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hand, and the sick man gestured to return your hand where it was. Then he called: O so-and-so, what do you find? He said: I find good; two have attended me, one black and the other white. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of them is nearer to you? He said: The black. He said: Good is little and evil is much. He said: Let me enjoy from you, O Messenger of Allah, a supplication. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the much and increase the little. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you see? He said: Good, may my father and mother be ransomed for you; good grows and I see evil shrinking, and the black has withdrawn from me. He said: Which of your deeds held you most? He said: I used to give water to drink. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Listen, O Salman, do you deny anything of me? He said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransomed for you; I saw you in places, and never saw you in your state today. He said: I know what he meets; there is no vein of him but it is death in its severity."
"6201","Abu al-Waqqas from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari; Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan told us; Mihran ibn Abi Umar told us; Ali ibn Abd al-Ala told us, from Abu al-Numan; Abu al-Waqqas told me; Salman al-Farisi told me, who said: Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah Almighty be pleased with them both, entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of the traits of the hypocrite: when he speaks he lies, when he promises he breaks, and when entrusted he betrays. They left the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heavy-hearted. I met them and said: Why do I see you heavy-hearted? They said: A hadith we heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Of the traits of the hypocrite: when he speaks he lies, when he promises he breaks, and when entrusted he betrays. He said: Did you not ask him? They said: We were awed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: But I shall ask him. I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Abu Bakr and Umar met me heavy-hearted, then I mentioned what they said. He said: I told them, but they did not place it where it belongs; rather the hypocrite, when he speaks while telling himself he lies, and when he promises while telling himself he will break, and when entrusted while telling himself he will betray."
"6202","Abd al-Rahman ibn Masud from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ubayd al-Ijli; Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari told us; Husayn ibn Muhammad told us; al-Husayn ibn al-Rammas told us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Masud, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us burdening ourselves for a guest with what we do not have."
"6203","Al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti; my father told us; Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz told us, from Abu Abd Allah al-Najrani, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman who said: Salman al-Farisi said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When one of you visits his brother and throws for him something shielding him from dust, Allah shields him from the torment of the Fire."
"6204","Abd Allah ibn Wadia from Salman. Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati; Abu Thabit Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah told us; Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim told us, from al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abd Allah ibn Wadia; Salman al-Khayr told me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bathes on Friday and perfects his washing, then touches perfume and wears of his clothes, then does not separate two, prays what is written for him, and when the imam comes out listens quietly then hears, he is forgiven between it and the next Friday."
"6205","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba told us; Shababa ibn Sawwar told us; Ibn Abi Dhib told us, from Said al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Wadia, from Salman al-Khayr, that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No man bathes on Friday, purifies himself as much as he can, oils of his oil or touches perfume of his house, then goes and does not separate two, then prays what is written for him, then listens quietly when the imam speaks, except he is forgiven between it and the next Friday."
"6206","Ata ibn Yasar from Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Ubayd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Salman al-Farisi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None enters Paradise except with a pass: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; this is a book from Allah for so-and-so son of so-and-so: admit him to a lofty garden whose clusters are near."
"6207","Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi, may Allah be pleased with him; he used to reside at Basra and there died. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you breaks fast, let him break with dates; if he finds none, let him break with water, for water is pure."
"6208","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Asim, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6209","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa; al-Humaydi told us; Sufyan told us; Asim ibn Sulayman told us, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from her uncle Salman ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you breaks fast, let him break upon dates; if he finds none, let him drink water, for it is pure."
"6210","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi told us; Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar told us, from Asim al-Ahwal; Hafsa bint Sirin told me, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you breaks fast, let him break upon dates if he finds; if he finds none, upon water, for water is pure."
"6211","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi; Sulayman ibn Harb told us; Hammad ibn Zayd told us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you breaks fast, let him break upon dates; if he finds none, upon water, for it is pure."
"6212","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Muslim ibn Ibrahim told us; Shuba told us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever finds dates, let him break upon them; whoever finds no dates, let him break upon water, for water is pure. Shuba did not mention al-Rabab."
"6213","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa; al-Humaydi told us; Sufyan told us, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With the boy is his aqiqa; so shed blood on his behalf and remove harm from him."
"6214","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With the boy is his aqiqa; so shed blood on his behalf and remove harm from him."
"6215","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6216","Narrated Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri; Musa ibn Ismail told us; Hammad ibn Salama told us, from Yunus and Habib ibn al-Shahid, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the boy is his aqiqa; so shed blood on his behalf and remove harm from him."
"6217","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Ubayd Allah ibn Aisha told us; Hammad ibn Salama told us; Qatada, Habib, Yunus and Ayyub told us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The boy is pledged by his aqiqa; so shed blood on his behalf and remove harm from him."
"6218","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari; Abbad ibn al-Walid al-Anbari told us; Hibban ibn Hilal told us; Hammad ibn Salama told us; Ayyub, Qatada, Yunus ibn Ubayd, Hisham ibn Hassan and Yahya ibn Atiq told us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the boy is his aqiqa; so shed blood on his behalf and remove harm from him."
"6219","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi told us; Sallam ibn Abi al-Muti told us, from Qatada, from Muhammad, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With the boy is his aqiqa; so shed blood on his behalf and remove harm from him."
"6220","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari; Hibban ibn Hilal told us; al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad told us; Abu Hammam al-Khariki told us; Abd al-Wahid ibn Wasil al-Haddad told us; Abu Naama al-Adawi told us; my maternal aunt Sumayta told me, who said: I heard my grandfather Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every newborn is pledged by his aqiqa; so shed blood on his behalf and remove harm from him."
"6221","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal told us; Hammad ibn Salama told us, from Ayyub, Hisham and Habib, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity to kin is charity and kinship."
"6222","Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salm al-Razi; Nuh ibn Anas al-Muqri told us; al-Sabbah ibn Muharib told us; Ashath ibn Abd al-Malik told us, from Ibn Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The charity of a poor one is charity, and to a relative two: charity and tender kinship."
"6223","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna; Musaddad told us; Yahya told us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Your charity to a Muslim is charity, and to a relative two: charity and kinship."
"6224","Narrated Musa ibn Harun; Humayd ibn Masada al-Shami told us; Zuhayr ibn Hunayda Abu al-Dhayyal al-Adawi told us; Abu Naama al-Adawi told us, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Umm al-Raih bint Sulay, from Salman ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your charity to a poor one is charity, and your charity to a relative is two charities: charity and kinship."
"6225","Narrated Khalaf ibn Ubayd Allah al-Dabbi; Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali told us; Ghalib ibn Qurran al-Anbari told us; Amr ibn Isa Abu Naama told us, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Umm al-Raih bint Sulay, from Salman ibn Amir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6226","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari; Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan told us; al-Ala ibn Abd al-Jabbar told us; Suwayd Abu Hatim told us, from Qatada, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Umm al-Raih bint Sulay, from Salman ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity to a relative - or he said: possessor of kinship - is two charities, and to a poor one is charity."
"6227","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa; al-Humaydi told us; Sufyan told us, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Hafsa, from Umm al-Raih, from Salman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity to a poor one is charity, and to a possessor of kinship two: charity and kinship."
"6228","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna; Musaddad told us; Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal told us; Ibn Awn told us, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Umm al-Raih bint Sulay, from Salman ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity to a poor one is charity, and to a possessor of kinship two: charity and kinship."
"6229","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba told us; Waki told us, from Ibn Awn, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Umm al-Raih bint Sulay, from Salman ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity to a poor one is charity, and to a possessor of kinship two: charity and kinship."
"6230","Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba; al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani told us. And narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani; al-Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri told us; Abu Asim told us; Naama al-Adawi told us; Bishr ibn Abd al-Aziz told us, from Salman ibn Amir al-Dabbi who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my father maintained kinship, honored the guest and fulfilled covenants. He said: And he did not reach Islam? He said: No. When I turned, he said: Bring the shaykh. He said: That will be in your offspring; they will never be humiliated, nor ever poor."
"6231","Salman ibn Khalid al-Khuzai. Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna and Abu Khalifa al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab; both said: Musaddad told us; Isa ibn Yunus told us, from Misar ibn Kidam, from Amr ibn Murra, from Salman ibn Khalid - I think from Khuzara - who said: I prayed and rested, as if they disapproved of that, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Bilal, establish the prayer; give us rest."
"6232","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz; Abu Nuaym told us; Abu Hamza al-Thumali Thabit ibn Abi Safiyya told us; Salim ibn Abi al-Jad told me; Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya told me, who said: I went with my father to an in-law of ours from Aslam, of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I heard him say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Give us rest with it, O Bilal - prayer. He said: I said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He became angry and turned to the people, telling them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man to a tribe of the Arabs; when he came to them he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to judge among your women as I wish. They said: Hearing and obedience to the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command. They sent a man to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: So-and-so came to us saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to judge among your women as I wish; if it is your command, hearing and obedience, and if otherwise we wished to inform you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry and sent a man of the Ansar, saying: Go to so-and-so, kill him and burn him with fire. He reached him and he had died and been buried; he ordered him dug up, then burned him with fire. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire. Then he turned to me, saying: Do you think I lied upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after this?"
"6233","Those named Salama. Salama ibn Amr ibn al-Akwa al-Aslami; of his reports. Narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj; Yahya ibn Bukayr told us, who said: Salama ibn al-Akwa - kunya Abu al-Abbas - and Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, both died in the year 74; and it is said: Salama ibn al-Akwa died at eighty years."
"6234","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari; Ishaq ibn Musa al-Ansari told us; Asim ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ashjai told us; Uthman ibn Abd Allah ibn Rafi told us, that he saw Abd Allah ibn Umar, Abu Said al-Khudri, Jabir ibn Abd Allah, Abu Usayd al-Saidi, Anas ibn Malik, Rafi ibn Khadij and Salama ibn al-Akwa: trimming the mustaches closely, plucking the armpits and cutting the nails."
"6235","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Usfuri; Nasr ibn Ali told us; Hammad ibn Masada told us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salama ibn al-Akwa: That he would pray in a single garment."
"6236","Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Hammad ibn Mas'adah narrated to us, from Yazid, from Salamah: that water used to be heated for him and he would perform ablution with it."
"6237","Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Humayd ibn Mas'adah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd narrated to us, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' when he performed ablution would take musk, melt it in his hand, then wipe his beard with it."
"6238","Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Hammad ibn Mas'adah informed us, from Yazid, who said: Salamah used to pray the forenoon (Duha) prayer."
"6239","What Salamah ibn al-Akwa' transmitted — Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman from Salamah ibn al-Akwa': Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, (haw) and Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Anbar al-Misri narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith ibn Khalid al-Taymi narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: I used to shoot wild game and hunt it, and I would gift its meat to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed me and said: Salamah, where do you stay? I said: We go far for hunting, O Messenger of Allah, for I only hunt along the upper part of a water channel near houses. He said: Had you hunted at al-Aqiq, I would have gone out ahead of you when you left and met you when you returned, for I love al-Aqiq."
"6240","Mus'ab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, (haw) and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Madani narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a servant boy called Yasar; he saw him praying well, so he freed him and sent him with some milch camels of his at al-Harrah where he stayed with them. Then some people from Uraynah of Yemen professed Islam and came while they were sick with fever, their bellies swollen, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent them to Yasar, and they drank from the camels' milk until their bellies subsided. Then they attacked Yasar and slaughtered him, placed thorns in his eyes, then drove off the camels. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent horses of the Muslims after them, their commander being Kurz ibn Jabir al-Fihri, who caught them and brought them, and he cut off their hands and feet and gouged out their eyes."
"6241","Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: Talhah ibn Ubaydillah (may Allah be pleased with him) bought a well in the direction of the mountain and slaughtered a camel, feeding the people, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You, O Talhah, are al-Fayyad (the most generous)."
"6242","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from Salamah ibn al-Akwa': Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' said: On the day of Khaybar my brother fought fiercely, then his sword rebounded upon him and killed him. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke about that and doubted concerning him: a man who died by his own weapon, and they doubted part of his affair. Salamah said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Khaybar, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to recite rajaz poetry in your praise, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him. Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Know what you say. So I said: [rajaz verses not preserved in the printed text]. When I finished my rajaz, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Who said this? I said: My brother said it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, some people hesitate to pray over him, saying: a man who died by his own weapon. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He died striving, struggling in jihad."
"6243","Ibn Shihab said: Then I asked Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', and he narrated to me from his father the like of what Abd al-Rahman had narrated to me, except that Ibn Salamah added with that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, when I said they hesitate to pray over him: They lied; he died striving, struggling in jihad, and he has his reward twice. And he gestured with his finger."
"6244","Isma'il ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffan al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik narrated to me, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' said: On the day of Khaybar my brother fought fiercely alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then his sword rebounded upon him and killed him. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"6245","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik al-Ansari, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' said: On the day of Khaybar my brother fought fiercely alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then his sword rebounded upon him and killed him. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about that: This is a man who died by his own weapon, and they doubted part of his affair. Salamah said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Khaybar I said to him: Permit me, O Messenger of Allah, to recite rajaz in your praise? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me. Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Know what you say. So I said: [rajaz verses not preserved in the printed text]. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have spoken the truth. When I finished my rajaz the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Who said this? He said: I said: My brother said it, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, some people hesitate to pray over him, saying: a man who died by his own weapon? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He died striving, struggling in jihad."
"6246","Ibn Shihab said: Then I asked Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' about that, and he narrated to me from his father the like of what Abd al-Rahman had narrated to me from him, except that Ibn Salamah added with that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said — when I said they hesitate to pray over him, and they doubted his case —: They lied; he died striving, struggling in jihad, and he has two rewards."
"6247","Umarah ibn Wathimah narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab informed me, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik al-Ansari informed him, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to him, that his brother, on the day of Khaybar, fought fiercely alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then his sword rebounded upon him and killed him. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about that: He died by his own weapon, and they doubted part of his affair. Salamah said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Khaybar, I said to him: Permit me, O Messenger of Allah, to recite rajaz in your praise? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me. Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Know what you say. So I said: [rajaz verses not preserved in the printed text]. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have spoken the truth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is the speaker of these verses? I said: My brother. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, some people hesitate to pray over him, saying: a man who died by his own weapon. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He died striving, struggling in jihad. Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri said: Then I asked Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' about that, and he narrated to me from his father the like of what Abd al-Rahman had narrated to me, except that Ibn Salamah added with that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He died striving, struggling in jihad, and he has two rewards, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured with his finger, moving them."
"6248","Al-Mufaddal ibn Muhammad al-Janadi narrated to us, Abu Hummah Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Zubaydi narrated to us, Abu Qurrah Musa ibn Tariq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj mentioned, from al-Zuhri, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik al-Ansari narrated to me, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' said: On the day of Khaybar my brother fought fiercely alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then his sword rebounded upon him and killed him. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke about that and doubted concerning him: a man who died by his own weapon. Salamah said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to recite rajaz. Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What are you saying? So I said: [rajaz verses not preserved in the printed text]. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have spoken the truth. When I finished my rajaz the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Who said this? I said: My brother said it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on him. I said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, some people hesitate to pray over him, saying: a man killed by his own weapon? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He died striving, struggling in jihad. Al-Zuhri said: Then I asked Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', and he narrated to me from his father the like of what Abd al-Rahman had narrated to me from him, except that Ibn Salamah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, when I said they hesitate to pray over him: They lied; he died striving, struggling in jihad, he has his reward twice. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured with his two fingers."
"6249","Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' said: On the day of Khaybar my brother fought fiercely alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then his sword rebounded upon him and killed him. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke about that and doubted his affair: a man who died by his own weapon, and they doubted part of his affair. Salamah said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Khaybar, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to recite rajaz in your praise? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him. Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Know what you say. So I said: [rajaz verses not preserved in the printed text]. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have spoken the truth. When I finished my rajaz the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who said this? I said: My brother said it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on him. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, some people hesitate to pray over him, saying: a man who died by his own weapon. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He died striving, struggling in jihad. Ibn Shihab said: Then I asked Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', and he narrated to me from his father the like of what Abd al-Rahman had narrated to me, except that Ibn Salamah added with that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, when I said they hesitate to pray over him: They lied; he died striving, struggling in jihad, and he has his reward twice. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured with his two fingers."
"6250","Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah from Salamah ibn al-Akwa': Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, from Jabir ibn Abdullah and Salamah ibn al-Akwa', a man of Aslam among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that they both said: We were in a raid, then a messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: Enjoy yourselves [with temporary marriage]."
"6251","Sa'id al-Maqburi from Salamah ibn al-Akwa': Al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Ma'mari narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Khallad al-Bahili narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, that Ibn Abbas and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr differed about mut'ah (temporary marriage). Urwah said: It is fornication. Ibn Abbas said: How do you know, O Urayyah? Then Salamah ibn al-Akwa' passed by them, so Ibn Abbas asked him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us westward for three months; I would go out with the army, staying when they stayed and halting in the evening when they halted. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes may enjoy himself with these women."
"6252","Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' from his father — Ikrimah ibn Ammar from Iyas ibn Salamah from his father: Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, (haw) and Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, both saying: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to us, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Khaybar: I shall certainly give the banner today to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love. He said: So he sent me to Ali, who had ophthalmia, and I brought him leading him by hand; he spat in his eye and it healed, and he gave him the banner."
"6253","Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi and Amr ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, both saying: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, (haw) and Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Surah al-Baghdadi and Abu Khalifah narrated to us, both saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, both saying: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: A man sneezed before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: May Allah have mercy on you. Then he sneezed again, and he said: May Allah have mercy on you. Then he sneezed again, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The man has a cold. The wording is that of the hadith of Asim ibn Ali."
"6254","Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Surah al-Baghdadi and Abu Khalifah narrated to us, both saying: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw Busr ibn Ra'i al-Ir eating with his left hand, so he said: Eat with your right hand. He said: I cannot. He said: May you never be able. So his right hand never reached his mouth thereafter."
"6255","Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Abbas al-Razi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, that a man was eating before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his left hand, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Eat with your right hand. He said: I cannot. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May you never be able. He said: So he never raised it to his mouth thereafter."
"6256","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, (haw) and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, both saying: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to me, from his father, who said: We went out with Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had appointed over us, and we raided Fazarah. When we drew near the water, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) commanded us and we halted for rest in the latter part of the night. When we had prayed the dawn prayer, Abu Bakr commanded us and we launched the raid, killing at the water those we killed. Salamah said: I saw a group of people among whom were offspring and women, and I was running after them; I feared they would reach the mountain before me, so I shot an arrow which fell between them and the mountain, and they halted. I brought them driving them to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) until I reached the water. Among them was a woman of Fazarah wearing a garment of leather, with her daughter who was among the most beautiful of the Arabs. Abu Bakr granted me her daughter as extra spoils. I did not uncover her garment until we arrived at Madinah; I had gone out and had not uncovered her garment. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me and said: O Salamah, give me the woman as a gift. I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah she has pleased me, and I have not uncovered her garment. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent and left me. Then he met me the next day in the market and said: O Salamah, give me the woman as a gift, may your father be sacrificed. I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I have not uncovered her garment, and she is yours. The next day he met me in the market and said: O Salamah, what has happened with the woman, may your father be sacrificed. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent her to the people of Makkah, in whose hands were captives of the Muslims, and he ransomed them with that woman, freeing them in exchange for her. The wording is that of the hadith of Abu al-Walid."
"6257","Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Asim ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: I captured a slave girl of Banu Fazarah; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me and said: May your father be sacrificed, give her to me as a gift. So I gave her to him, and he ransomed with her some Muslim captives."
"6258","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to us, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) over us, and we raided some polytheists and killed them. Our battle cry was: Die, die. Salamah said: I killed with my own hand on that night nine heads of households."
"6259","Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Kaysan al-Missisi narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, who said: We came from al-Hudaybiyyah with the Messenger of Allah, and we pledged allegiance to him at the foot of a tree. He said: I pledged allegiance among the first people. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Salamah, pledge allegiance to me. I said: O Prophet of Allah, by Allah I have already pledged to you among the first people. He said: And also. He said: So I pledged to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me while I had no shield with me. And he mentioned the hadith."
"6260","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, (haw) and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, saying: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to me, from his father, who said: We raided Hawazin with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). While we were sitting taking our forenoon meal, a man came on a red camel; he pulled a strap from the camel's saddle girth and hobbled his camel with it, then came walking until he sat with us eating lunch. He looked at the faces of the people and saw weakness in their appearance, most of them on foot. When he had looked at the people's faces he went out running until he reached his camel, mounted it and rode off — he was a scout for the disbelievers. A man of ours from Aslam followed him on his grey she-camel; I followed him running on my feet and caught up with him, coming to the haunch of the she-camel, then I advanced until I seized the camel's halter, drew my sword and struck his head. Then I brought his she-camel leading it, with his spoils upon it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me with the people and said: Who killed the man? They said: Ibn al-Akwa'. He said: All his spoils are yours. The wording is that of the hadith of Abu al-Walid."
"6261","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to me, from his father, who said: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Hudaybiyyah, then we left returning to Madinah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of our horsemen today is Abu Qatadah, and the best of our foot soldiers today is Salamah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me two shares: the share of a horseman and the share of a foot soldier together."
"6262","And with the same chain, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever bears arms against us is not of us."
"6263","And with his chain he said: We went out to Khaybar, and Amir was reciting rajaz, saying: [verses not preserved in the printed text]. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is this? They said: Amir. He said: May Allah forgive your sin, O Amir. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never sought forgiveness for a man accompanying him except that he was martyred. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, would that you had let us enjoy Amir longer. When we arrived at Khaybar, Marhab came out brandishing his sword — he was their king — saying: [challenge verses not preserved]. Amir came out to him, saying: Mughamir. They exchanged two blows; Marhab's sword fell upon Amir's shield, and Amir went to strike back with it, but his sword returned upon himself, cutting the jugular vein in which his life lay. Some of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Amir's deeds are void; he killed himself. So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) weeping and said: O Messenger of Allah, are Amir's deeds void? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who said this? I said: Some of your companions. He said: Whoever said that has lied; rather he has his reward twice. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Ali ibn Abi Talib; I came to him while he had ophthalmia. He said: I shall certainly give the banner today to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love. So I brought him leading him while he had ophthalmia, until I brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he spat in his eye and it healed, then he gave him the banner. Then Marhab came out, saying: [verses not preserved]. Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: [reply verses indicated by al-Sandarh in print]. He struck him, splitting Marhab's head and killing him, and the conquest came at the hands of Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"6264","Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Kaysan al-Missisi narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: We raided Hawazin with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we were struck by severe hardship, so that we considered slaughtering some of our mounts. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gather some of your provisions. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a leather mat to be spread, and the people brought dates and scattered them. I stretched to estimate how much it was, and it was like the resting place of a sheep. We all ate until we were full, though we were fourteen hundred, and we filled our bags from it. Then the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a little water in a leather water-skin, ordered it poured into a cup, and we performed ablution with it until we all had done so."
"6265","And with his chain he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a red tent when a man came on a barren horse followed by its foal, and said: Who are you? He said: I am the Messenger of Allah. He said: When is the Hour? He said: It is unseen, and none knows the unseen but Allah. He said: When will we receive rain? He said: It is unseen, and none knows the unseen but Allah. He said: What is in my horse's belly? He said: It is unseen, and none knows the unseen but Allah. He said: Give me your sword? He said: Here it is. He took it, drew it and brandished it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will never be able to do what you intended. Then he said: This man came forward saying: I shall come to him, ask him, then take my sword and kill him. So he sheathed the sword."
"6266","Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Anbar al-Basri narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: My uncle came with a man of Ajlan leading him and his horse, among seventy of the polytheists, until he halted with them before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave them. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pardoned them, and Allah the Mighty and Majestic revealed: {And He is the One who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah after He had given you victory over them} to the end of the verse."
"6267","Abdan ibn Ahmad and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim al-Anbari narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with al-Hasan and al-Husayn upon his grey mule, until he brought them into the chamber of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one in front of him and one behind him."
"6268","Abdan narrated to us, al-Abbas narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: We visited with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a man suffering fever. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have never seen anyone hotter than him? He said: Shall I not inform you of those hotter on the Day of Resurrection: these two riding men coming now — referring to two men then among his companions."
"6269","Suwayd ibn al-Khattab from Iyas ibn Salamah: Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Suwayd ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, he said: I heard my father saying: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever draws the sword against us is not of us."
"6270","Ayyub ibn Utbah from Iyas: Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Ayyub ibn Utbah al-Yamami narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When dinner is served and the prayer comes, begin with dinner."
"6271","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, both saying: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, (haw) and Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Surah al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, both saying: Ayyub ibn Utbah al-Yamami narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bears arms against us is not of us."
"6272","Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, Ayyub ibn Utbah narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of our horsemen is Abu Qatadah, and the best of our foot soldiers is Salamah."
"6273","Umar ibn Rashid al-Yamami from Iyas ibn Salamah: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Umar ibn Rashid al-Yamami narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, from his father, who said: I rarely heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) begin with a supplication except that I heard him begin with: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High, the Bestower."
"6274","Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Umar ibn Rashid, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man keeps behaving arrogantly and exalting himself until he is recorded among the tyrants, and there befalls him what befell them."
"6275","Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Khalid ibn Hayyan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, Abu Sa'id, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, Umar ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Aslam — may Allah grant them safety; and Ghifar — may Allah forgive them. It is not I who said it, but Allah said it."
"6276","Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim Abu Yahya Sa'iqah narrated to us, Abd al-Samad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, Umar ibn Rashid al-Yamami narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: I pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among those who pledged beneath the tree."
"6277","Ya'la ibn al-Harith al-Muharibi from Iyas ibn Salamah: Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ghassan al-Nahdi and Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, (haw) and Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Surah al-Baghdadi and Abu Khalifah narrated to us, both saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, all saying: Ya'la ibn al-Harith al-Muharibi narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, from his father, who said: We used to pray the Friday prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the walls had no shade in which to take shelter."
"6278","Abu Maryam Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Qasim from Iyas ibn Salamah: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Shabib al-Assal al-Asbahani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Amr al-Bajali narrated to us, Abu Maryam Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, from his father, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the funeral of a man of the Ansar was brought. He said: Has he left a debt upon him? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, two dinars. He said: Pray over your companion. Abu Qatadah said: They are upon me, O Messenger of Allah. So he prayed over him. Then another was brought, and he said: Is there a debt upon him? They said: No. He said: Has he left a treasure? They said: Two dinars. He said: Two brands of Fire. And he prayed over him."
"6279","Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Assal narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Amr al-Bajali narrated to us, Abu Maryam narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, from his father, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a funeral was brought. The people said: If only, and if only. Then another was brought, and the people said: If only, and if only. They praised one with good and the other with evil. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are Allah's witnesses on earth, and the angels are Allah's witnesses in heaven."
"6280","Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi from Iyas ibn Salamah: Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi narrated to us, Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The stars were made a security for the inhabitants of heaven, and my household are a security for my ummah."
"6281","Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: {Allah will see your deeds, and His Messenger, and the believers}."
"6282","Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at a funeral, and the people praised him with good praise. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It has become due. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what has become due? He said: The angels are Allah's witnesses in heaven, and you are Allah's witnesses on earth; when you testify, it becomes due."
"6283","Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sallam ibn Abi al-Hayfa' al-Asadi narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledged allegiance on behalf of Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) with one hand upon the other, saying: O Allah, Uthman is engaged in Your need and the need of Your Messenger."
"6284","Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ali ibn Mujahid Abu Mujahid al-Kabuli narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dedicated among the sacrificial camels in the year of al-Hudaybiyyah a camel that had been under Abu Jahl on the day of Badr, with a silver nose-ring on its head. Abdullah said: My father narrated it to us from him long ago, then abandoned him afterwards."
"6285","Muhammad ibn Iyas ibn Salamah from his father: Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub and Sulayman ibn Bilal — or one of them — informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Muhammad ibn Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', that his father narrated to him that Salamah ibn al-Akwa' came to Madinah, and Buraydah ibn al-Husayb met him and said: Have you apostatized from your migration, O Salamah? He said: Allah forbid; I am here with the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Go out to the desert, O Aslam, smell the winds and dwell in the ravines. They said: We fear that will alter our migration? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are migrants wherever you are."
"6286","Ibn Abi Dhi'b from Iyas ibn Salamah: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj al-Askari and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Madini Fustuqah narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man and any unmarried woman who mutually agree, their companionship is for three nights; if they wish to extend further they may extend, and if they wish to part they may part. The wording is that of the hadith of Muhammad ibn Ali."
"6287","Ali ibn Yazid ibn Hukaymah al-Aslami from Iyas ibn Salamah: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Ali ibn Yazid ibn Hukaymah al-Aslami narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated me behind him repeatedly, wiped my head, and sought forgiveness for me and for my offspring equal to the number of fingers on my hand."
"6288","Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Ali ibn Yazid ibn Hukaymah al-Aslami narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to us, from his father, who said: The milch camels of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were raided, so I kept shooting arrows at them until the horsemen came to me from the direction of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6289","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Ali ibn Yazid ibn Hukaymah al-Aslami of Madinah narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Amir ibn al-Akwa': Dismount, O Amir, and let us hear some of your ditties. So he dismounted reciting rajaz: [verses not preserved in the printed text]. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: May your Lord have mercy on you. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: O Prophet of Allah, may my father be sacrificed for you, would that you had let us enjoy Ibn al-Akwa' longer. The next morning we attacked Khaybar. Part of Amir's story was that his sword turned back upon him and killed him. The people said: Amir killed himself. Salamah went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Part of Amir's fate was that his sword turned upon him and killed him, yet people claim he killed himself? He said: They lied; by Him in whose hand is my soul, I see him as if swimming in Paradise like the swimming of a water-duckling."
"6290","Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi from Iyas: Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us, from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, that he heard Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrating from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be upright, though you will never encompass; know that the best of your deeds is prayer, and none preserves prayer except a believer."
"6291","Abu Umays from Iyas ibn Salamah: Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to me, (haw). And al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, both saying: Sharik narrated to us, Utbah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, who said: The battle cry of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: Die, die. Ali ibn Hakim added: In some of his raids."
"6292","Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Abu al-Umays narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, who said: A spy of the polytheists came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was on a journey; he sat conversing with his companions, then slipped away. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek him and kill him. I outran them to him, killed him, took his spoils, and he granted them to me."
"6293","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Utbah ibn Abdullah, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came and entered the mosque. He said: Catch him, for he is a spy. I caught him — I was swift — and killed him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted me his spoils."
"6294","Muhammad ibn Bashir al-Aslami and al-Rabi' ibn Abi Salih from Iyas: Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Nu'man al-Farra' narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ya'la al-Aslami narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Bashir al-Aslami and al-Rabi' ibn Abi Salih, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, who said: Amir ibn al-Akwa' duelled a man, struck him and killed him, but the sword struck Amir's leg. He began saying: I have killed myself, and died. That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: He has two rewards."
"6295","Umar ibn Musa al-Ansari from Iyas: Ya'qub ibn Ghaylan al-Umani and Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, both saying: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Sayfi ibn Rib'i narrated to us, from Umar ibn Musa al-Ansari, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not leave camels untied and unmilked; tie their udders firmly, for the devils suckle from them."
"6296","Amr ibn Yahya ibn Sa'd ibn Zurarah from Ibn Salamah from his father: Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Mu'adh ibn Muhammad al-Ansari, that Amr ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn Zurarah informed him, from Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Excellent camels are thirty, from which one is given out in its zakat, one is mounted in the path of Allah, and one is lent for milking; they are better than forty, fifty, sixty, seventy, eighty, ninety and a hundred. And woe to the owner of a hundred from the hundred."
"6297","Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi from Salamah ibn al-Akwa': Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, my father narrated to me, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about praying in the bow and the quiver? He said: Pray in the bow, and set aside the quiver — meaning the arrow case."
"6298","Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Anbar al-Basri narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to us, from his father, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: Uyaynah ibn Hisn attacked the milch camels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and drove them off. Salamah ibn al-Akwa' al-Aslami said: I went out with my bow and arrows — I used to shoot game — until, when I was at Thaniyyat al-Wada', I saw them driving them off. I ran along the mountain at Sal', then shouted: O morning raid! My shout reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was cried among the people: Alarm, alarm! I went out shooting at them, saying: Take them, I am Ibn al-Akwa'. Before long I saw the horsemen of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) threading through the trees; eight horsemen caught up with them, the first to reach them being Abu Qatadah ibn Rib'i, who speared a man of Banu Fazarah called Mas'adah, stripped his cloak and covered him with it, then went on after the enemy with the horsemen. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by while the people were alarmed, saying: Abu Qatadah is slain. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not Abu Qatadah, but rather the one slain by Abu Qatadah; leave him and his spoils. Then he said: Press on after the people. So they pressed on, recovering what they recovered of the milch camels, while they went off with the rest. Muhammad ibn Talhah said: And in the hadith: He named those who went out seeking the milch camels: Ukkashah ibn Mihsan, al-Miqdad ibn Amr — he who is called Ibn al-Aswad, ally of Banu Zuhrah, and Muhriz ibn Nadlah al-Asadi, ally of Banu Abd Shams. It was said none of the enemy was killed except him. And of the Ansar: Sa'd ibn Zayd al-Ashhali, who was the commander of the people, Abbad ibn Bishr al-Ashhali, Zuhayr ibn Rafi' al-Harithi, Abu Qatadah ibn Rib'i al-Sulami, and Mu'adh ibn Ma'is al-Zuraqi. Abu Ayyash al-Zuraqi was one of the five men. He said: I came forward on a horse of mine, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Ayyash, would that you gave this horse to one who is a better horseman than you. He said: I said: I am the best horseman of the Arabs. The horse had not run fifty cubits before it threw me and broke my leg. I said: Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth. Then I mounted upon my horse my cousin Mu'adh ibn Ma'is al-Zuraqi."
"6299","What Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Rabi'ah narrated from Salamah ibn al-Akwa': Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, (haw) and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, both saying: Attaf ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi narrated to us, Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me that he heard Ibn al-Akwa' saying: I said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I am out hunting, and I pray while I have only a single shirt on? He said: Button it up, even if you find nothing but a thorn."
"6300","Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, freedman of Salamah, from Salamah: Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever attributes to me what I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"6301","Abu Muslim narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Yazid, from Salamah, who said: I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of al-Hudaybiyyah, then I stepped aside. He said: O Salamah, will you not pledge allegiance? I said: I have pledged. He said: Come forward and pledge. So I drew near and pledged to him. I said: Upon what did you pledge, O Abu Muslim? He said: Unto death."
"6302","Abu Muslim narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Yazid, from Salamah, who said: I raided with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seven raids, and with Zayd ibn Harithah seven raids, whom he used to appoint over us."
"6303","Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Usfuri narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Hammad ibn Mas'adah narrated to us, from Yazid, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: I raided with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seven raids: Uhud, Hunayn, Khaybar, al-Hudaybiyyah, and the day of Dhu Qarad. He said: And I forgot the rest."
"6304","Abu Muslim narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Yazid, from Salamah, who said: I went out intending al-Ghabah, when I heard a boy of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf saying: The milch camels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have been seized. I said: Who seized them? He said: Ghatafan and Fazarah. So I ascended the mountain pass and cried: O morning raid! O morning raid! Then I set off running after them until I recovered them from them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with some of his companions, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, the people are thirsty; we hastened before they could water. He said: O Ibn al-Akwa', you have gained mastery, so act with mildness; the people — Ghatafan — are being entertained as guests."
"6305","Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih and Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn al-Harith al-Misri al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, Yahya ibn Rashid narrated to me, Yazid, freedman of Salamah ibn al-Akwa', narrated to me, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution once, wipe his head once, and give salutation once."
"6306","Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn al-Mundhir al-Qazzaz al-Basri narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated me behind him on his she-camel until we entered Madinah."
"6307","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the night preceding the morning of Khaybar: I shall certainly give the banner tomorrow to a man whom Allah and His Messenger love; Allah will grant victory through him. Then there was Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him the banner, and Allah granted victory through him."
"6308","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a crier on the day of Ashura announcing: Whoever has not fasted, let him complete the rest of his day; and whoever has fasted, let him complete his fast."
"6309","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Maghrib when the sun had set and disappeared behind the veil."
"6310","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah, who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a funeral was brought. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, pray over this? He said: Has he left anything? It was said: No. He said: Is there a debt upon him? They said: No. So he prayed over him. While I was standing, another funeral was brought. He said: Is there a debt upon him? They said: No. He said: Has he left anything? They said: Yes, three dinars. He said: Three brands of Fire. Then we remained as long as Allah willed, then a funeral was brought. He said: Has he left anything? They said: No. He said: Is there a debt upon him? They said: Yes. He said: Pray over your companion. Abu Qatadah said: His debt is upon me, O Messenger of Allah. So he prayed over him."
"6311","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a funeral was brought. They said: Pray over it, O Messenger of Allah. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"6312","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a group of Aslam shooting arrows. He said: Shoot, O sons of Isma'il, for your father was an archer. Shoot while I am with the sons of so-and-so. One of the two parties withheld their hands. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is wrong with you that you do not shoot? They said: Shall we shoot, O Messenger of Allah, while you are with them? He said: Shoot, and I am with all of you."
"6313","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to some people of Aslam competing in archery. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"6314","Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khaybar, travelling by night. A man of the people said to Amir: O Amir, will you not let us hear some of your ditties — Amir being a poet. So he dismounted chanting for the caravan, saying: [verses not preserved in the printed text]. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is this driver? They said: Amir ibn al-Akwa'. He said: May Allah have mercy on him. A man of the people said: It has become due, O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); would that you had let us enjoy him longer. He said: Then we came to Khaybar and besieged them until severe hunger struck us. Then Allah granted it as victory to us. In the evening of the day it was conquered, the people saw many cooking pots. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What are these fires? For what are you kindling them? They said: For the flesh of domestic donkeys. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pour them out and break them. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we pour them out? He said: Or that. When the people had dispersed, Amir's sword was short; he reached for a Jew's leg and struck it, and the point of his sword recoiled and struck Amir's knee, and he died from it. I grieved, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), seeing my state, said: What is wrong with you? I said to him: May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, they claim Amir's deeds are void. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They lied; who said it? He has the reward twice; he is a striver, a struggler in jihad."
"6315","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd narrated to us, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khaybar. A man of the people said: O Amir, would that you let us hear some of your ditties. So he dismounted chanting. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"6316","Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: When the wind blew fiercely, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, a fertilizing wind, not a barren one."
"6317","Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu Musa al-Ansari narrated to us, Asim ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ashja'i narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd narrated to us, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one swears a false oath upon the pulpit except that he takes his seat in the Fire."
"6318","Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hatim narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd narrated to us, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', that he entered upon al-Hajjaj, who said: O Ibn al-Akwa', have you apostatized backwards, returning to the desert? He said: No; rather the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me to dwell in the desert."
"6319","Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Usfuri narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Hammad ibn Mas'adah informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', that he would prostrate at the place of the mushaf, glorifying Allah therein, and he mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek out that place."
"6320","Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Hammad ibn Mas'adah informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, from Salamah, who said: Amir, my uncle, came and said: Give me your weapon. So I gave it to him. Then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Provide me a weapon. He said: Where is your weapon? I said: I gave it to Amir, my uncle. He said: I find none resembling you except the one who said: Grant me a brother dearer to me than myself. So he gave me his bow, his shield, and three arrows from his quiver."
"6321","Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Hammad ibn Mas'adah informed us, from Yazid, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: Hunger struck us on the day of Khaybar, and the people kindled fires. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What are these fires? They said: Domestic donkeys. He said: Pour out what is in them and break the pots. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we pour out what is in them and wash them? He said: Or that."
"6322","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Yazid, freedman of Salamah, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: During Ramadan in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whoever wished fasted, and whoever wished broke the fast and ransomed with the food of a poor person, until this verse was revealed: {So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it}."
"6323","Buraydah ibn Sufyan al-Aslami from Salamah: Abu Shu'ayb Abdullah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Buraydah ibn Sufyan al-Aslami narrated to me, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the banner to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, sending him to one of the fortresses of Khaybar; he fought, then returned without victory, having striven hard. He said: I shall certainly give the banner tomorrow to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger; Allah will grant victory at his hands; he is not one who flees. So he called Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) while he had ophthalmia, spat in his eyes, then said: Take this banner until Allah grants you victory. Salamah said: By Allah, he went out hurrying, and I followed behind him, until he planted the banner in a pile of stones. A Jew looked down upon him from the top of the fortress and said: Who are you? He said: I am Ali ibn Abi Talib. The Jew said: You have overcome them, by what was revealed to Musa. He did not return until Allah granted him victory."
"6324","Ata', freedman of al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from Salamah: Sahl ibn Musa Shiran al-Ramahurmuzi and Abdullah ibn Ahmad narrated to us, both saying: al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim al-Anbari narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Ata', freedman of al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, narrated to us, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall certainly give the banner today to a man whom Allah and His Messenger love, and who loves Allah and His Messenger. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Ali; I brought him while he had ophthalmia, and he spat in his eyes."
"6325","Yazid ibn Khusayfah from Salamah: Hafs ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from Salamah, who said: I travelled with the Messenger of Allah, and I never saw him pray after Fajr, nor after Asr at all."
"6326","Zayd ibn Abd al-Rahman from Salamah ibn al-Akwa': al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Harish narrated to us, Abu Hammam Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: I came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the direction of al-Aqiq, until, when we were at the mountain pass called Thaniyyat al-Hawd at al-Aqiq, he gestured with his hand toward the east and said: I see the places where Allah's enemy the Dajjal will descend; he will come until he alights at such-and-such, then the rabble of the people will go out to him. There is no mountain pass of Madinah but upon it is an angel or two guarding it. With him are two images: the image of Paradise and the image of a red Fire. With him are devils resembling the dead, saying to the living: Do you know me? I am your brother, I am your father, I am your kinsman; have I not died? This is our Lord, so follow him. Allah will decree what He wills concerning him. Allah will send to him a man of the Muslims who will silence and refute him, saying: This is the liar, O people; let him not deceive you, for he is a liar speaking falsehood; your Lord is not one-eyed. He will say: Will you follow me? He will refuse, so he will cut him in two, being granted that power. He will say: I shall restore him for you. Allah will resurrect him, more severe in belying him and more severe in reviling him than ever. He will say: O people, what you have seen is a trial you are tried with, a temptation you are tempted with. If he were truthful, let him restore me another time; nay, he is a liar. Then he will order him to this Fire — which is the image of Paradise. He will emerge toward Syria."
"6327","Salamah ibn Qays al-Ashja'i: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar and al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you perform ablution, rinse out your nose; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6328","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you sniff water into your nose, blow it out; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6329","Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you perform ablution, rinse out your nose thoroughly; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6330","Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you perform ablution, rinse out your nose; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6331","Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Abu Bilal al-Ash'ari narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays al-Ashja'i, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you sniff water into your nose, blow it out; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6332","Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you sniff water into your nose, blow it out; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6333","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When you perform ablution, rinse out your nose thoroughly; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6334","Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you perform ablution, rinse out your nose; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6335","Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti narrated to us, Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Sufyan informed me, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays al-Ashja'i, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When you perform ablution, rinse out your nose; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6336","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas and Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you perform ablution, rinse out your nose; and when you cleanse with stones, use an odd number."
"6337","Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays al-Ashja'i, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the Farewell Pilgrimage: Four things — I am not today more miserly with them than the day I heard them. He said: Do not associate anything with Allah, do not kill the soul which Allah has forbidden except by right, do not commit adultery, and do not steal."
"6338","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir and Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the Farewell Pilgrimage: Verily they are only four. I am not today more miserly with them than the day I heard them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do not associate anything with Allah, do not kill the soul which Allah has forbidden except by right, do not commit adultery, and do not steal."
"6339","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, raising it to Salamah ibn Qays, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abu Musa while he was reciting, and said: This one has been given of the melodious voices of the family of Dawud."
"6340","Salamah ibn Yazid al-Ju'fi: Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrating from al-Sha'bi, from Alqamah ibn Qays, from Salamah ibn Yazid, who said: My brother and I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, our mother in the pre-Islamic age used to honour guests and uphold kinship; will any of her deeds benefit her? He said: No. We said: She buried alive a sister of ours who had not reached puberty? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The buried infant and the one who buries are in the Fire, unless the one who buries attains Islam and embraces it."
"6341","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Shayban, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Alqamah, from Salamah ibn Yazid al-Ju'fi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The woman who buries alive and the buried infant are in the Fire."
"6342","Al-Hasan ibn Fadalal al-Sayrafi narrated to us, Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Jabir, from Yazid ibn Murrah, from Salamah ibn Yazid al-Ju'fi, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: {Verily We have created them a creation, then made them virgins, loving, of equal age}, saying: Of previously married and unmarried women."
"6343","Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mustamirr al-Uruqi narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Za'idah, from Simak ibn Harb, from Alqamah ibn Wa'il al-Hadrami, from Salamah ibn Yazid al-Ju'fi, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what if there are rulers over us after you who take the right due upon us but withhold the right which Allah has made for us — shall we fight them and disobey them? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon them is what they were charged with, and upon you is what you were charged with."
"6344","Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Adam narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from Jabir, from Yazid ibn Murrah, from Salamah ibn Yazid, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: {Verily We have created them a creation} — meaning previously married women; and virgins: those who were so in this world."
"6345","Salamah ibn Salamah ibn Waqsh al-Ansari, of Aqabah and Badr: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah: Concerning the naming of those of the Ansar who attended al-Aqabah, then of al-Aws, then of Banu Abd al-Ashhal: Salamah ibn Salamah ibn Waqsh, who attended Badr."
"6346","Al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the naming of those of the Ansar who attended al-Aqabah, then of Banu Abd al-Ashhal: Salamah ibn Salamah ibn Waqsh."
"6347","Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham al-Sadusi narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, concerning the naming of those of the Ansar who attended Badr, then of al-Aws, then of Banu Abd al-Ashhal: Salamah ibn Salamah ibn Waqsh ibn Zughbah ibn Za'wra' ibn Abd al-Ashhal ibn Jusham ibn al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Aws."
"6348","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Zayd ibn Jubayrah ibn Mahmud ibn Abi Jubayrah al-Ansari of Banu al-Ashhal narrated to me, from his father Jubayrah ibn Mahmud, from Salamah ibn Salamah ibn Waqsh, companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that they both entered a wedding feast while Salamah was in ablution. They ate, then went out, and Salamah performed ablution. Jubayrah said to him: Were you not in ablution? He said: Yes, but I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — we had gone out from an invitation we were called to, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in ablution — he ate, then performed ablution. I said to him: Were you not in ablution, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes, but a new matter occurs, and this is of what has occurred."
"6349","Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, (haw) and Sahl ibn Musa Shiran al-Ramahurmuzi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdah al-Dabbi narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Salih ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed me, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Salamah ibn Salamah ibn Waqsh, who said: There was a Jewish man among our houses. And he mentioned the hadith."
"6350","Salamah ibn Sakhr al-Bayadi al-Ansari: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Salman ibn Sakhr al-Ansari, that he made his wife upon himself like the back of his mother until Ramadan passed. She grew plump and full, and he fell upon her in the middle of Ramadan. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as if magnifying that. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Can you free a slave? He said: No. He said: Can you fast two consecutive months? He said: No. He said: Can you feed sixty poor persons? He said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Farwah ibn Amr, give him that faraq — a basket holding fifteen sa' — let him give it to sixty poor persons. He said: Upon one poorer than me? By Him who sent you with the truth, there is no household between its two lava plains more in need of it than us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed, then said: Take it to your family."
"6351","Muhammad ibn Hasan ibn Kaysan al-Missisi narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to him that Salamah ibn Sakhr made his wife like the back of his mother until Ramadan passed. When half had passed he fell upon her at night. He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He said: Free a slave. He said: I cannot find one. He said: Can you fast two consecutive months? He said: No. He said: Then feed sixty poor persons. He said: I cannot find. He said to Farwah ibn Amr — who used to estimate dates on the trees in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) —: Give him that basket containing fifteen sa' of dates. He said: Go and feed sixty poor persons."
"6352","Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Salman ibn Sakhr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him a basket containing fifteen sa', saying: Feed it to sixty poor persons, a mudd for each poor person."
"6353","Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna Abu Amir narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, that Salman ibn Sakhr al-Bayadi made his wife upon himself like the back of his mother if he touched her until Ramadan passed. When half of Ramadan had passed she grew plump and full, pleasing him, so he touched her at night. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking about that. He said: Free a slave. He said: I cannot find one. He said: Fast two consecutive months. He said: I cannot. He said: Feed sixty poor persons. He said: I cannot find. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a faraq containing fifteen sa' of dates, or sixteen sa' of dates. He said: Take them and give charity with them to sixty poor persons."
"6354","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Salman ibn Sakhr al-Ansari, that he made zihar from his wife, then fell upon her before expiating. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him with a single expiation."
"6355","Ubayd ibn Ghannam ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata', from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Salamah ibn Sakhr al-Bayadi, who said: I was a man indulging much in women; I did not think any man indulged in that more than I did. When Ramadan came I made zihar from my wife until Ramadan ended. While she was conversing one night, something of her was uncovered to me, so I leapt upon her and had intercourse with her. In the morning I went to my people and informed them of my affair, saying to them: Ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on my behalf. They said: We shall not do so, lest a scripture from Allah descend concerning us, or there be concerning us a command from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving lasting disgrace upon us. Rather we shall surrender you with your offence; go yourself and mention your case to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So I went out until I came to him and informed him of the news. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is it really you? I said: It is really I, and here I am, O Messenger of Allah, patient with Allah's judgment upon me. He said: Then free a slave. I said: By Him who sent you with the truth, I own nothing this morning but my own neck. He said: Then fast two consecutive months. I said: O Messenger of Allah, tribulation befell me only on account of fasting. He said: Then give charity and feed sixty poor persons. I said: By Him who sent you with the truth, we spent this night of ours with no supper. He said: Then go to the keeper of the charity of Banu Zurayq and tell him to hand over to you; feed sixty poor persons and benefit from the rest."
"6356","Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Salamah ibn Sakhr, who said: I made zihar in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then fell upon her before expiating. I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he gave me a ruling of expiation."
"6357","Salamah al-Muhabbiq al-Hudhali — the name of al-Muhabbiq being Sakhr of Banu Lihyan: Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Bakr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Jawn ibn Qatadah, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning a man who fell upon his wife's slave girl: If he coerced her, she is free and he owes the like of her; if she obeyed him willingly, she belongs to him and he owes the like of her."
"6358","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Qabisah ibn Hurayth, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged concerning a man who had intercourse with his wife's slave girl: If he coerced her, she is free, and he owes her mistress the like of her; if she obeyed him willingly, she remains his slave, and he owes her mistress the like of her."
"6359","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan al-Basri, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, that a man approached his wife's slave girl, and that was raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: If she obeyed him willingly, she is a slave, and she has the like of her — meaning for her mistress; if he coerced her, she is free, and her mistress has the like of her. Ali said: I said to Sufyan: Qatadah says: from al-Hasan, from Qabisah ibn Hurayth, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq. Sufyan said: Amr said: Between them is a person, or a man. Al-Hudhali — meaning Abu Bakr al-Hudhali — said to him: Between them is Qabisah ibn Hurayth. Sufyan said: I know this al-Hudhali only as being of the people of Salamah."
"6360","Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Salamah ibn Rabi'ah ibn al-Muhabbiq, who said: I heard a woman asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a slave girl of hers with whom her husband had gone on a journey and had intercourse with her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he coerced her, she is free, and he owes the like of her; if she obeyed him willingly, she is his slave girl, and he owes the like of her."
"6361","Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: I asked al-Hasan about a man who falls upon his wife's slave girl? He said: Qabisah ibn Hurayth al-Ansari narrated to me, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, that a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to travel and raid constantly; his wife sent with him a slave girl of hers, saying: She will wash your head, serve you and guard your baggage — but she did not make her lawful to him. His journey in that direction grew long, and he fell upon the slave girl. When he returned, the slave girl informed her mistress of that. She became intensely jealous and angry, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of what he had done. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: If he coerced her, she is free and he owes the like of her; if he approached her willingly on her part, she belongs to him and he owes the like of her price. And he did not impose a hadd punishment in it."
"6362","Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, both saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, (haw) and Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, both saying: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Jawn ibn Qatadah, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey and came upon a hanging water-skin, seeking water to drink. It was said: It is carrion. He said: The slaughter-like purification of skin is its tanning."
"6363","Abdan narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mustamirr al-Uruqi narrated to us, Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Jawn ibn Qatadah, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to a woman seeking water to drink; a water-skin was brought and he drank. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, it is carrion. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The tanning of skin is its purification."
"6364","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Jawn ibn Qatadah, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the raid of Tabuk, called for water from a woman. She said: I have nothing but water in the skin of a dead animal. He said: Have you not tanned it? She said: Yes. He said: Its lawful purification is its tanning."
"6365","Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal al-Darir narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, without mentioning Jawn ibn Qatadah."
"6366","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ubaydillah ibn al-Hasan al-Anbari al-Hujaymi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Jawn, from Salamah ibn al-Muhabbiq, that a man of Quraysh travelled, and his wife sent with him a slave girl of hers; he had intercourse with her. When he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she informed him of that. He said: If she obeyed him willingly, she belongs to him and he owes the like of her; if he coerced her, she is free and he owes the like of her."
"6367","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq informed me, from Mu'adh ibn Sa'wah al-Rasibi, from Sinan ibn Salamah al-Hudhali, from his father — who had accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he sent two sacrificial camels with a man, saying: Mark them on their humps, then dip the sandals in their blood, then strike their sides with them, so it be known they are sacrificial camels."
"6368","Ahmad ibn Isma'il ibn al-Harb al-Adawi al-Basri narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Harb ibn Shaddad informed us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir informed us, from al-Hanafi, from Sinan ibn Salamah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he passed on the day of Khaybar by cooking pots containing flesh of the people's donkeys, and ordered them overturned."
"6369","Salamah ibn Nu'aym al-Ashja'i: al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi narrated to us, Kinanah ibn Jabalah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Salamah ibn Nu'aym al-Ashja'i, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever dies not associating anything with Allah will enter Paradise."
"6370","Sahl ibn Musa Shiran al-Ramahurmuzi narrated to us, Isa ibn Shadhan narrated to us, Musa ibn Mas'ud narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Salamah ibn Nu'aym al-Ashja'i, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: There is no god but Allah, will enter Paradise."
"6371","Salamah Abu Amr ibn Salamah al-Jarmi: Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, Amr ibn Salamah Abu Yazid al-Jarmi narrated to us, he said: We were beside water on the people's route; we would ask them: What is this matter? They would say: A man claims he is a prophet, that Allah sent him, and that Allah revealed to him such-and-such. I heard nothing of that but I memorized it, as if it were pasted into my breast with glue, until I had gathered much Qur'an from it. He said: The Arabs would delay their Islam until the conquest, saying: See, if he overcomes them, he is truthful, he is a prophet. When the event of the conquest came to us, every people hastened with the Islam of their folk. My father went with the Islam of the people of our quarter, stayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as long as Allah willed him to stay, then came back. When he drew near us we went out to meet him. When we saw him he said: By Allah, I have come to you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in truth. He said: Then he said: He commands you with such-and-such, forbids you such-and-such, and that you pray such-and-such prayer at such-and-such time; when prayer comes, let one of you call the adhan and let the most versed of you in Qur'an lead you. He said: They looked at the people of our quarter and found none more versed in Qur'an than I, because of what I had memorized from the caravans. So they put me forward, and I would lead them in prayer though I was six years old. He said: I had a cloak on; when I prostrated it would ride up from me. A woman of the quarter said: Will you not cover from us the backside of your reciter? He said: So they clothed me with a shirt from the entrepôt of Bahrayn, and I never rejoiced in anything more than my rejoicing in that shirt."
"6372","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, he said: I heard Amr ibn Salamah saying: We were at a settlement past which those who came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pass; they would say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said such, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said such. I memorized much Qur'an from that. My father went as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among a group of his people; he taught them prayer, saying: Let the most reciting of you lead you. He said: I was the most reciting of them because of what I had memorized, so I would lead them, wearing a cloak which, when I prostrated, uncovered me. A woman of the people said: Cover from us the private part of your reciter. So they bought me an Omani shirt, and I never rejoiced in anything after Islam as I rejoiced in it. I would lead them, being seven or eight years old."
"6373","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Amr ibn Salamah, who said: We were at a water settlement on the people's route. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"6374","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: We were at a great water settlement on the highway; riders would come to us and we would ask them: What does this man say? — meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They would say: He says such, commands them such, and forbids you such. I was a boy of six years; I heard nothing but it was as if written in my heart. The people would say: See what the man's people do. When Makkah was conquered, the people sent their delegations to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); my father was the delegate of his people. He came to them saying: I have come to you from the Messenger of Allah; he commands you such and forbids you such; when prayer comes, let the most versed of you in Qur'an lead you. They looked and found none more versed in Qur'an than I, so they put me forward and I led them in prayer, wearing a cloak of mine, or a wrap of mine. An old woman said: Will you not cover from us the backside of your reciter? A garment was bought from the entrepôt of Bahrayn, and a woman of the quarter cut it into a shirt for me. I never rejoiced in anything after Islam as I rejoiced in that shirt."
"6375","Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Zuhayr narrated to us, Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Amr ibn Salamah, who said: Some men of the quarter came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him saying: Let the most versed of you in Qur'an lead you. So they put me forward while I was a boy, and I would lead them wearing a patched cloak with a tear in it; when I prostrated, my backside would show. They said to my father: Will you not cover his backside from us? I would encourage them to learn the Qur'an. Zuhayr said: He remained the imam of his people in prayer and over their funerals."
"6376","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Mis'ar al-Jarmi narrated to me, he said: I heard Amr ibn Salamah, that his father and some men of his people came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the people embraced Islam; they learned the Qur'an and fulfilled their needs, then asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Who will lead us in prayer? Or who will pray for us? He said: The most accomplished of you will lead you in prayer, or will pray for you — or he said: the most gathering of you of the Qur'an. They returned to their people and asked them, finding none who had gathered more than I had gathered, though I was a boy wearing a wrap. So they put me forward, and I led them in prayer — or he said: I prayed for them. I remained the imam of Jarm to this day of mine; he would lead them in their mosque and pray over their funerals."
"6377","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilabah, from Amr ibn Salamah, who said: I was a boy meeting the caravans coming from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), asking them to recite to me. They informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let the most versed of you in Qur'an lead you. So I would lead them."
"6378","Salamah ibn Nufayl al-Sakuni, then al-Taraghimi: Abu Zayd al-Hawti narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Artah ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, from Damrah ibn Habib, from Salamah ibn Nufayl, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a questioner asked him: Were you brought food from heaven? He said: Yes, I was brought food in a heated vessel. He said: O Prophet of Allah, what remained of it beyond you? He said: Yes. He said: What was done with it? He said: It was raised to heaven. It is revealed to me that I shall not remain among you but a little, then you shall not remain after me but a little, saying: When, when? Then you will come in scattered groups. Before the Hour there will be severe pestilence, and after it years of earthquakes."
"6379","Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi narrated to us, al-Abbas ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Hani' ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ablah narrated to us, from his uncle Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Salamah ibn Nufayl, who said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It is revealed to me that I am to be taken in death without delay, and that you will follow me in scattered groups, striking one another's necks. There will remain of my ummah people fighting upon the truth; Allah will turn astray through them the hearts of peoples, and Allah will provide them from them, until the Hour comes and Allah's promise arrives. Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, and the stronghold of the believers' abode is in Syria."
"6380","Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, (haw) and Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih al-Himsi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Aftas narrated to me, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, Salamah ibn Nufayl al-Sakuni narrated to me, who said: I drew near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until my knees almost touched his thigh. I said: O Messenger of Allah, horses have been abandoned, weapons laid down, and peoples claim there is no fighting. He said: They lied; now fighting has come. There shall remain of my ummah a community standing upon the truth, manifest over the people; Allah will turn astray the hearts of a people who fight them, that they may gain from them. And he said, turning his back to Yemen: I find the breath of the Most Merciful from here. It has been revealed to me that I am restrained without delay, and you will follow me in scattered groups. Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, and their people are aided upon them."
"6381","6359 - Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti narrated to us, Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muhajir narrated to us, that al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to him from Jubayr ibn Nufayr from Salama ibn Nufayl, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stronghold of the abode of Islam is in al-Sham."
"6382","6360 - Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi narrated to us, my father narrated to me from his father, Nasr ibn Alqama narrated to me, tracing the hadith back to Jubayr ibn Nufayr from Salama ibn Nufayl, who said: While I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, the horses have been let loose, weapons have been laid down, and some people claim there is no more fighting and that the war has laid down its burdens? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They have lied; now fighting has come. A party of my umma will continue to fight in the cause of Allah, unharmed by those who oppose them; Allah will cause the hearts of some people to deviate so that He provides for them from them, and they will fight until the Hour comes. Horses will continue to have good tied to their forelocks until the Hour comes, and war will not lay down its burdens until Gog and Magog emerge."
"6383","6361 - Abd Allah ibn Sad ibn Yahya al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Farwa Yazid ibn Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yasin al-Zayyat narrated to us from Abu Salama al-Himsi from Yahya ibn Jabir from Salama ibn Nufayl, who said: A young man came and stood before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said in a loud voice: O Messenger of Allah, what of one who did not leave any evil deed without doing it, nor any sin without committing it, and there was no share of wrongdoing, small or great, but he took his portion of it with his right hand, and one whose sins, if divided among the people of Madina, would overwhelm them? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you accepted Islam? - or: Are you a Muslim? He said: As for me, I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. He said: Go, for Allah has replaced your evil deeds with good deeds. He said: O Messenger of Allah, and my treacheries and immoral acts? He said: And your treacheries and your immoral acts - three times - and the young man turned away saying: Allah is Most Great, and I kept hearing him proclaiming takbir until he disappeared from me, or was hidden from me."
"6384","612 - Salama ibn Hisham ibn al-Mughira al-Makhzumi. 6362 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr informed me, saying: Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia and Salama ibn Hisham and al-Walid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughira fled from the polytheists to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with Ayyash and Salama bound together riding behind each other on one camel, and al-Walid driving them; al-Walid's finger was injured, and he said: May my way be ruined. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) learned of their departure to him and their condition before the people knew, so he prayed Fajr and bowed in the first of the two rak'as, and when he raised his head he supplicated for them before prostrating, saying: O Allah, save Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia; O Allah, save Salama ibn Hisham; O Allah, save al-Walid; O Allah, save the oppressed among the believers; O Allah, make Your grip severe upon Mudar, and make it years upon them like the years of Yusuf."
"6385","613 - Salama ibn Umayya, brother of Yala ibn Umayya ibn Khalaf al-Jumahi. 6363 - Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Usfuri al-Basri narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Shahid narrated to us, Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us from Ata ibn Abi Rabah from Safwan ibn Abd Allah ibn Safwan from his two uncles Yala and Salama, sons of Umayya, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the expedition of Tabuk, and with us was a companion of ours from the people of Makka; he fought a man, and the man bit his forearm, so he pulled it from his mouth and his two front teeth fell out. He went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to ask for blood-money, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of you goes to his brother and bites him like a stallion bites, or as a stallion bites, then he comes to ask for blood-money? He has no right to it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared it void."
"6386","614 - Salama ibn Sad al-Anazi. 6364 - Abu Khalifa narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Said al-Karabisi, known as Shu'ba, who used to attend Ali ibn al-Madini, narrated to us, Hafs ibn Salama ibn Hafs ibn al-Musayyab ibn Shayban ibn Qays narrated to us from Qays ibn Salama from Salama ibn Sad, that he came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he and a group of his family and children; they sought permission and entered, and he said: Who are these? It was said to him: This is the delegation of Anaza. He said: Bravo, bravo, bravo; what an excellent tribe is Anaza, wronged yet aided; welcome to the people of Shuayb and the in-laws of Musa. Ask, O Salama, for your need. He said: I have come to ask you about what you have made obligatory upon me concerning camels, sheep, and goats, and he informed him. Then he sat with him a while, then sought permission to leave, and he said to him: Leave. He had barely risen when he said: O Allah, provide Anaza with sufficiency, neither mere subsistence nor excess."
"6387","Of those named Salama. 616 - Salama ibn Qaysar al-Hadrami. 6365 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf and Shuayb ibn Yahya al-Tujibi narrated to us; and Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri narrated to us, Said ibn Ufayr narrated to us; and al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us; they said: Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us, Zabban ibn Faid narrated to us from Lahi'a ibn Uqba from Amr ibn Rabia al-Hadrami, who said: I heard Salama ibn Qaysar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever fasts a day seeking the Face of Allah, Allah will distance him from Hell the distance of a crow that flew as a fledgling until it died of old age."
"6388","Of those named Salim. 617 - Salim ibn Ubayd al-Ashjai. 6366 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Bayan al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us from Sufyan from Salama ibn Kuhayl from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, from Salim ibn Ubayd, who was of the people of al-Suffa."
"6389","6367 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, Salama ibn Nubayt narrated to us from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind from Nubayt ibn Sharit from Salim ibn Ubayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lost consciousness during his illness, then regained consciousness and said: Has the time of prayer come? I said: Yes. He said: Order Bilal to call the adhan, and order Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer. Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: My father is a tender-hearted man; if only you would order someone else to lead the people in prayer. Then he lost consciousness again, then regained consciousness and said: Has the time of prayer come? I said: Yes. He said: Order Bilal to call the adhan, and order Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer. Aisha said: My father is a tender-hearted man; if only you would order someone else to lead the people in prayer. Then he lost consciousness, then regained consciousness and said: Has the prayer been established? I said: Yes. He said: Bring me a man to lean upon. Burayda came, and another man, and he leaned upon them and came to the mosque. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was standing leading the people in prayer; Abu Bakr began to step back, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevented him, and he was seated beside Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) until he finished his prayer. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I will not hear any man say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has died, but I will strike him with the sword. Then he took my forearm and leaned upon me and walked until we came. He said: Make room, and they made room for him; he bent over him and touched him and said: Indeed you are dead and indeed they are dead. They said: O companion of the Messenger of Allah, has the Messenger of Allah died? He said: Yes. Then they knew it was as he said. They said: O companion of the Messenger of Allah, shall prayer be offered over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. They said: How shall we pray over him? He said: Groups will enter, proclaim takbir, supplicate and pray, then leave, and others will come, until they finish. They said: O companion of the Messenger of Allah, shall the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) be buried? He said: Yes. They said: And where shall he be buried? He said: Where he was taken, for Allah Almighty did not take him except in a good spot. Then they knew it was as he said. Then he stood and said: Your companion is with you, and he ordered them to wash him, then he went out. The Muhajirun gathered to consult and said: Let us go to our brothers among the Ansar, for they have a share in this matter. They went, and a man of the Ansar said: From us an amir and from you an amir. Umar took the hand of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: Tell me, who possesses these three: The second of the two when they were in the cave - who were they? When he said to his companion: Do not grieve - who was his companion? Allah is with us. Then he took the hand of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and struck upon it, and said to the people: Pledge allegiance to him. So they pledged allegiance to him, a good and fine pledge."
"6390","6368 - Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isa al-Tabba narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us from Mansur from Hilal ibn Yasaf from Salim ibn Ubayd, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man sneezed and said: Peace be upon you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And upon your mother. Then he said: When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, abundantly - or: Praise be to Allah in every circumstance - and let whoever is with him say: May Allah have mercy on you, and let him reply to them: May Allah forgive me and you."
"6391","6369 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us from Mansur from Hilal ibn Yasaf from a man, who said: We were with Salim ibn Ubayd on a journey when a man sneezed and said: Peace be upon you. Salim ibn Ubayd said to him: Upon you and upon your mother; perhaps you resented something I said to you: Upon you and upon your mother? He said: I wished you had not mentioned my mother, for good or ill, before people. He said to him: I only said to you what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a man sneezed and said: Peace be upon you, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you and upon your mother. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sneezes let him say: Praise be to Allah in every circumstance, and let his brother or companion say to him: May Allah have mercy on you, and let him say: May Allah forgive us and you."
"6392","Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa ibn Utba ibn Rabia; he was present at Badr and was martyred on the day of Yamama, may Allah have mercy on him. Of the reports of Salim and his death. 6370 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of those who were present at Badr from Quraysh, then from Banu Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf: Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa."
"6393","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Ibn Lahi'a from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of those martyred on the day of Yamama: Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa ibn Utba ibn Rabia."
"6394","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj, Nafi' informed us that he heard Ibn Umar (may Allah have mercy on him) say: Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, used to lead the early Muhajirun, the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Ansar in prayer in the mosque of Quba; among them were Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), Abu Salama, Zayd, and Amir ibn Rabia."
"6395","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, Yahya ibn Uthman al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Abi al-Ash'ath narrated to us from Hisham ibn Urwa from Nafi' from Ibn Umar: Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, used to lead the Muhajirun who had emigrated to Madina, among them Umar and others of the Muhajirun, because he knew the most Quran among them."
"6396","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj, Abd Allah ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Mulayka informed me that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr informed him that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him that Sahla bint Suhayl ibn Amr came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Salim - meaning Salim, the freedman of Hudhayfa - is with us in our house, and he has reached what men reach, and knows what men know. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Breastfeed him; you will become unlawful to him."
"6397","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz al-Basri, Abu Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qaddah informed me, al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to me from Aisha, that the wife of Abu Hudhayfa came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, comes in to see me while I am lightly dressed, and I feel something in myself. He said: Breastfeed him; what you feel will leave you."
"6398","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud, Said ibn Maslama al-Umawi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us from Yahya ibn Said and Rabia ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from Aisha: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sahla bint Suhayl to nurse Salim so that what was in the heart of Abu Hudhayfa would go away."
"6399","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiy, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyayna informed us from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim from his father from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: Sahla bint Suhayl came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I see something in the face of Abu Hudhayfa concerning Salim. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Breastfeed him. She said: He is grown. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled and said: Do I not know that he is a man? Then she came later and said: By Him who honored you with what He honored you with, I no longer see anything in the face of Abu Hudhayfa."
"6400","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Razzaq from Malik ibn Anas from Ibn Shihab from Urwa from Aisha: Abu Hudhayfa ibn Utba ibn Rabia, who was a Badri, had adopted Salim, who is called Salim the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, just as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had adopted Zayd. Abu Hudhayfa married Salim - regarding him as his son - to his niece Fatima bint al-Walid ibn Utba, who was of the early Muhajirat and at that time among the best unmarried women of Quraysh. When Allah Almighty revealed concerning that what He revealed: Call them by their fathers - the verse - each of those adoptees was called by his father; if his father was unknown he was referred back to his patrons. Sahla bint Suhayl, wife of Abu Hudhayfa, of Banu Amir ibn Luayy, came and said: O Messenger of Allah, we used to regard Salim as a son; he comes in to see me while I am scantily clad, and we have only one room; what do you think? He said to her: Breastfeed him; you will become unlawful to him."
"6401","What Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, transmitted. Narrated Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us from Abu Ishaq from Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil from Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, that he was with Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) on a roof in Ramadan while he was praying; he came to him and said: Will you not eat, O successor of the Messenger of Allah? He gestured with his hand, and did so twice; on the third time he said: Bring me your food. He ate, prayed two rak'as, then entered the mosque and the prayer was established."
"6402","Narrated Ahmad ibn Ishaq ibn Wadih al-Assal al-Misri, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us from Musa ibn Uqba from Abu Ishaq from Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil from Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, the same."
"6403","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti, Tamim ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us from Sharik from Abu Ishaq from Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil from Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfa, the same."
"6404","619 - Salim ibn Harmala ibn Zuhayr al-Adawi. Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim al-Anbari narrated to us; and Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Qattan al-Hamdani narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Isam al-Jurjani narrated to us; both said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Utba ibn Salim ibn Harmala ibn Zuhayr ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, my father Abd al-Aziz informed me that Abu Utba informed him that his father Salim came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while a young boy; the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) blessed him and supplicated for him, and he was washed with the leftover of his ablution water; that day he wore a forelock, having reached puberty or nearly so."
"6405","620 - Salim, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj al-Askari, Ismail ibn Ibrahim al-Tarjumaniy narrated to us, Umar ibn Harun narrated to us from Jafar ibn Muhammad from his father from Salim, servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make their hair into four braids; when they bathed they gathered them at the middle of their heads."
"6406","Of those named Sulaym. 621 - Sulaym ibn Jabir, Abu Jurayy al-Hujaymi; also said to be Jabir ibn Sulaym, and the correct view is Sulaym ibn Jabir. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Arim Abu al-Numan and Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; and Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us; they said: Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, Aqil ibn Talha narrated to us, Abu Jurayy al-Hujaymi narrated to me, saying: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we are people of the desert; we would like you to teach us a deed by which Allah may benefit us. He said: Do not belittle any good deed, even pouring from your bucket into the vessel of one seeking water, even speaking to your brother with a cheerful face. Beware of letting the lower garment hang below the ankles, for it is of arrogance, which Allah Almighty does not love. If a man reviles you for what he knows about you, do not revile him for what you know about him, for the reward of that is yours and its burden is upon him."
"6407","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu al-Khalil Abd al-Salam narrated to us, Ubayda al-Hujaymi narrated to us from Abu Tamim, who said: Abu Jurayy Jabir said: I rode a young camel of mine and came to Makka seeking him, and there he was sitting (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And upon you. I said: We desert people have harshness in us; teach me words by which Allah will benefit me. He said: Fear Allah, and do not belittle any good or kindness, and beware of letting the lower garment hang, for it is of arrogance, and Allah Almighty does not love the arrogant. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, you mentioned letting the garment hang, yet a man's leg may have sores or something he is ashamed of? He said: No harm up to mid-calf, or up to the ankles. A man before you wore a cloak and strutted in it; Allah Almighty looked at him from above His Throne and detested him, and commanded the earth, which swallowed him, and he keeps sinking into the earth. So beware of incurring the wrath of Allah Almighty."
"6408","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us from Yunus ibn Ubayd from Ubayda al-Hujaymi from Abu Tamima al-Hujaymi from Jabir ibn Sulaym, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was sitting wrapped in a woolen cloak whose fringe had fallen over his feet. I said: Which of you is Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah? He gestured with his hand to himself. I said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I am of the desert people, with their harshness, so advise me. He said: Do not belittle any good deed, even meeting your brother with an open face, even pouring from your bucket into the vessel of one seeking water. If a man insults you for what is in you, do not insult him for what you know is in him, for its reward will be yours and its burden upon him. Beware of letting the lower garment hang, for letting it hang is of arrogance, and Allah does not love arrogance. And never revile anyone. Then I never reviled anyone afterward, neither sheep nor camel."
"6409","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, al-Muthanna Abu Ghifar narrated to us, Abu Tamima al-Hujaymi narrated to us from Abu Jurayy, who said: I said: Upon you be peace, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do not say: Upon you be peace; Upon you be peace is the greeting of the dead. Say: Peace be upon you. I said: Are you the Messenger of Allah? He said: I am the Messenger of Allah, who, when harm befalls you and you call upon Him, removes it from you, and when a year of drought befalls you and you call upon Him, He makes it easy for you. I said: Entrust a charge to me. He said: Never revile anyone, never belittle any good deed, speak to your brother with a cheerful face, raise your lower garment to mid-calf, and if you refuse then to the ankles. Beware of letting the garment hang, for it is of arrogance, and Allah does not love arrogance. If a man insults you for what he knows of you, do not insult him for what you know of him, for its burden is only upon him."
"6410","Narrated Musa ibn Sahl Abu Imran al-Jawni, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Muthanna ibn Sad Abu Ghifar narrated to us, Abu Tamima al-Hujaymi narrated to us from Abu Jurayy, who said: I came to a man from whose word the people issued forth only; I said: By Allah, for the sake of this man - who is this? They said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: Upon you be peace, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do not say: Upon you be peace, for it is the greeting of the dead; rather say: Peace be upon you. Then he mentioned something similar."
"6411","Narrated Sulayman ibn al-Hasan al-Attar al-Basri, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us, Salim Abu Jumay narrated to us, Rashid Abu Muhammad al-Himmani narrated to us from Zayd ibn Hilal from Abu Tamima al-Hujaymi from Sulaym ibn Jabir, who said: I heard of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I called for my mount and said: I shall surely go to this man and hear from him. I came to him and found him sitting wrapped in a cloak, and I heard him answering a questioner: Do not belittle any good deed, even pouring from the excess of your bucket into the vessel of one seeking water. Beware of letting the garment hang, for it is of arrogance, and Allah does not love arrogance. If a man insults you, or says what is not in you, do not insult him nor say to him what is not in him, so its reward will be yours and its burden upon him. Never revile anyone. I have not reviled anything - neither camel, nor sheep, nor man - since I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid reviling."
"6412","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Wahb ibn Baqiyya narrated to us, Khalid - meaning Ibn Abd Allah - al-Tahhan al-Wasiti informed us from Khalid al-Hadhdha from Abu Tamima al-Hujaymi from a man, who said: I sought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but did not find him, so I sat down. I saw a group of five or six men, with a man settling disputes among them over wounds; he settled among them for compensation in sheep. When the people stood, a man said: O Messenger of Allah; when I heard that I said: Upon you be peace, O Messenger of Allah - three times - and he said to me - three times: Upon you be peace is the greeting of the dead."
"6413","Narrated Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, Sahl ibn Tammam ibn Bazi narrated to us, Qurra ibn Khalid narrated to us from Qurra ibn Musa from Abu Jurayy al-Hujaymi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"6414","622 - Sulaym al-Ansari, then al-Sulami; martyred on the day of Uhud. Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki, al-Qanabi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us from Amr ibn Yahya from Muadh ibn Rifaa al-Zuraqi, that a man of Banu Salima called Sulaym came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we remain at our work and come in the evening, then Muadh ibn Jabal comes and calls for prayer; we come to him and he prolongs it for us? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Muadh, do not be a source of trial; either pray with me or lighten it for your people. Then he said: O Sulaym, what of the Quran do you have? He said: I have that I ask Allah for Paradise and seek refuge with Him from the Fire; by Allah, I do not know how to murmur as you murmur nor as Muadh murmurs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is my murmuring and Muadh's murmuring anything but asking Allah for Paradise and seeking refuge with Him from the Fire? But tomorrow you shall see when we meet the enemy - and the people were preparing for Uhud - and the man went out and was martyred."
"6415","Of those named Sufyan. 623 - Sufyan ibn al-Hakam al-Thaqafi, may Allah have mercy on him. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar and al-Thawri from Mansur from Sufyan ibn al-Hakam, or al-Hakam ibn Sufyan: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he performed ablution, would take a handful of water and sprinkle his private part with it."
"6416","624 - Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi. Of the reports of Sufyan ibn Abd Allah. Narrated Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri, Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Wahb narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd informed me from Amr ibn Shuayb from his father from his grandfather, that Banu Shababa, a clan of Fahm, used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a tenth on honey they owned - one skin out of every ten skins - and he would protect two valleys for them. When Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came to power, he appointed Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi over that region; they refused to pay him anything, saying: We used to pay it only to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Sufyan wrote to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) about that, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to him: Honeybees are but flying insects whose rain Allah Almighty drives as provision to whomever He wills; if they pay you what they used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), protect their valleys for them, otherwise leave them to the people. So they paid him what they used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he protected their valleys for them."
"6417","Narrated Musa ibn Harun, Kamil ibn Talha narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us, Amr ibn Shuayb narrated to us from his father from his grandfather, that a clan of Fahm used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from honey they owned, one skin out of every ten skins. When Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came to power, he appointed Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi over there, and he mentioned something like the hadith of Usama ibn Zayd."
"6418","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qanabi narrated to us from Malik from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dili from a son of Abd Allah ibn Sufyan from his grandfather Sufyan ibn Abd Allah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) sent him as a collector of zakat; he would count the young lambs against people, and they said: You count the young lambs against us but take nothing from them? When he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), he mentioned that to him, and Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Yes, count against them the young lamb carried by the shepherd, but do not take it, nor take the animal set aside for food, nor the one that nurses its young, nor the pregnant one, nor the ram of the flock; take the yearling and the two-year-old - that is fairness between the average and the best of the livestock."
"6419","And what Sufyan ibn Abd Allah transmitted. Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki, al-Qanabi narrated to us; and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us; and Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us; and Abu Khalifa al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us; they said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Maiz al-Amiri from Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me of a matter to hold fast to. He said: Say: My Lord is Allah, then be steadfast. I said: What do you fear most for me? He took hold of his own tongue, then said: This."
"6420","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Hiql ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Muawiya ibn Yahya narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Maiz al-Amiri, that Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me of a matter to hold fast to. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: My Lord is Allah, and be steadfast. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you fear most for me? He took hold of his own tongue, then said: This."
"6421","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us from Shu'ba from Yala ibn Ata from Abd Allah ibn Sufyan from his father, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, say to me a word in Islam after which I will ask no one. He said: Say: I believe in Allah, then be steadfast. I said: What should I guard against, O Messenger of Allah? He gestured toward his tongue."
"6422","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, Ali ibn Ma'bad al-Raqqi narrated to us, Khalid ibn Hayyan narrated to us from Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud from Salim ibn Ajlan al-Aftas from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr from Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi, who said: I said to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Prophet of Allah, tell me a word I may benefit by, and make it brief, that I may retain it. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not become angry. He repeated it several times asking him about that, and the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Do not become angry."
"6423","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati, Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us from Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Mujammi' from Abd al-Karim from Alqama ibn Sufyan ibn Abd Allah al-Thaqafi from his father, who said: We were among the delegation that came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he pitched two tents for us near the house of al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba, and Bilal would bring us our food for breaking fast. We would say: O Bilal, has the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broken his fast? He would say: Yes; I did not come to you until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broke his fast. Then he would put it down, and he would eat and we would eat, and Bilal would bring us our sahur."
"6424","625 - Sufyan ibn Atiyya ibn Rabi'a al-Thaqafi. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mukhtar al-Razi narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Isa ibn Abd Allah from Sufyan ibn Atiyya ibn Rabi'a al-Thaqafi, who said: Our delegation from Thaqif came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and accepted Islam in the middle of Ramadan; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to fast with him what remained, and did not order them to make up what they had missed."
"6425","626 - Sufyan ibn Asad al-Hadrami. Narrated Khayr ibn Arafa al-Misri, Haywa ibn Shurayh al-Himsi narrated to us; and Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us; both said: Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Abu Shurayh Dubara ibn Malik al-Hadrami narrated to me, saying: I heard my father narrate from Sufyan ibn Asad al-Hadrami that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: It is enough of a betrayal to tell your brother something he believes you in, while you are lying about it."
"6426","627 - Sufyan al-Muharibi. Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Rawh ibn Jamil Abu Muhammad al-Anazi al-Khawwas narrated to us, Yazid ibn Amr ibn Sufyan al-Muharibi narrated to me, my grandfather narrated to me from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Forbid your people from the drink of earthen jars, for it is unlawful from Allah and His Messenger."
"6427","628 - Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani. Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me from Abu Ushshana from Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani, that he was at the shade of the mount of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Farewell Pilgrimage - or that a man narrated that to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - who said: An evening excursion in the cause of Allah Almighty, or a morning excursion, is better than the world and what is in it."
"6428","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Amr ibn Sawwad al-Sarhi narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh informed me, saying: I heard Said ibn Abi Shamir al-Sabai say: I heard Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A hundred years will not come while anyone remains alive on its back."
"6429","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh, who said: I heard Said ibn Abi Shamir say: I heard Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The hundredth year will not come while anyone remains alive on its back."
"6430","629 - Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr al-Azdi al-Shanawi. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Hisham ibn Urwa informed me from his father from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Yemen will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew. Then al-Sham will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew."
"6431","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qanabi narrated to us from Malik; and Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas informed us from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Yemen will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew."
"6432","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, my father narrated to me, Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to us from his father, that Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr informed him that Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr al-Azdi informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Yemen will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew. Then Iraq will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew. Then al-Sham will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew."
"6433","Narrated Muhammad ibn Muadh al-Halabi, Abd Allah ibn Maslama al-Qanabi narrated to us, my father narrated to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Yemen will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew. Then Iraq will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew. Then al-Sham will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew."
"6434","Narrated Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki and Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Khuza'i ibn Ziyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil al-Muzani al-Basri, both said: Ibn Aisha narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yemen will be conquered, and men will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, but Madina is better for them if they only knew. Then Iraq will be conquered, and men will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew. Then al-Sham will be conquered, and men will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew. Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki added in his hadith: Urwa said: Then I met Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr at his death, and he informed me of this hadith."
"6435","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, al-Mundhir ibn Abd Allah narrated to us from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Yemen will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew."
"6436","Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, my father narrated to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Yemen will be conquered, and people will come driving on, carrying their families and those who obey them, while Madina is better for them if they only knew."
"6437","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qanabi narrated to us from Malik; and Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Malik informed us from Yazid ibn Khusayfa, that al-Saib ibn Yazid informed him that he heard Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, a man of Azd Shanu'a and of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrating to people at the door of the mosque, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever keeps a dog that neither guards crops nor livestock, a qirat is deducted from his deeds every day. He said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Ka'ba."
"6438","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qanabi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us from Yazid ibn Khusayfa from al-Saib ibn Yazid, that Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever keeps a dog that neither guards crops nor livestock, a qirat is deducted from his deeds every day. Al-Saib said: O Sufyan, did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Ka'ba."
"6439","630 - Sufyan ibn Abi al-Awja Abu Layla al-Ansari. What Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated from his father; al-Hakam ibn Utayba from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, al-Hasan ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Nasr al-Sakuni narrated to us from Ibn Abi Layla from al-Hakam from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant truly believes until I am dearer to him than himself, my family dearer to him than his family, and my being dearer to him than his own being."
"6440","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Azhar ibn Jamil narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: I read to al-Fudayl ibn Maysara, from Abu Jarir, that al-Hakam ibn Utayba and Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to him that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to them from his father, that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys a camel whose milk has been withheld, if he dislikes it let him return it along with a sa' of dates."
"6441","Abd Allah ibn Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrating from his uncle Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik informed us from Abd Allah ibn Isa from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Abu Layla, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the charity storehouse, and with him was Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him); he took a date and put it in his mouth, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inserted his finger and took it out of his mouth, then said: We are a household for whom charity is not lawful."
"6442","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, Harb ibn al-Hasan al-Tahhan narrated to us, al-Muttalib ibn Ziyada narrated to us from Abd Allah ibn Isa from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Abu Layla, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Conquest; he said: Abu Sufyan is among the arak trees. We entered and seized him; the Muslims kept coming to him, concealing their swords, until they brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said to him: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan; I have brought you this world and the Hereafter, so accept Islam and you will be safe. Al-Abbas was his friend, and said to him: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a man who loves renown. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a caller proclaiming in Makka: Whoever closes his door is safe, whoever lays down his weapon is safe, and whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe. Then he sent al-Abbas with him until they sat at the pass of al-Thaniyya. Banu Sulaym advanced, and he said: O Abbas, who are these? He said: These are Banu Sulaym. He said: What have I to do with Sulaym? Then Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) advanced with the Muhajirun, and he said: O Abbas, who are these? He said: This is Ali ibn Abi Talib among the Muhajirun. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced among the Ansar, and he said: O Abbas, who are these? He said: These are the red death; this is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the Ansar. Abu Sufyan said: I have seen the kingdom of Khosrau and Caesar, but I have never seen the like of the kingdom of your nephew. Al-Abbas said to him: It is but prophethood."
"6443","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Fasawi, Bashshar ibn Musa al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Khalid ibn Nafi' al-Ash'ari narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Isa narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from his father, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on an expedition when severe thirst struck us; we complained of that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Is there any leftover water in a waterskin? A man brought leftover water in a waterskin; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dug a pit in the ground, placed a leather mat over it, put his palm upon the ground, then said to the owner of the waterskin: Pour the water upon my palm and mention the name of Allah. He did so. Abu Layla said: I saw water springing from between the fingers of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until the people drank their fill and all were watered."
"6444","Abu Farwa Muslim ibn Salim al-Juhani narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Dirar ibn Surad Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Ali ibn Hisham narrated to us from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman from Abu Farwa from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall surely give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love. Then he called Ali and gave it to him."
"6445","Abu Fazara narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti and Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, both said: Ali ibn Abis narrated to us from Abu Fazara from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Abu Layla, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) observed i'tikaf in the last ten nights of Ramadan in a dome of palm leaves."
"6446","Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrating from his father. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, my father narrated to me, Zuhayr narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Isa narrated to us from his father from his grandfather from Abu Layla, that al-Hasan or al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) was in the lap of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he urinated, and I saw his urine come in streams. I jumped toward him, and he said: Leave my son until he finishes urinating; do not startle him until he finishes urinating. Then he followed it with water. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him, and he entered the charity storehouse; the boy took a date and put it in his mouth, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it out and said: Charity is not lawful for us."
"6447","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, my father narrated to me, Waki' narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us from his brother Isa from his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from his grandfather Abu Layla, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when al-Hasan came crawling until he climbed onto his chest and urinated upon him; we rushed to take him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My son, my son. Then he called for water and poured it over it."
"6448","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us from Ibn Abi Layla from his brother Isa, or al-Hakam, or both, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sees a snake and does not kill it out of fear of seeking it out, is not of us."
"6449","Qays ibn Muslim al-Jadali narrating from Ibn Abi Layla. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ya'la ibn al-Harith al-Muharibi narrated to us, my father narrated to me from Ghaylan ibn Jami' from Qays ibn Muslim from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that his father informed him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided sheep, assigning one sheep for every ten of his companions; it was plunder. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day ordered the cooking pots to be overturned, and it was the day of Khaybar."
"6450","Thabit al-Bunani narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us from Ibn Abi Layla; and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, Abda ibn Sulayman, Waki', and Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ruwasi narrated to us from Ibn Abi Layla from Thabit al-Bunani from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a prayer that was not obligatory; he passed by mention of the Fire and said: I seek refuge with Allah from the Fire; woe to the people of the Fire. The wording is that of the hadith of Uthman ibn Abi Shayba."
"6451","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us from Ibn Abi Layla from Thabit al-Bunani from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: Abu Layla said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about snakes in houses. He said: When you see them in your dwellings, say: We adjure you by the covenant Allah took upon you through Nuh; we adjure you by the covenant Sulayman took upon you, that you not harm us. If they return, kill them."
"6452","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati, Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to us from Thabit al-Bunani from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade house snakes, saying: When you see any of them in your dwellings, say: We adjure you by the covenant Nuh took upon you; we adjure you by the covenant Sulayman ibn Dawud took upon you. If they return, kill them."
"6453","Adi ibn Thabit narrating from Abu Layla. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, al-Muttalib ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Ibn Abi Layla from Adi ibn Thabit from Abu Layla, grandfather of Muhammad, that he passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying voluntarily; he passed by a verse mentioning the Fire and said: Woe to the people of the Fire; I seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the Fire."
"6454","631 - Sufyan ibn Qays ibn Aban al-Thaqafi. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Hammad ibn Fadala al-Sayrafi al-Basri, Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'la ibn Ka'b narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam informed me, my mother Umayma narrated to me from her mother Ruqayqa, that she informed her, saying: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came seeking support at al-Taif, he entered upon her; she ordered a drink of sawiq for him, and he drank. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ruqayqa, do not worship their idol nor pray to it. I said: Then they will kill me. He said: When they say that to you, say: My Lord is the Lord of this idol; and when you pray, turn your back to it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left them. The daughter of Ruqayqa said: My two brothers Sufyan and Wahb, sons of Qays ibn Aban, informed me, saying: When Thaqif accepted Islam, we went out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: What became of your mother? We said: She perished in the state you left her in. He said: Your mother had accepted Islam then."
"6455","Of those named Safina. 632 - Safina Abu Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). What Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated from Safina. Narrated Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd informed me that Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Uthman narrated to him from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, that Safina, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: I boarded the sea, and the ship I was in broke; I mounted a plank of its planks, and the plank cast me into a thicket wherein was a lion. It came toward me intending to attack, and I said: O Abu al-Harith, I am the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It lowered its head, came to me, pushed me with its shoulder until it brought me out of the thicket, placed me upon the road, and growled; I thought it was bidding me farewell, and that was my last encounter with it."
"6456","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah al-Harawi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd narrated to us from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir from Safina, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar, without mentioning Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Uthman."
"6457","Umar ibn Safina narrating from his father. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri narrated to us; and Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk informed us from Burayh ibn Umar ibn Safina, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his father from his grandfather, who said: He was cupped, and said: Take this blood and bury it away from beasts, birds, and people. He went aside and drank it, then mentioned that to him, and he laughed. The wording is that of Ahmad ibn Salih."
"6458","Narrated Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri, Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us from Burayh ibn Umar ibn Safina from his father from his grandfather, who said: I ate bustard meat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6459","Thabit al-Bajali narrating from Safina. Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, Dirar ibn Surad narrated to us, Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us from Shaqiq ibn Abi Abd Allah from Thabit al-Bajali from Safina, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came seeking permission and knocked lightly upon the door. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Safina, open for him."
"6460","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm narrating from Safina. Narrated Ubayd al-Ijli, Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Qarm narrated to us from Fitr ibn Khalifa from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm from Safina, freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a bird, and said: O Allah, bring me the most beloved of Your creation to You to eat with me of this bird. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, befriend - [text ends here]."
"6461","Abu Rayhana Abd Allah ibn Matar narrating from Safina. Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us from Abu Rayhana from Safina: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform ablution with a mudd and bathe with a sa'."
"6462","Said ibn Jumhan narrating from Safina. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abu Nuaym narrated to us; and Abu Khalifa narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us; and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us; they said: Hashraj ibn Nubata narrated to us, Said ibn Jumhan narrated to me, saying: I asked Safina about his name, and he said: I shall tell you my name; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me Safina. I said: Why did he name you Safina? He said: He went out with his companions, and their baggage became heavy upon them. He said: Spread your cloak. I spread it, and he placed their baggage in it, then carried it upon me and said: Carry, for you are but a ship. He said: Had I carried that day the load of a camel, or two camels, or five wasqs, it would not have been heavy upon me."
"6463","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, both said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey; whenever a man grew weary he would cast upon me his sword and shield, until I carried a great deal. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are Safina."
"6464","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, my father Muhammad ibn Abi Shayba narrated to me from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey; each of his companions whose baggage became heavy upon him would load it upon me, until I carried a great deal of that. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me and said: You are but Safina."
"6465","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Hashraj ibn Nubata narrated to us, Said ibn Jumhan narrated to me, Safina narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The caliphate among my umma is thirty years, then kingship after that. Then Safina said to me: Count; I counted the caliphate of Abu Bakr, the caliphate of Umar, the caliphate of Uthman, and the caliphate of Ali (may Allah be pleased with them), and found it thirty."
"6466","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The caliphate after me among my umma is thirty years, then kingship."
"6467","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, Sawwar ibn Abd Allah ibn Sawwar al-Anbari narrated to us, Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The caliphate of prophethood is thirty years, then Allah gives to whom He wills - or he said: His dominion to whom He wills. Said said: Abu Bakr held two years, Umar ten, Uthman twelve, and Ali six."
"6468","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abu Nuaym narrated to us; and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us; he said: Hashraj ibn Nubata narrated to us, Said ibn Jumhan narrated to us from Safina, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: There was no prophet before me but he warned his umma of the Dajjal; he is blind in his left eye, with a thick growth over his right eye, and between his eyes is written: disbeliever. He will emerge with two valleys, one a paradise and the other a fire; his paradise is a fire and his fire is a paradise. With him are two angels resembling two prophets among the prophets, one on his right and the other on his left. That is the trial of the people. He will say: Am I not your lord? I give life and cause death? One of the two angels will say: You have lied - and none of the people will hear him except his companion, who will say to him: You have spoken the truth - and the people will hear him and think he affirmed the Dajjal, and that is a trial. Then he will travel until he comes to Madina, but he will not be permitted therein. He will say: This is the town of that man. Then he will travel until he comes to al-Sham, and Allah Almighty will destroy him at the pass of Afiq."
"6469","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Naila al-Asbahani, Hudba ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina, that a man invited Ali, who prepared food for him; Fatima (may Allah be pleased with her) said: If only we would invite the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to eat with us. He sent a man to him; he came, took hold of the two doorposts, saw a figured curtain in a corner of the house, and turned back. Fatima (may Allah be pleased with her) said to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): Follow him and see what turned him back. He followed him and said: What turned you back, O Messenger of Allah? He said: It is not for me to enter an adorned house."
"6470","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us from Said ibn Jumhan from Safina, who said: I was a slave of Umm Salama; she said: I shall free you, on condition that you serve the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as long as you live. I said: Had you not stipulated it upon me, I would never have parted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as long as I lived. So she freed me, stipulating it upon me."
"6471","Of those named Suwayd. 633 - Suwayd ibn Muqarrin al-Muzani. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri from Salama ibn Kuhayl from Muawiya ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin from Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, who said: We, the sons of Muqarrin, were seven in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with a servant girl, our only one; one of us slapped her, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Free her. I said: We have no servant but her, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She will serve you until you can do without her, then set her free."
"6472","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, Ubayd Allah ibn Muadh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us from Salama ibn Kuhayl from Muawiya ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin from Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, who said: We, the sons of Muqarrin, were seven in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with a servant girl, our only one; one of us slapped her, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Free her. I said: We have no servant but her, O Messenger of Allah? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She will serve you until you can do without her, then set her free."
"6473","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, Ubayd Allah ibn Muadh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us from Salama from Muawiya ibn Suwayd from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6474","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us from Mansur and Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman from Hilal ibn Yasaf, who said: We were staying in the house of Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, when an old man with sharp temper and ignorance, with a slave girl, slapped her face. I never saw Suwayd angrier than that day. Then he said: Could you do nothing but the noble part of her face? I saw myself as the seventh of seven sons of Muqarrin, with no servant but one servant girl; the youngest of us slapped her face, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to free her."
"6475","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil, Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us from Husayn from Hilal ibn Yasaf, who said: We were in the house of Suwayd ibn Muqarrin when a slave girl of his came out and said something to a man - I do not know what it was - and he slapped her. Suwayd ibn Muqarrin saw that and said: You slapped her face; I saw myself as the seventh of seven with only one servant; a man slapped him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to free him."
"6476","Narrated Yusuf al-Qadi, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir said to me: What is your name? He said: I said: Shu'ba. Then Abu Shu'ba narrated to me from Suwayd ibn Muqarrin al-Muzani, who said: He saw a man slap a slave boy of his, and said: Do you not know that the face is inviolable? I saw myself as the seventh of seven brothers in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with only one servant; one of us slapped him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to free him."
"6477","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, Said ibn Amr al-Ash'athi narrated to us, Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us from Mutarrif ibn Tarif from Sawada ibn Abi al-Ja'd, who said: I was sitting with Suwayd ibn Muqarrin when he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is killed defending against injustice is a martyr."
"6478","634 - Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man al-Ansari. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Uyayna and Ibn Abi Sabra from Yahya ibn Said from Bushayr ibn Yasar from Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khaybar until we reached al-Sahba, between which and Khaybar is an evening's journey; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the people for their provisions, and nothing was brought but sawiq. He chewed and we chewed, then he stood, rinsed his mouth, then prayed Zuhr and Asr."
"6479","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, al-Qanabi narrated to us from Malik; and Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Malik informed us; and Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-San'ani narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, Malik narrated to me from Yahya ibn Said from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man informed him that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Khaybar; when we were at al-Sahba, which is at the nearer part of Khaybar, he alighted and prayed Asr, then called for provisions; nothing was brought but sawiq, so he ordered it to be moistened; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate and we ate. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose for Maghrib, rinsed his mouth and we rinsed ours, then prayed without performing ablution. The wording is that of the hadith of al-Qanabi."
"6480","Narrated Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Husayn, Yahya ibn Abd Allah al-Bablutti narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to me from Bushayr ibn Yasar, Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man narrated to me, saying: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Khaybar; when we were at al-Sahba and had prayed Asr, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for the people's provisions; they brought sawiq, they ate and drank. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose for Maghrib prayer, rinsed his mouth and the people rinsed, and they prayed without doing more than that."
"6481","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, Bushayr ibn Yasar al-Ansari, freedman of the Ansar, narrated to me that Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man, of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him that they went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khaybar; he said: When we were at al-Sahba, an evening's journey from Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for food; nothing was found but sawiq, so we ate, then drank water over it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rinsed his mouth, stood, and prayed."
"6482","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yahya ibn Said narrated to me from Bushayr ibn Yasar from Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Khaybar and alighted at a stop near it called al-Sahba; he called his companions for what remained of their provisions; they brought him nothing but sawiq, so he mixed it and ate, then rinsed his mouth, stood, prayed Maghrib, and did not perform ablution."
"6483","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, my father narrated to me, Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us from Bushayr ibn Yasar from Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khaybar… and he mentioned something similar."
"6484","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us from Yahya ibn Said from Bushayr ibn Yasar from Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"6485","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us from Bushayr ibn Yasar from Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year he raided Khaybar, and he mentioned the same."
"6486","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari narrated to us from Bushayr ibn Yasar from Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)… and he mentioned similar."
"6487","635 - Suwayd ibn Hanzala. Narrated Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la from his grandmother from her father Suwayd ibn Hanzala, who said: We went out intending to meet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with us was Wa'il ibn Hujr; an enemy of his seized him, and the people were scrupulous about swearing oaths, but I swore that he was my brother, so he was released. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: The people were scrupulous about swearing, but I swore that he was my brother. He said: You spoke the truth; the Muslim is the brother of the Muslim."
"6488","Narrated Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la from his grandmother from her father Suwayd ibn Hanzala, who said: We went out intending to meet the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with us was Wa'il ibn Hujr; an enemy of his seized him, and the people were scrupulous about swearing oaths, but I swore that he was my brother, so they released him. We came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and I informed him, and he said: You spoke the truth; the Muslim is the brother of the Muslim."
"6489","636 - Suwayd ibn Qays al-Abdi, nicknamed Abu Marhab. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abu Nuaym narrated to us; and Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us; both said: Sufyan narrated to us from Simak ibn Harb, Suwayd ibn Qays informed me, saying: Makhrafa al-Abdi and I brought cloth from Hajar and came with it to Makka; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us at Mina and bought trousers from us, and there was a weigher who weighed for a fee. He said: O weigher, weigh and give extra."
"6490","638 - Suwayd Abu Uqba. Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir ibn al-Bakhtari, al-Himsi narrated to us, Bishr ibn Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza narrated to us, my father narrated to me from al-Zuhri, Uqba ibn Suwayd informed me from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Uhud is a mountain that loves us and we love it."
"6491","Narrated Musa ibn Harun and Abd Allah ibn Najiya, both said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ma'n ibn Muhammad ibn Nadla al-Ghifari narrated to us, that he heard Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman narrate from Uqba ibn Suwayd from his father, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about sheep? He said: It is for you, or for your brother, or for the wolf. He said: And I asked him about camels? When he grew angry it was known by the redness of his cheeks, and he said: What have you to do with it? It has its water-skin and its sandals; it comes to water and goes forth to pasture; leave it until it meets its master. And I asked him about lost property? He said: Announce it, then secure its string and its container; if its seeker comes, give it to him, otherwise do with it as you wish."
"6492","Narrated Abu Zur'a al-Dimashqi, Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza informed us from al-Zuhri, who said: Uqba ibn Suwayd informed me that he heard his father, who was of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from an expedition; when Uhud appeared to us, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Most Great; Uhud is a mountain that loves us and we love it."
"6493","639 - Suwayd ibn Hubayra. Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us from Abu Na'ama al-Adawi, Muslim ibn Budayl narrated to me from Iyas ibn Zuhayr Abu Talha from Suwayd ibn Hubayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best wealth of a man is a well-tended date-palm row or a well-kept mare."
"6494","Narrated Idris ibn Ja'far al-Attar, Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, Abu Na'ama narrated to us from Muslim ibn Budayl from Iyas ibn Zuhayr from Suwayd ibn Hubayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best wealth of a man is a well-kept mare or a well-tended date-palm row."
"6495","640 - Suwayd Abu Abd Allah al-Alhani. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti, Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Yazid ibn Said ibn Dhi Uswan narrated to us from Utba ibn Abi Hakim from Abd Allah ibn Suwayd al-Alhani, a clan of the Ash'ariyyin, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - or one who heard him narrated to me - say: Allah has made this tribe of Lakhm and Judham a source of aid in al-Sham through backs and udders, just as He made Yusuf in Egypt a source of aid for its people."
"6496","641 - Suwayd ibn Ghafala, a mukhadram. Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us from Hilal ibn Khabbab from Maysara Abu Salih from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, who said: I traveled - or one who traveled with the collector of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me - and in the covenant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: Do not take from a milch animal, do not combine what is separate, and do not separate what is combined."
"6497","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Bukayr al-Tayalisi al-Basri, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us from Uthman ibn Abi Zar'a from Abu Layla al-Kindi from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, who said: The collector of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us; I took his hand and he took my hand, and I read in his covenant: Do not combine what is separate, nor separate what is combined, out of fear of charity. A man came to him with a huge she-camel, and he refused to take it; then he came to him with another below it, and he refused to take it. Then he said: What land will carry me, and what sky will shade me, if I come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having taken the best property of a Muslim man?"
"6498","Of those named Sawad. 642 - Sawad ibn Qarib al-Sadusi. Narrated Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri, Bishr ibn Hujr al-Shami narrated to us, Ali ibn Mansur al-Anbari narrated to us from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Waqqasi from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, who said: While Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was sitting in the mosque, a man passed at the rear of the mosque; a man said: O Commander of the Faithful, do you know this passerby? He said: No; who is he? He said: This is Sawad ibn Qarib, a man of the people of Yemen with honor and standing among them; he is the one to whom his familiar spirit brought news of the advent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Bring him to me. He was summoned to him. He said: Are you Sawad ibn Qarib? He said: Yes. He said: Are you the one to whom your familiar spirit brought news of the advent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. He said: Are you still upon your soothsaying as before? He became severely angry and said: O Commander of the Faithful, none has confronted me with this since I accepted Islam. Umar said: Glory be to Allah; what we were upon of polytheism was greater than your soothsaying. Inform me of your familiar spirit's coming to you with news of the advent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Yes, O Commander of the Faithful; one night, between sleep and wakefulness, my familiar spirit came to me, struck me with his foot, and said: Rise, O Sawad ibn Qarib; understand and comprehend, if you can comprehend; a Messenger has been sent from Luayy ibn Ghalib calling to Allah Almighty and to His worship. Then he began reciting: The camels with their saddles. He said: I paid no heed to his words, and said: Let me sleep, for I am drowsy this evening. On the second night he came to me, struck me with his foot, and said: Did I not tell you, O Sawad ibn Qarib, rise, understand and comprehend, if you can comprehend; a Messenger has been sent from Luayy ibn Ghalib calling to Allah Almighty and to His worship. Then the jinni began reciting: The camels. He said: I paid no heed to his words. On the third night he came to me, struck me with his foot, and said: Did I not tell you, O Sawad ibn Qarib, understand and comprehend, if you can comprehend; a Messenger has been sent from Luayy ibn Ghalib calling to Allah Almighty and to His worship. Then the jinni began reciting: The camels with their saddles. Love of Islam fell into my heart, and I desired it. In the morning I saddled my mount and set out toward Makka; partway I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had emigrated to Madina. I came to Madina and asked about the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I was told: In the mosque. I reached the mosque, hobbled my she-camel, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with people around him. I said: Hear my words, O Messenger of Allah. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Bring him near, bring him near. He kept drawing me close until I was before him. He said: Come, inform me of your familiar spirit's coming to you. I said: Najiyyu had'i al-dhi'lib al-wajna' al-sabasib. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions rejoiced at my Islam with intense joy, until it was seen in their faces. He said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) leapt to him and embraced him, saying: I had loved to hear this from you."
"6499","Narrated Muhammad ibn Harun ibn Muhammad ibn Bakkar ibn Bilal al-Dimashqi, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Ya'la ibn Ata narrated to us, Abu Ma'mar Abbad ibn Abd al-Samad narrated to us, saying: I heard Said ibn Jubayr say: Sawad ibn Qarib al-Azdi informed me, saying: I was sleeping upon a mountain of the Sarat mountains when a comer came, struck me with his foot, and said: Rise, O Sawad ibn Qarib; a Messenger has come to you from Luayy ibn Ghalib. I sat upright, and he turned away reciting: The camels with their saddles. He said: Then I returned and slept; he came to me, struck me with his foot, and said: Rise, O Sawad ibn Qarib; a Messenger has come to you from Luayy ibn Ghalib. He turned away reciting: The camels with their packsaddles. He said: Then I returned and slept; he came to me, struck me with his foot, and said: Rise, O Sawad ibn Qarib; a Messenger has come to you from Luayy ibn Ghalib. I sat upright, and he turned away reciting: The camels. He said: In the morning I mounted a camel of mine until I came to Makka, and there the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had appeared; I informed him of the news and followed him."
"6500","643 - Sawad ibn Amr al-Ansari. Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Shuayb al-Simsar, al-Hasan ibn Bishr al-Bajali narrated to us, al-Mu'afa ibn Imran narrated to us from Hisham ibn Hassan from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Sawad ibn Amr al-Ansari, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man to whom beauty has been made beloved, and I have been given of it what you see; I do not like anyone to surpass me even in the strap of my sandal - or he said: the thong of my sandal - is that of arrogance? He said: No. I said: What is arrogance, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Whoever belittles the truth and despises people."
"6501","Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar, al-Hasan ibn Qaza'a narrated to us, Asim ibn Hilal narrated to us from Ayyub from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Sawad ibn Amr al-Ansari, who said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man to whom beauty and comeliness have been made beloved, such that I do not like anyone to surpass me even by a sandal strap; is that of arrogance? He said: No; but arrogance is whoever despises people and rejects the truth."
"6502","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that Sawad ibn Amr was a handsome man who said: O Messenger of Allah, I have been given beauty and comeliness, and it has been made beloved to me; I do not like anyone to excel me even by a sandal strap; is it of arrogance? He said: No; but arrogance is whoever rejects the truth and despises people."
"6503","Of those named Sawada. 644 - Sawada ibn al-Rabi' al-Jarmi. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Mu'alla ibn Rashid al-Ammi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, Sulayman al-Jarmi narrated to us from Sawada ibn al-Rabi' al-Jarmi, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he granted me a she-camel and said to me: Stick to horses, for good is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection."
"6504","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad, Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, Sulayman al-Jarmi narrated to us from Sawada ibn al-Rabi', who said: I saw a ring upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6505","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Murajja ibn Raja narrated to us, Salm ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us from Sawada ibn al-Rabi', who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he granted me a she-camel. He said: When you return to your household, tell them to do their deeds well, order them to trim their nails, and not to scratch therewith the udders of their livestock when milking."
"6506","Of those named Sulayman. 645 - Sulayman ibn Surad al-Khuza'i, nicknamed Abu al-Mutarrif. Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqa, Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us from al-Haytham ibn Adi, who said: Sulayman ibn Surad died in the year sixty-five. Muhammad ibn Ali said, and it reached me: Sulayman ibn Surad al-Khuza'i set out with al-Musayyab ibn Najaba al-Fazari with four thousand; they encamped at al-Nukhayla seeking vengeance for the blood of al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him), under Sulayman ibn Surad, at the beginning of Rabi' al-Akhir of the year sixty-five. Then they marched to Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad, met his vanguard, fought, and Sulayman ibn Surad and Ibn Najaba were killed in the month of Rabi' al-Akhir."
"6507","What Sulayman ibn Surad transmitted. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us from Abu Ishaq, who said: I heard Sulayman ibn Surad al-Khuza'i say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Ahzab: Now we shall raid them and they shall not raid us."
"6508","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us from Abu Ishaq from Sulayman ibn Surad, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Ahzab: Today we shall raid them and they shall not raid us."
"6509","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, Ubayd ibn Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Abu Sinan al-Shaybani narrated to us from Abu Ishaq al-Sabi'i, who said: Khalid ibn Urfuta said to Sulayman ibn Surad - or Sulayman to Khalid ibn Urfuta: Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever is killed by his belly will not be punished in his grave? One of them said to his companion: Yes."
"6510","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to me, Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to me from Ismail ibn Muslim from Shimr ibn Atiyya from Sulayman ibn Surad, that a bedouin prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a bow; some people took it, and when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salam, the bedouin said: Where is the bow? Some people seemed to laugh, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not frighten a Muslim."
"6511","Narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani, my grandfather Ahmad ibn Abi Shuayb narrated to us, Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us from al-A'mash from Adi ibn Thabit from Sulayman ibn Surad, who said: Two men traded insults before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); one of them became intensely angry, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know a word which, had he said it, what he feels would have left him; had he said: I seek refuge with Allah from Satan, what he feels would have left him."
"6512","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, my father narrated to me, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us from al-A'mash from Adi ibn Thabit al-Ansari from Sulayman ibn Surad, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard two men disputing, one of them angry, his anger intensifying; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know a word which, had he said it, Satan would have left him. A man came to him and said: Say: I seek refuge with Allah from Satan."
"6513","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us from Abd al-Akram, a man of Kufa, from his father from Sulayman ibn Surad, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and stayed three nights, and we could not manage food - or: we could not. Abd Allah ibn Ahmad said: I mentioned this hadith to my father - may Allah have mercy on him - and he approved of it."
"6514","646 - Sulayman ibn Ukayma al-Laythi. Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Massisi, Said ibn Amr al-Sakuni al-Himsi narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Salama narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Abd Allah ibn Sulayman ibn Ukayma al-Laythi narrated to me from his father from his grandfather, who said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: May our fathers and mothers be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah; we hear hadith from you but cannot render it as we heard it? He said: If you do not declare unlawful lawful nor declare lawful unlawful, and you get the meaning right, there is no harm."
"6515","Of those named Sinan. 647 - Sinan ibn Sanna al-Aslami. Narrated Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani, Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Hurra from his uncle Hakim ibn Abi Hurra from Sinan ibn Sanna al-Aslami, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The grateful eater is like the patient faster."
"6516","648 - Sinan ibn Salama ibn al-Muhabbiq Abu Tarif al-Hudhali. Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna, Musaddad narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Hajjaj al-Ahwal narrated to us from Salama ibn Junada from Sinan ibn Salama, that a man of the Muhajirun gave in charity a large land of his to his mother; she died with no heir but him, so he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My mother so-and-so was of the dearest and most honored of people to me; I gave her in charity a large land of mine, and she died with no heir but me; how do you command me to deal with it? He said: Allah has made your reward due and returned your land to you; do with it as you wish."
"6517","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj al-Askari, Bishr ibn al-Walid al-Kindi narrated to us, Qaza'a ibn Suwayd narrated to us from al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj from Salama ibn Junada from Sinan ibn Salama, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I gave my mother a charity, and she has perished; what shall I do? He said: Allah has returned your land to you and accepted your charity."
"6518","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Fasawi, Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Adam narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: I heard Imran narrate from Khalid al-Ashajj from Sinan ibn Salama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon a dead young she-goat and said: It would not harm its owners had they benefited from its skin."
"6519","649 - Sinan ibn Wabara al-Juhani. Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar, Humayd ibn al-Rabi' al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Shaybani narrated to us from Kharija ibn Rafi' al-Juhani from his father from Sinan ibn Wabara al-Juhani, who said: We raided with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the expedition of al-Muraysi'; our watchword was: O victorious one, kill, kill."
"6520","650 - Sinan ibn Gharafa. Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abd al-Khaliq ibn Zayd ibn Waqid narrated to us from his father from Atiyya ibn Qays from Busr ibn Ubayd Allah from Sinan ibn Gharafa, who had companionship, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning a man who dies among women, and a woman who dies among men, with neither having a mahram: They are given tayammum and not washed."
"6521","651 - Sunayn Abu Jamila. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us from al-Zuhri, that a man came to his family having picked up a foundling; he went to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and mentioned it to him, and Umar said: Perhaps al-Ghuwayr means distress. The man said: It was picked up only while I was away. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) asked about him and he was praised well, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Its guardianship is yours, and its maintenance upon us from the public treasury."
"6522","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Malik informed us from Ibn Shihab, Abu Jamila narrated to me that he found a foundling in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he brought him with it, and Umar suspected him concerning him, but he was praised well. Umar said: He is free, his guardianship is yours, and his maintenance from the public treasury."
"6523","Narrated Abu Khalifa, Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, Juwayriya ibn Asma narrated to us from Malik ibn Anas from al-Zuhri, that Abu Jamila informed him - while we were sitting with Said ibn al-Musayyab - claiming that Abu Jamila had reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and was with him in the year of the Conquest."
"6524","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Ibn Shihab informed him that a man informed him that he had picked up an illegitimate child and brought him to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him); he said: Go and seek a wet nurse with Allah's wealth, and its guardianship is yours. Ibn Shihab said: The man who picked him up and brought him to Umar ibn al-Khattab informed me of that himself."
"6525","Of those named Simak. 652 - Simak ibn Kharasha Abu Dujana al-Ansari, a Badri martyred on the day of Yamama. Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, my father narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us from Abu al-Aswad from Urwa, concerning the naming of those who attended Badr of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'ida: Abu Dujana Simak ibn Aws ibn Kharasha ibn Lawdhan ibn Abd Wudd ibn Zayd ibn Tha'laba."
"6526","Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Fulayh, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab: ""Concerning the naming of those of the Ansar who were present at Badr, then of Banu Sa'idah: Abu Dujanah Simak ibn Kharashah, and he is the one who took the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud."""
"6527","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, narrated to us my father, narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, concerning the naming of those martyred on the day of Yamamah, of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Simak ibn Kharashah, and he is Abu Dujanah."
"6528","Narrated to us Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani, narrated to us Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Salamah, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: ""Concerning the naming of those martyred on the day of Yamamah, of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Simak ibn Kharashah, and he is Abu Dujanah."""
"6529","Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Fulayh, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab: ""Concerning the naming of those martyred on the day of Yamamah, of the Ansar, then of Banu Sa'idah: Simak ibn Kharashah, and he is Abu Dujanah."""
"6530","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Minjab ibn al-Harith, narrated to us Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Ali entered upon Fatimah on the day of Uhud and said: Take this sword, it is not blameworthy. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If you fought well, indeed Sahl ibn Hunayf and Abu Dujanah Simak ibn Kharashah fought well."""
"6531","Narrated to us Mas'adah ibn Sa'd al-Attar al-Makki and Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Anbar al-Basri, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir, and narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Qurmuti, of the descendants of Amir ibn Rabi'ah, narrated to us Uthman ibn Ya'qub al-Uthmani, they both said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi, from Khalid ibn Sulayman ibn Abd Allah ibn Khalid ibn Simak ibn Kharashah, from his father, from his grandfather, that Abu Dujanah, on the day of Uhud, marked himself with a red headband, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at him while he strutted in his walk between the two ranks and said: ""It is a walk that Allah hates except in this place."""
"6532","Those named Salit. 653 - Salit Abu Sulayman al-Ansari, a Badri. Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, narrated to us my father, narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah: ""Concerning the naming of those who were present at Badr of the Ansar, then of Banu Adi ibn al-Najjar: Salit ibn Qays ibn Amr ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Malik ibn Adi ibn Amir."""
"6533","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki, narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Yahya al-Madani, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Salit al-Ansari, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the Hijrah, with him Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, and Amir ibn Fuhayrah, the freed slave of Abu Bakr, and Ibn Urayqit guiding them on the way, he passed by Umm Ma'bad al-Khuza'iyyah, and she did not know him, so he said to her: ""O Umm Ma'bad, do you have any milk?"" She said: No, by Allah, and indeed the sheep are far away grazing. He said: ""Then what is this ewe that I see in the corner of the tent?"" She said: A ewe kept back by weakness from the flock. He said: ""Do you permit me to milk her?"" She said: By Allah, no male has ever mounted her, and she is at your disposal. So he wiped her back and her udder, then called for a vessel that would satisfy a group, and milked into it until he filled it, and gave his companions to drink, quenching after the first draught, then he milked into it again and filled it, and left it with her, and departed. When her husband came in the evening he said to her: O Umm Ma'bad, what is this milk, when there is no milch animal in the house and the sheep are far away grazing? She said: No, by Allah, except that a man passed by us, bright in appearance, handsome of face, with long lashes, intense blackness of the eyes, and a huskiness in his voice, a branch between two branches, neither marred by tallness nor despised for shortness, no corpulence over him, nor slightness disfiguring him, as if his neck were a jug of silver; when he spoke, splendour was upon him, and when he was silent, dignity was upon him; his speech like arranged beads, the most handsome of his companions to look at and the best of them in face, surrounded, not refuted, with companions crowding around him; when he commanded they hastened to his command, and when he forbade they stopped at his prohibition. He said: This is the description of the companion of Quraysh; had I seen him I would have followed him, and I would strive to do so. And they did not know in Makkah where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had headed, until they heard a caller calling out on Abu Qubays:"
"6534","654 - Salit ibn Thabit ibn Waqsh al-Ansari, martyred with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud. Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, narrated to me my father, narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah: ""Concerning the naming of those martyred on the day of Uhud with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of the Ansar, then of Banu al-Nabt: Salit ibn Thabit ibn Waqsh."""
"6535","Those named Sabrah. 655 - Sabrah ibn Ma'bad ibn Awsajah al-Juhani, and there is disagreement concerning his lineage. Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Nasr Abu Ja'far al-Tirmidhi, narrated to us al-Harith ibn Ma'bad, from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah ibn Ma'bad ibn Amr ibn Suharah ibn Jurayj ibn Dhuhl ibn Zayd ibn Juhaynah ibn Quda'ah."
"6536","Al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah from his father. Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah al-Juhani, that his father informed him that they set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage until they alighted at Usfan, entering ihram for Hajj, and a man of Banu Mudlij stood up to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), called Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum, and said: O Messenger of Allah, decree for us the decree of a people as if born today. He said: ""Allah, mighty and majestic, has admitted for you into this Hajj of yours an Umrah; so when you arrive, whoever circumambulates the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah has become released, except one who has a sacrificial animal with him."" When we became released he said: ""Enjoy yourselves with these women,"" and enjoyment in our view is marriage. So we proposed that to the women, but they refused unless they fixed a term between us and them. That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: ""Do so."" So I set out, I and a cousin of mine, I having a cloak and he having a cloak, his cloak being better than mine, while I was younger than him. We came to a woman; his cloak pleased her, and my youthfulness pleased her. Then her affair ended in her saying: Hand over your cloak. And the term between me and her was ten. So I spent the night with her, then in the morning I went out to the mosque, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing between the Corner and the Door, saying: ""O people, I had permitted you enjoyment of these women; yet Allah, mighty and majestic, has forbidden that until the Day of Resurrection. So whoever has any of them with him, let him release her way, and do not take back anything of what you gave them."""
"6537","Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Madinah in the Farewell Pilgrimage, until when we were at Usfan, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Umrah has entered into Hajj until the Day of Resurrection."" So Suraqah ibn Malik said to him: O Messenger of Allah, teach us the teaching of a people as if born today: is this Umrah of ours for this year of ours, or forever? He said: ""No, rather forever."" When we arrived in Makkah we circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then he commanded us to temporary marriage with women. So we returned to him and said: They have refused except for a named term. He said: ""Then do so."" So I set out, I and a companion of mine, I wearing a cloak and he wearing a cloak, and we entered upon a woman and offered ourselves to her. She kept looking at my companion's cloak, seeing it better than my cloak, and looking at me, seeing me younger than him. She said: A cloak in place of a cloak, and she chose me. So I married her for my cloak and spent that night with her. In the morning I went to the mosque, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the pulpit delivering a sermon, and I heard him saying: ""Whoever has married a woman for a term, let him give her what he named for her, and let him not take back anything of what he gave her, and let him part from her, for Allah, mighty and majestic, has forbidden her to you until the Day of Resurrection."""
"6538","Narrated to us Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi, narrated to us Abu Hudhayfah, narrated to us Sufyan, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"6539","Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, informed us Abu Ubayd, narrated to us Bishr ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage in the Farewell Pilgrimage."
"6540","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal, narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd, narrated to us Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Sufyan ibn Sa'id, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like the hadith of Ma'mar."
"6541","Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Muslim ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us Shu'bah, from Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted temporary marriage, then after three days I came to him while he was forbidding it, prohibiting it most severely."
"6542","Narrated to us Bishr ibn Musa, narrated to us al-Humaydi, narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had permitted us temporary marriage. When we arrived in Makkah, I and a cousin of mine, we came to a young girl, I having a cloak and my cousin having a cloak, my cousin's cloak being better than mine, while I was younger than my cousin. She kept looking at me, saying: A cloak like a cloak, and she chose me. So I gave her my cloak, then I stayed with her as Allah willed. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him standing between the Door and Zamzam, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""We had permitted you this temporary marriage; so whoever has any of these women with him, let him release her, for Allah, mighty and majestic, has forbidden it until the Day of Resurrection, and do not take back anything of what you gave them."""
"6543","Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Abdah ibn Sulayman, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: We set out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage, and he mentioned like the hadith of Abu Nu'aym in full."
"6544","Narrated to us Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj, narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr, narrated to me al-Layth, (ha) and narrated to us Yusuf al-Qadi, narrated to us Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, narrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa'd, narrated to me al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah al-Juhani, from his father Sabrah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted us temporary marriage. So I set out, I and a man, to a woman of Banu Amir, as if she were a tall young she-camel, and we offered ourselves to her. She said: What will you give us? I said: My cloak, and my companion said: My cloak; my companion's cloak was better than mine, while I was younger than him. When she looked at my companion's cloak it pleased her, and when she looked at me I pleased her. Then she said: You and your cloak suffice me. So I stayed with her three days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever has with him any of these women whom he enjoys, let him release her way,"" and the wording is that of the hadith of Yahya ibn Bukayr."
"6545","Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Musaddad, narrated to us al-Mu'tamir, he said: I heard Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrating from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah on the day of the Conquest, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them temporary marriage with women. So I set out, I and a cousin of mine, I having a worn cloak and my cousin having a new fresh cloak, I being handsome, excelling my cousin in beauty, my cousin being below me in beauty. We set out until when we were at the upper part of Makkah or its lower part, a girl of Banu Amir ibn Sa'sa'ah met us, like a young she-camel. We said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has commanded us, or has permitted us, to enjoy women. So would you have one of us?"" She said: Has he done so? We said: Yes. She said: Yes. So we spread our two cloaks; she kept looking at the two cloaks, then looking at me and at my cousin. When my cousin saw her looking at his garment, he said: This cloak of mine is new and fresh, while my cousin's cloak is worn, used. And the girl said: And this one's cloak is not bad. He repeated that statement once or twice, then she chose me. Then we did not leave Makkah until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it."
"6546","Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Musaddad, narrated to us Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like it."
"6547","Narrated to us Mas'adah ibn Sa'd al-Attar al-Makki, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Mansur, (ha), and narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Harmalah ibn Yahya, they both said: narrated to us Ibn Wahb, informed me Amr ibn al-Harith, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah al-Juhani, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted us temporary marriage in the year of the Conquest. So I set out, I and another man, to a young woman like a tall young she-camel, to enjoy her. We sat before her, I wearing a cloak and he wearing a cloak. We spoke to her and dowered her with our two cloaks; I was younger than him, while his cloak was better than mine. She kept looking at me once and at his cloak once, then she accepted me, so I married her and stayed with her three days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it, so I parted from her, or something like this."
"6548","Narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, narrated to us Salamah ibn Shabib, narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn A'yan, narrated to us Ma'qil ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, informed me al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage, saying: ""It is unlawful from this day of ours until the Day of Resurrection, and whoever gave anything let him not take it back."""
"6549","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah al-Asqalani, narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Sari, narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn A'yan al-Harrani, narrated to us Ma'qil ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage."
"6550","Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Ali ibn al-Madini, narrated to us Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim, from his father, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage on the day of the Conquest."
"6551","Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage with women."
"6552","Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ulayyah, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage with women on the day of the Conquest."
"6553","Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Ali ibn al-Madini, (ha) and narrated to us Bishr ibn Musa, narrated to us al-Humaydi, they both said: narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage."
"6554","Narrated to us Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli, narrated to us Salamah ibn Rawh, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, narrated to me al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah al-Juhani, that his father informed him, he said: I contracted temporary marriage in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a woman of Banu Amir for two red cloaks, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us temporary marriage with women."
"6555","Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Musaddad, narrated to us Abd al-Warith, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from al-Zuhri, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it in the Farewell Pilgrimage."
"6556","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah, narrated to us Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, from Bahr al-Saqqa, from al-Zuhri, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage with women on the day of the conquest of Makkah."
"6557","Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Musaffa, narrated to us Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: When it was the day of the conquest of Makkah and the people entered it, a man of Qays had agreed with a woman, giving her two garments, while I was more handsome in face than him, and I had a garment with me. So I said to her: I will give you this garment, that I may enjoy you. So she left the Qaysi and said: Yes. I promised her I would return to her. Then I entered the mosque, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding it. So I returned and took my garment from her."
"6558","Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Musaddad, (ha) and narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, narrated to us Hudbah ibn Khalid, they both said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, he said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage with women at the time of the Conquest. I said: Who narrated to you? He said: A man narrated to me, from his father."
"6559","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah, narrated to us Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, from Abu Hanifah, from Yunus, from his father, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father Sabrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage with women on the day of the conquest of Makkah."
"6560","Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Nu'aym ibn Hammad, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to enjoy ourselves at the conquest of Makkah, so we enjoyed women, then he forbade us it."
"6561","Narrated to us Abu Hanifah Muhammad ibn Hanifah al-Wasiti, narrated to us my uncle Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Mahan ibn Abi Hanifah, narrated to us my father, narrated to us Salamah ibn Salih, from Abu Farwah, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade temporary marriage with women."
"6562","Narrated to us Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us Nu'aym ibn Hammad, (ha) and narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani, they both said: narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Let one of you screen himself in his prayer, even with an arrow."""
"6563","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, narrated to me my father, narrated to us Harmalah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, narrated to me my uncle Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather Sabrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Let one of you screen himself in his prayer, even with an arrow."""
"6564","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki, narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib, narrated to us Sabrah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his uncle Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi', from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like it."
"6565","Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like it."
"6566","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ukbari, narrated to us al-Humaydi, narrated to us Harmalah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, narrated to me my uncle Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi', from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Pray in the resting places of sheep, and do not pray in the resting places of camels."""
"6567","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki, narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd, narrated to us Sabrah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah ibn Ma'bad al-Juhani, from his uncle Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like it."
"6568","Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab, narrated to us Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like it."
"6569","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani, narrated to us my father, (ha) and narrated to us Khalaf ibn Amr al-Amiri, narrated to us al-Humaydi, they both said: narrated to us Harmalah ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to me my uncle Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Teach the boy prayer at seven years, and strike him for it at ten."""
"6570","Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The boy is commanded to pray when he reaches seven years; when he reaches ten, strike him for it."""
"6571","Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam, narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubab, narrated to us Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, narrated to me my father, from my grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When the boy reaches seven years, command him to pray; when he reaches ten, strike him for it."""
"6572","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki, narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib, narrated to us Sabrah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his uncle Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi', from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like it."
"6573","Narrated to us Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari, narrated to us al-Humaydi, narrated to us Harmalah ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to me my father, from my grandfather, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted at al-Hijr, he said: ""Do not drink anything of their water."" Some of them had kneaded dough and mixed hays, so they threw it away."
"6574","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki, narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd, narrated to us Sabrah ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, that his father narrated to him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his companions when he alighted at al-Hijr: ""Whoever has kneaded anything from this, meaning their well, let him throw it away."" So the owner of dough threw away his dough, and the owner of hays his hays."
"6575","Narrated to us Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani, narrated to us Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli, narrated to us Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his companions when he departed from al-Hijr, and he mentioned similar to it."
"6576","Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not pray in the resting places of camels, and pray in the resting place of sheep."""
"6577","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Nusayr al-Asbahani, narrated to us Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Shadhakuni, narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Ubayd al-Darawardi, from his father, from al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: I was present with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day saying: ""Whoever is here of Ma'add, let him stand."" So Amr ibn Murrah al-Juhani stood, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: ""Sit down,"" until he did that three times. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Quda'ah ibn Malik ibn Himyar."""
"6578","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and Abdan ibn Ahmad, they both said: narrated to us Hisham ibn Ammar, narrated to us Sabrah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Sabrah, from his father, from his grandfather, in marfu' form, he said: A cloud formed, and the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We hoped this cloud would rain upon us. He said: ""This is a cloud commanded to rain upon, meaning the valley."""
"6579","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki, narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Humayd, narrated to us Sabrah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah, that his father narrated to him, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a cloud and said: O Messenger of Allah, we hoped this cloud would rain upon us. He said: ""It was commanded to rain upon Balil,"" meaning a valley called Balil."
"6580","656 - Sabrah ibn Fatik al-Asadi, brother of Khuraym. Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and Abdan ibn Ahmad, they both said: narrated to us Hisham ibn Ammar, narrated to us Mu'awiyah ibn Yahya, narrated to us al-Zubaydi, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Sabrah ibn Fatik al-Asadi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The Balance is in the Hand of Allah; He raises peoples and lowers a people, and the heart of the son of Adam is between two fingers of the fingers of the Most Merciful; if He wills He causes it to deviate, and if He wills He keeps it upright."""
"6581","657 - Sabrah ibn Abi Fakih. Narrated to us Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Abd Allah ibn Najiyah, they both said: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Fudayl, from Musa al-Thaqafi Abu Ja'far, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Sabrah ibn Abi Fakih, and he was of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Satan sat for the son of Adam on all his paths; so he sat for him on the path of Islam and said: Will you accept Islam and leave your religion and the religion of your fathers? Then he sat for him on the path of Hijrah and said: Will you emigrate and leave your birthplace, to be like a horse in its tether? Then he sat for him on the path of jihad and said: Will you fight jihad? Then you will be killed, your wife will be married, and your wealth divided?"" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever does that, Allah guarantees him Paradise."""
"6582","658 - Sabrah ibn Abi Sabrah al-Ju'fi, grandfather of Khaythamah ibn Abd al-Rahman. Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, they both said: narrated to us Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from al-Hajjaj, from Umayr ibn Sa'id, from Sabrah ibn Abi Sabrah, that his father came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: ""What are your children?"" He said: Abd al-Uzza and Sabrah and al-Harith. He said: ""Do not name him Abd al-Uzza,"" so he named him Abd Allah. Then he said: ""The best of names are Abd Allah and Abd al-Rahman."" And he supplicated for him and his child, and they remained in honour until today. Abu Muhammad al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal said: Khaythamah ibn Abd al-Rahman is of them."
"6583","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Yahya al-Himmani, (ha) and narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Najiyah, narrated to us Abu Ma'mar al-Qati'i, they both said: narrated to us Abbad ibn al-Awwam, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Umayr ibn Sa'id, from Sabrah ibn Abi Sabrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"6584","659 - Those named Suraqah. Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum al-Mudliji; he used to dwell in the vicinity of Madinah. What Suraqah ibn Malik narrated. Ibn Abbas from Suraqah ibn Malik. Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Abu Kurayb, narrated to us Firdaws al-Ash'ari, narrated to us Mas'ud ibn Sulayman, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, from Suraqah ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Umrah has entered into Hajj."""
"6585","Jabir ibn Abd Allah from Suraqah ibn Malik. Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami, (ha) and narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Hammad ibn Fadalah al-Sayrafi al-Basri, narrated to us Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali, they both said: narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Habib ibn Nudbah, narrated to us Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Suraqah ibn Malik, he said: O Messenger of Allah, inform us about this Umrah of ours: is it for this year of ours, or forever? He said: ""No, rather forever; Umrah has entered into Hajj until the Day of Resurrection."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform us about our religion as if we look at it: by what the pens have run with and the decrees established? Or for a matter we begin anew? He said: ""Rather by what the pens have run with and the decrees established."" He said: Then for what is action, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Act, for each is made easy for what he was created for."""
"6586","Narrated to us Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, narrated to us Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, narrated to us Zuhayr, narrated to us Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering ihram, even the women and children. When we arrived in Makkah we circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: ""Whoever has no sacrificial animal with him, let him release."" We said: Which release? He said: ""The full release."" So we approached women, wore clothes, and touched perfume. When it was the day of Tarwiyah we entered ihram for Hajj, and the first circumambulation between al-Safa and al-Marwah sufficed us. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to share in camels and cattle, every seven of us in a badanah. Then Suraqah ibn Malik came and said: O Messenger of Allah, clarify for us our religion as if we were created just now? Tell us about this Umrah of ours: is it for this year of ours, or forever? He said: ""Rather forever."""
"6587","Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Sahl ibn Uthman, narrated to us al-Muharibi, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we entered ihram for Hajj from Dhu al-Hulayfah, and we entered ihram on behalf of the children, and circumambulated on their behalf, and performed sa'y on their behalf. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us, so we released the full release, until when it was the evening of Tarwiyah we entered ihram for Hajj from al-Batha. And he did not mention the story of Suraqah ibn Malik."
"6588","Narrated to us Bishr ibn Musa, narrated to us Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri, narrated to us Abu Hanifah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum al-Mudliji said: O Messenger of Allah, inform us about this religion of ours, as if we were created for it just now? In what do we act? In something in which the decrees are established and the pens have run, or in a matter begun anew? He said: ""Rather in what the pens are fixed."" Suraqah said: Then for what is action, O Messenger of Allah? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Act, for every worker is made easy for what he was created for."" And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited this verse: ""As for he who gives and fears Allah, and believes in the best reward, We shall ease him to ease; and as for he who withholds and deems himself free of need, and denies the best reward, We shall ease him to hardship."""
"6589","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and Ubayd ibn Ghannam, they both said: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us Ali ibn Hashim, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, narrate to us about this Umrah of ours: is it for this year of ours or forever? He said: ""Rather forever."" He said: O Messenger of Allah, inform us about our deeds, as if we were created just now: something the Book has established and the decrees have run with? Or something we begin anew? He said: ""No, rather something the Book has established and the decrees have run with."" He said: O Messenger of Allah, then for what is action? He said: ""Act, for each is made easy for what he was created for."""
"6590","Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, narrated to us Abu al-Mu'afa al-Harrani, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Salamah, from Abu Abd al-Rahim, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering ihram for Hajj. We arrived in Makkah and circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us and said: ""Whoever of you has not driven a sacrificial animal, let him release, and make it an Umrah."" We said: What release, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""The full release."" So we approached women, wore clothes, and perfumed ourselves with perfume. Then people said: Release, while between us and Arafah are four days? That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood among us as if angry and said: ""By Allah, you know that I am the most God-fearing of you; had I known you would say that, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal. So respond to what you are commanded."" Then Suraqah ibn Malik stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, this Umrah of ours which you commanded us: is it for this year of it or forever? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Rather forever."" Then Suraqah said: Inform us, O Messenger of Allah, about our religion, as if we were created now: is it in something the pens have dried upon and the decrees have run with, or in what is begun anew? He said: ""Rather in what the pens have dried upon and the decrees have run with."" Then Suraqah said: Then for what is action, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Act, for every worker is made easy."" Then he recited: ""As for he who gives and fears Allah, and believes in the best reward, We shall ease him to ease; and as for he who withholds and deems himself free of need, and denies the best reward, We shall ease him to hardship."""
"6591","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Rashdin, narrated to us Ahmad ibn Salih, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, narrated to me Amr ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Suraqah ibn Malik said: O Messenger of Allah, do we act for a matter already concluded, or for a matter we begin anew? He said: ""For a matter already concluded."" Then Suraqah said: Then for what is action? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every worker is made easy."""
"6592","Narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi, narrated to us Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli, he said: I read to Ma'qil ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as pilgrims, wanting only Hajj, intending nothing else, until when he was at Sarif, A'ishah menstruated. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her while she wept, and said: ""What makes you weep?"" She said: Affliction befell me. He said: ""You are but of the daughters of Adam; befalls you what befalls them."" He said: So we arrived in Makkah with four nights passed of Dhu al-Hijjah, and we circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwah as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us. Then we discussed among ourselves and said: We set out as pilgrims, wanting only Hajj, intending nothing else, yet when only four nights remain between us and Arafat, our male organs drip semen from women? That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood delivering a sermon and said: ""Umrah has entered into Hajj; had I faced of my affair what I now look back on, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal, and were it not for the sacrificial animal I would have released. So whoever has no sacrificial animal with him, let him release."" Then Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum al-Mudliji said: O Messenger of Allah, narrate to us the narration of a people as if born today: is this Umrah of ours for this year of ours, or forever? He said: ""Forever."" So we came to Arafah. When it was time to depart, A'ishah said: O Messenger of Allah, you have circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, while I did not circumambulate? He said: ""You have like what the people have."" She said: Yet I feel in myself? He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped for her at the upper valley of Makkah, and mounted Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr behind her until she came to al-Tan'im."
"6593","Narrated to us Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi, narrated to us Asim ibn Ali, narrated to us al-Rabi' ibn Subayh, from Ata, from Jabir, he said: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the morning of four passed of Dhu al-Hijjah, entering ihram for Hajj, all of us. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us; we circumambulated the House, prayed, and performed sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwah. Then he commanded us, so we shortened, then said: ""Release."" We said: O Messenger of Allah, what release? He said: ""The release of what becomes lawful for the lawful of women and perfume."" He said: So women were approached, and the censers diffused. He said: And it reached him that some of them said: Does one of us go to Mina with his organ dripping semen? So he addressed them, praised Allah, mighty and majestic, and extolled Him, then said: ""Had I faced of my affair what I now look back on, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal; had I not driven the sacrificial animal I would have shaved. Take your rites."" He said: So the people all stayed, until when it was the day of Tarwiyah and they wanted to head to Mina, they entered ihram for Hajj. He said: The sacrificial animal was upon whoever found, and fasting upon whoever did not find; and he shared among them in their sacrificial animals: the camel between seven, and the cow between seven. And their circumambulation of the House and their sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwah was one tawaf and one sa'y, for their Hajj and their Umrah. And he did not mention the story of Suraqah ibn Malik."
"6594","Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us Abu Shihab Musa ibn Nafi', he said: I arrived in Makkah performing tamattu' with Umrah, arriving three days before Tarwiyah. So the people of Makkah said to me: Your Hajj now becomes Makkan. So I entered upon Ata ibn Abi Rabah seeking his fatwa, and he said: Narrated to me Jabir ibn Abd Allah that he performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day he drove the budn, and they had entered ihram for Hajj singly. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: ""Release from your ihram with tawaf of the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and remain lawful, until when it is the day of Tarwiyah, enter ihram for Hajj, and make what you arrived with tamattu'."" They said: How do we make it tamattu' when we named Hajj? He said: ""Do what I commanded you; were it not that I drove the sacrificial animal I would have done like what I commanded you, but nothing unlawful becomes lawful of me until the sacrificial animal reaches its place."" So they did. And he did not mention the story of Suraqah ibn Malik."
"6595","Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived with four passed of Dhu al-Hijjah. When they circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Make it an Umrah."" When it was the day of Tarwiyah they called labbayk for Hajj. When it was the day of Nahr they circumambulated the House, and did not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah. And he did not mention the story of Suraqah ibn Malik."
"6596","Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us Arim Abu al-Nu'man, narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, narrated to us Abd al-Malik ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, (ha) and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions arrived on the morning of the fourth, entering ihram for Hajj unmixed with anything. When we arrived he commanded us, so we made it an Umrah, and that we release to our wives. Regarding that, talk was spoken. Ata said: Jabir said: Then one of us goes to Mina with his organ dripping semen? And Jabir said: That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood as a preacher and said: ""It reached me that peoples say such-and-such; by Allah, I am more dutiful and more God-fearing than them. Had I faced of my affair what I now look back on, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal; were it not that the sacrificial animal is with me I would have released."" Then Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, is it for us or forever? He said: ""Rather forever."" And Ali ibn Abi Talib came and one said: Labbayk with what the Prophet entered ihram with, and the other said: Labbayk with the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to remain upon his ihram, and shared him in the sacrificial animal."
"6597","Narrated to us Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Salih, narrated to me al-Layth, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Jurayj, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram, and we entered ihram with him for Hajj purely, until we arrived in Makkah on the morning of the fourth of Dhu al-Hijjah. We circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded whoever of us had not driven a sacrificial animal to release. He said: And he did not insist concerning the matter of women. Jabir said: So we said: He left us, until when only five nights remained between us and Arafah, he commanded us to release, so we come to Arafat with madhiy dripping from our male organs, while he did not release; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had driven the sacrificial animal. He said: Our saying reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood addressing the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then mentioned what reached him of their saying, and said: ""You know that I am the most God-fearing of you, and the most truthful and most dutiful; were it not that I drove the sacrificial animal I would have released, and had I faced of my affair what I now look back on, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal."" Jabir ibn Abd Allah said: So we heard and obeyed, and released. Al-Layth said: meaning tamattu'. And al-Layth did not mention the story of Suraqah ibn Malik."
"6598","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi, narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi, narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us Amr ibn Dinar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Makkah on the morning of the fourth. He commanded us to release. We said: Which release, O Prophet of Allah? He said: ""Release the full release."" We said: We go to Mina with our urethras dripping semen. He said: ""Release the full release; had I faced of my affair what I now look back on, I would have done like what you did."" He said: So we released and approached women. And Ibn Uyaynah did not mention the story of Suraqah ibn Malik."
"6599","Narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Ma'mari, narrated to us Dawud ibn Rushayd, narrated to us Hassan ibn Ibrahim, from Ya'qub ibn Ata, from his father, from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the adhan among us for Hajj, and set out, until when we arrived at the mosque at the tree he alighted and we alighted. Then we mounted, and stood waiting until he mounted. When we arrived in Makkah he circumambulated and we circumambulated; he prayed two rak'ahs, reciting in them ""Say: O disbelievers"" and ""Say: He is Allah, One."" Then we circumambulated al-Safa and al-Marwah, until when we were at al-Marwah, he said: ""Whoever has not driven a sacrificial animal, let him release."" We said: O Messenger of Allah, we intended Hajj. He said: ""Hajj has entered into Umrah."" So we approached women and perfume. When we arrived, with four days between us and coming to Mina, shall we come to Mina with our male organs dripping from our wives? Then Suraqah ibn Malik stood and said: Inform us, O Messenger of Allah, like a people born today. He said: ""Hajj has entered into Umrah."" He said: For us or forever? He said: ""No, rather forever."""
"6600","Narrated to us Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Musaddad, narrated to us Abu Awanah, from Abu Bishr, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We arrived entering ihram for Hajj. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to make it an Umrah and to release, while he had the sacrificial animal with him, so he could not make it an Umrah. And Abu Bishr did not mention in his hadith the story of Suraqah ibn Malik."
